{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line","id":1135965,"title":"Do mages need a bit of a nerf to bring them in line?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-08-31T15:33:20-07:00","id":1135965,"title":"Do mages need a bit of a nerf to bring them in line?","content":"I see that a common opinion is that Mages are overpowered compared to the other splats. Is this ok or will it seriously unbalance the game if mages are allowed to stomp around the World of Darkness as the most powerful beings around. Should there be some ways to bring them in line with everyone else? Here are some ideas that I just brainstormed<br /> <br /> 1/ Don't allow rotes so easily with some really powerful spells, psychic domination comes to mind. Maybe allow the rote later on with learning, but not readily available at character creation<br /> 2/ Don't allow 3 dice with a willpower point when casting a spell<br /> 3/ Allow other supernaturals to use supernatural tolerance to resist spells<br /> 4/ Don't allow 1 free potency if potency is the nature of the spell ( I recall a rule about that)<br /> <br /> Just some ideas, what are your thoughts?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T15:40:11-07:00","id":1135969,"title":"","content":"In my opinion, for a crossover game, Mages can and will steal the focus and be the most powerful thing around if you're not incredibly careful. Even using them as antagonists for other gamelines can be risky if you use the rules as written, in my opinion, and play them as knowledgeable threats. A lone Mage might not be too bad in the latter case, if made carefully, but even a small cabal can do just about anything they need to.<br /> <br /> As for house rules, you can see some I made for Withstanding in my homebrew hub, which includes using Supernatural tolerance as a withstand trait. But that alone won't really brings others up to the same level as Mages.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\"><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/themes/stvault/images/affiliatebanner4.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /></a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Christopher%20Falco&amp;affiliate_id=259490\" target=\"_blank\">Storytellers' Vault supplements (WoD, CoD)</a><br /> <a href=\"https://www.patreon.com/FalconianP\" target=\"_blank\">My Patreon! (CofD bonuses include: Fae Sorcery/Technomancy for Vampires, Quick Conversion for Inferno, and more!)</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1130-falco1029","name":"falco1029","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1130_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1045"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T15:53:17-07:00","id":1135977,"title":"","content":"4 isn't a concern anyway, the free steps up on Potency or Duration is equal to Arcana-1.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T15:58:27-07:00","id":1135979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1135977#post1135977\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">4 isn't a concern anyway, the free steps up on Potency or Duration is equal to Arcana-1.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's a lot isn't it? If this would be eliminated it would mean that there would be more free steps available and it would hit the dice pool harder.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T16:01:00-07:00","id":1135982,"title":"","content":"Oh, it was just the wording of the sentence. Okay, yes that would reduce Mages in power a bit because they would have less (not more) free steps available.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T16:27:57-07:00","id":1135991,"title":"","content":"The gameline isn't made to be balanced against other splats. They're balanced against themselves which is all that matters.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1452235-proin-s-legacy-hub\" target=\"_blank\">proin's Legacy hub</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","name":"proindrakenzol","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1514"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T16:32:03-07:00","id":1135992,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>proindrakenzol</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1135991#post1135991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The gameline isn't made to be balanced against other splats. They're balanced against themselves which is all that matters.</div> </div> </div> </div>It matters in the context of crossover games where you may need to make adjustments. Hopefully that will be something the Crossover Guide will delve into.<br /> <br /> I agree though it's not a problem in playing an MtA game.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T16:36:25-07:00","id":1135993,"title":"","content":"I thought part of the appeal of mage was a system that contextualizes great power and flexibility.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> One way you can preserve that is by making magic more dangerous and/or making things cost more to use.<br /> <br /> You preserve the raw strength and capability of mages, but you make them think more about how rewards justify the risks.<br /> <br /> <br /> I'd be fine with playing a mage the way they're intended to be played, yet mindful of a homebrew rule that forces me to spend mana way more often and or exponentially increases paradox.<br /> <br /> <br /> There is definitely methods of homebrewing a rule or world condition that makes mages much harder to play - but nerfing them entirely within the context of the World of Darkness line seems unfair to the people that are perfectly fine with mages sitting where they do. Particularly if they're not super interested in cross-overs.<br /> <br /> <br /> Within the context of a game of JUST mages, you can still make other splats exceedingly dangerous as antagonists through resources and planning. But if you're running a cross-over, the mage player severely outclasses everyone else in terms of utility.<br /> <br /> In that situation, you'd probably be forced to devote resources to simply occupying the mage, if not outright striking them down instantly by catching them off guard - as their main strength is planning. But then you might run into an issue where everyone knows you, as ST, are gunning for the mage all the time. More importantly, that the mage is a far bigger target or threat to the antagonist. Everyone else essentially becomes small fish. Some people may be okay with that.<br /> <br /> <br /> Again, if you keep all the capabilities the same, you could probably stand to keep the mage in line by making it far more costly to do cool stuff. Mages are cool and all, but the abyss is no joke. Neither is running out of mana and/or willpower because most, or all, of your abilities require resource expenditure. <br /> <br /> You, as the mage, might be left as a benchwarmer: either recovering from resource expenditure or biding your time for the best play.<br /> <br /> <br /> Historically, being squishy (or susceptible to being caught off guard) or running out of mana sit among the highest concerns for mages in various media.<br /> <br /> Add to that the threat of the abyss, and possibly the ire of other mages (Guardians) for being careless, and you may have the tools to limit mages already within your grasp.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T16:44:10-07:00","id":1135996,"title":"","content":"Without changing actual rules the best way to keep Mages in check is to add complications from multiple sources. The more balls a Mage has to juggle the less likely he is to try stealing the show from non-Mage characters, and the more likely he is to let those characters handle their areas of expertise.<br /> <br /> Even in just a straight Mage game this is the best way to prevent Mages from just one-shotting their problems that so many STs complain about.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T17:15:49-07:00","id":1136004,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line#post1135965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see that a common opinion is that Mages are overpowered compared to the other splats. Is this ok or will it seriously unbalance the game if mages are allowed to stomp around the World of Darkness as the most powerful beings around. Should there be some ways to bring them in line with everyone else? Here are some ideas that I just brainstormed<br /> <br /> 1/ Don't allow rotes so easily with some really powerful spells, psychic domination comes to mind. Maybe allow the rote later on with learning, but not readily available at character creation<br /> 2/ Don't allow 3 dice with a willpower point when casting a spell<br /> 3/ Allow other supernaturals to use supernatural tolerance to resist spells<br /> 4/ Don't allow 1 free potency if potency is the nature of the spell ( I recall a rule about that)<br /> <br /> Just some ideas, what are your thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> No to all. What are you trying to bring them in line with? Other splats? Tough. Crossovers are the problem, just dont do them.<br /> <br /> 3) basically would mean the mage cant do anything, the reason supernatural tolerance isnt used to resist spells is mages have stupidly low dicepools once they have to add spellfactors to spells.<br /> combined with your other suggestions that are all about the mage loosing dice, then you arnt nerfing mages to bring them in line with others you are outright curb stomping them into oblivion.<br /> <br /> You dont sit down to a d&amp;d game and let one person play a character from rifts do you. Just because the other ST games use the same system doesnt make it fine to crossover games.<br /> <br /> You say its going to seriously unbalance &quot;the&quot; game? Mages arnt unbalanced in the only game that being a mage matters in, IE: Mage.<br /> <br /> If you are wanting to run a X splat game, make all your players play the X splat, dont dilute the themes of the game just for that one special snowflake who wants to play Y, its usually never worth it.<br /> <br /> Also: 4chan is that way ----&gt;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 09-01-2017, 02:14 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T17:20:47-07:00","id":1136005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136004#post1136004\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3) basically would mean the mage cant do anything, the reason supernatural tolerance isnt used to resist spells is mages have stupidly low dicepools once they have to add spellfactors to spells.</div> </div> </div> </div>That would only be the case if mages were targeting people with very high Supernatural Tolerance. Starting characters are going to have higher Withstand ratings than Tolerance. And as you increase Gnosis your spellcasting dicepool rises appropriately. Since 'their' Supernatural Tolerance caps out at 10, and a mage's 'natural' dicepool at 15, not counting archmastery, with yantras you can go way over the top.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T17:23:04-07:00","id":1136006,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136005#post1136005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That would only be the case if mages were targeting people with very high Supernatural Tolerance. Starting characters are going to have higher Withstand ratings than Tolerance. And as you increase Gnosis your spellcasting dicepool rises appropriately. Since 'their' Supernatural Tolerance caps out at 10, and a mage's 'natural' dicepool at 15, not counting archmastery, with yantras you can go way over the top.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Does the person mean adding supernatural tolerance to withstand? Since not doing so is indeed helping mages at the lower end of the scale, even so at the higher end of the scale it still means needing -10 or more dice for potency. I thought they meant adding supernatural tolerance to withstand but its still bad if not, even a supernatural tolerance of 4 is probably more withstand that a mage would normally meet and -8 to beat it. Remember each point of resistance is -2 dice that soon stacks up faster than the mage gains dice<br /> <br /> Supernatural tolerance 10 vs max gnosis + arcana of 15 means they are -5 dicepool without spellfactors..","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 08-31-2017, 08:30 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T17:28:33-07:00","id":1136009,"title":"","content":"Supernatural Tolerance is very specifically not added to withstand, so presumably there is a good reason for that. <br /> <br /> But yeah, I don't think these hacks would work. Rotes aren't all that important, Buffing withstand would just increase the reliance on praxes, and generally speaking a mage's versatility is the issue, not their dice pools.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T17:40:10-07:00","id":1136011,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136009#post1136009\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But yeah, I don't think these hacks would work. Rotes aren't all that important, Buffing withstand would just increase the reliance on praxes, and generally speaking a mage's versatility is the issue, not their dice pools.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, you would likely want to limit versatility if you had some problem with 'bringing them in line.' Something like blocking inferior arcana off entirely, or making so the untrained limit is a hard limit rather than a minor experience increase with an in-world speedbump.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T17:49:37-07:00","id":1136014,"title":"","content":"Or just up your game as an ST instead of relying on a mechanics crutch. It's not really crossover if one splat has to weaken itself to be &quot;acceptable.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T17:51:47-07:00","id":1136015,"title":"","content":"Is maryshelly even talking about crossover?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T17:53:56-07:00","id":1136017,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>proindrakenzol</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1135991#post1135991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The gameline isn't made to be balanced against other splats. They're balanced against themselves which is all that matters.</div> </div> </div> </div>This thread acknowledges that, but still wants an alternative.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T18:12:59-07:00","id":1136026,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136015#post1136015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is maryshelly even talking about crossover?</div> </div> </div> </div>It's definitely an easy conclusion to come to when talking about &quot;bringing them in line with everyone else.&quot; That's also usually the context in which this topic gets raised.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T18:17:39-07:00","id":1136028,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>nofather</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136015#post1136015\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Is maryshelly even talking about crossover?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> They want the game to be brought inline with something, one assumes that it is another game line. Which is retarded. This is a really bad bait. Mage doesnt need to be brought in line with anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T18:20:01-07:00","id":1136029,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136028#post1136028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They want the game to be brought inline with something, one assumes that it is another game line. Which is retarded. This is a really bad bait. Mage doesnt need to be brought in line with anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with the sentiment, but you should probably dial back the attitude.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T19:17:46-07:00","id":1136050,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136028#post1136028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They want the game to be brought inline with something, one assumes that it is another game line. Which is retarded. This is a really bad bait. Mage doesnt need to be brought in line with anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's no need to be insulting. This wouldn't be the first thread asking if mages were 'too powerful' for their own mysteries and setting. If you don't like the thread, you aren't obligated to post and can just let people discuss it in peace.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1107_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1107\" data-vbnamecard=\"1107\">maryshelly</a> Are you talking about crossover games or mages in general?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T19:46:03-07:00","id":1136057,"title":"","content":"The first trick to running a crossover game is the same for any other game with more than one player, avoid overlapping roles. It's okay to have, for example, two heavy hitters if the story is combat intensive, even better if one is a brick and the other a back stabbing type, as long as each character gets their chance to shine.<br /> <br /> \u200bFrom personal experience I think everyone is way off base thinking the Mage will dominate every encounter, usually the Mage ends up taking a supporting role. He doesn't use life to boost his strength to superhuman levels and make himself a one man army, instead he uses it on the Werewolf and gleefully watches as it chews its way through hardened steel like it was tissue paper.<br /> \u200bThe Mage doesn't boost his presence to become the most persuasive person in the room, he boosts the Deava than watches as the Vampire dominates entire crowds.<br /> <br /> \u200bIf the players approach every situation as a competition to prove their character build is best than yes, somebody is going to dominate and leave all of the others frustrated (at my table it's usually the guy who always rolls lucky at dice). If instead they approach every challenge as a group, with the character written for that role taking the lead (and a variety of challenges aimed at different character's strengths), most players won't care who took the last shot but will be satisfied with their own contributions.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T19:50:08-07:00","id":1136059,"title":"","content":"I was thinking about crossover. Such as a pack of Iron masters hunting Mages as prey, or the encounters Elder Vampires can have with Supernaturals as suggested in Thousand years of night.<br /> <br /> But, I was also thinking about general power level, and how Mages affect the world relative to other Supernaturals, and how they compare to the human race ( the baseline humans in the core chronicles of darkness book)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly","name":"maryshelly","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1107_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"337"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T20:02:29-07:00","id":1136067,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>2ptTakrill</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136057#post1136057\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The first trick to running a crossover game is the same for any other game with more than one player, avoid overlapping roles. It's okay to have, for example, two heavy hitters if the story is combat intensive, even better if one is a brick and the other a back stabbing type, as long as each character gets their chance to shine.<br /> <br /> \u200bFrom personal experience I think everyone is way off base thinking the Mage will dominate every encounter, usually the Mage ends up taking a supporting role. He doesn't use life to boost his strength to superhuman levels and make himself a one man army, instead he uses it on the Werewolf and gleefully watches as it chews its way through hardened steel like it was tissue paper.<br /> \u200bThe Mage doesn't boost his presence to become the most persuasive person in the room, he boosts the Deava than watches as the Vampire dominates entire crowds.<br /> <br /> \u200bIf the players approach every situation as a competition to prove their character build is best than yes, somebody is going to dominate and leave all of the others frustrated (at my table it's usually the guy who always rolls lucky at dice). If instead they approach every challenge as a group, with the character written for that role taking the lead (and a variety of challenges aimed at different character's strengths), most players won't care who took the last shot but will be satisfied with their own contributions.</div> </div> </div> </div>You often find that without some overlapping roles things get very boring for players or hard for the ST. If one person is playing a super combat munchkin and another isnt, then the ST has to pitch fights so that they challenge the combatant but dont just murder the others. If one person is the investigator then them spending hours doing all the investigating while the combat peeps just sit around doing nothing isnt that great either.<br /> <br /> If i was playing a mage in a crossover, (which i would avoid like the plague anyway) why would i want to give up my time in the spotlight to another player by buffing them and sitting back? If i have mind and can mind control groups of people far easier than the vampire AND do so during the day then i'll do it. i'll probably be able to use mind instead of dominate/obfuscate/auspex for the entire rest of the game.<br /> <br /> Wanting to play something else in a X game is usually really selfish, imagine wanting to play a vampire in a demon game and expecting all the other demon players to only do stuff at night? Storytellers really need to just have the balls to say NO about allowing Y splat in a X game or running a free for all crossover (which happens how often, i think people moan about it far more than actually playing them or that they happen because the ST doesnt have the willpower to just say NO over it being vital to the story that there be a crossover).<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T20:13:04-07:00","id":1136069,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136028#post1136028\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">They want the game to be brought inline with something, one assumes that it is another game line. Which is retarded.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:red\">Using that word to mean stupid is not acceptable around here. Don't do it again.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T20:14:22-07:00","id":1136071,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136059#post1136059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about crossover. Such as a pack of Iron masters hunting Mages as prey, or the encounters Elder Vampires can have with Supernaturals as suggested in Thousand years of night.<br /> <br /> But, I was also thinking about general power level, and how Mages affect the world relative to other Supernaturals, and how they compare to the human race ( the baseline humans in the core chronicles of darkness book)</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Not much of a story if one group of real players is hunting another group of real players? How often are people running games where 2 players are werewolfs hunting the other 2 player mages? What happens when one side beats the other side? (And id bet on the Mages)<br /> <br /> You can play an entire mage/demon/vampire/etc etc game without even mentioning other supernaturals, and if you want to then usually the best way to do it is to use dread powers etc like they did in hunter.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 08-31-2017, 11:57 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T21:57:34-07:00","id":1136085,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136059#post1136059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about crossover. Such as a pack of Iron masters hunting Mages as prey, or the encounters Elder Vampires can have with Supernaturals as suggested in Thousand years of night.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well in this case you just have to plan things out like you would with any antagonist. Look at the character sheets belonging to your players and assess their capabilities, then build challenges accordingly, ideally avoiding a 'rocks fall, everybody dies' scenario unless there's a suitable warning, 'the crevice ahead is shielded from above by dozens of boulders held up by a flimsy-looking pole.' From there you use the group's strengths. For vampires it's manipulation and politicking, an elder vampire didn't get to become one by rushing headlong into combat at every moment, they've let their enemies tie themselves up in knots dealing with other issues and taken them out one by one, seduced them over to the vampire's side, or just outsurvived them. Werewolves are more of an iminent, physical threat, but they don't really jump into the sacred hunt on a whim, otherwise they'd be horrible spirit hunters. They do research and reconnaisance and prepare for their hunts accordingly, and against larger threats are more likely to work on harrying them down, taking out allies and contacts and crippling infrastructure until they take down the target. From a mage point of view, on both sides, protecting oneself from these things is easier theorized than done, as everyone has family or friends and relies on other members of the cabal, or outsiders, to make up for their shortcomings. Sure, a cabal may be a group, but the mentor who advises them in times of need is alone. Their sanctum may be good for ritual magic, but what happens when the enemy already has someone on the inside?<br /> <br /> If you want specific advice for how to develop a mage antagonist for a specific game line, I suggest you make a thread in the appropriate game's forum. If you want advice for making antagonists OF a certain game line for a mage game, you should probably make a new thread devoted to the topic.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But, I was also thinking about general power level, and how Mages affect the world relative to other Supernaturals, and how they compare to the human race ( the baseline humans in the core chronicles of darkness book) </div> </div> </div>Flatscans really got the shaft in second edition. Everyone has gone up in power, and it looks like each book is increasing the distance between them and regular humanity. They have gotten a lot of more powerful offshoots, however, like wolf-blooded, the micro-templates from Hurt Locker and isolated cases of strange powers. In my experience, running my own game and reading others, at a certain point, humans in mage stories are going to be relegated to background. They're there, players might investigate one but they're under no obligation to actually deal with them, and PCs only really have to be aware when it becomes a danger that might cause paradox. Of course, this can be a weakness, as there's many enemies and dangers that can hide within humanity. And obviously as storyteller you can shake it up as much as you want, by making mysteries revolving around humans, eventually it's going to be easy enough to get around.<br /> <br /> In turn, however, you start dealing with other issues. Goetia, spirits, Abyssal whatevers and Supernal intruders, the many varied interruptions with the Fallen World, and of course other mages. One of the big themes of the game is the mage versus herself, so you'll always be able to play with that.<br /> <br /> Mage, all the supernatural games really, are a significant detour from usual level 1-13 fare. Once you start a game with what you think is a good antagonist for 'beginning characters' only to see a pack of werewolf PCs converging on them and realizing your 'boss' is going to be dead in one turn you can either balk or start planning appropriately. Take advantage of PC weaknesses, read their aspirations and backgrounds, see what the players want to happen and make it possible, though not without a hitch. Maybe they want to fly off to the astral plane and build a castle, in which case humanity, or at least humans, are out of the picture entirely.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T21:59:20-07:00","id":1136086,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1534_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1534\" data-vbnamecard=\"1534\">totalgit</a> Kindly, stop posting if you have nothing constructive to add.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T22:08:41-07:00","id":1136087,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136086#post1136086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1534_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1534\" data-vbnamecard=\"1534\">totalgit</a> Kindly, stop posting if you have nothing constructive to add.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I think you'll find that i gave you the alternative you wanted. Go use witches from hunter or psychics from second sight or something else if you think Supernal mages are to powerful for whatever splat is your favourite. You cant come into a mage forum and start saying &quot;they need to be nerfed because they dont play well with other lines&quot; when they arnt supposed too in the first place.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T22:46:14-07:00","id":1136096,"title":"","content":"Mages, Mumies and Demons are more powerful than other gamelines. That has been established and is intentional design. So what should you do about the rules in a crossover: nothing.<br /> That a Mage of the same &quot;level&quot; is more powerful than a Vampire doesn't make the game less playable. If you have a PC-group only from one splat it is fairly easy. You adjust the NPC to your needs. A starting Mage might be a formidable antagonist for a group of Vampires, but of course you don't throw an Archmage on a group of freshly changed Werewolfs.<br /> <br /> If you have a group of different splats it get's a bit more tricky, but there is actually a game mechanic dealing with powerlevels: experience. Either just give the non-Mage splats some additional experience or take some away from the Mage (e.g. take 2 Arcana dots away at creation and limit max Arcana dots to 2). You might have to tweek a little there, but if you want all your players to feel equally powerful that is by far the easiest solution. And the major advantage is that it just works with all the rules, without changing anything that might turn out to totally break the system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11939-hardcorehannes","name":"HardcoreHannes","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-08-31T23:39:18-07:00","id":1136106,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136087#post1136087\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You cant come into a mage forum and start saying &quot;they need to be nerfed because they dont play well with other lines&quot; when they arnt supposed too in the first place.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's really not what happened here, though. This thread posited a reasonably asked question on how to tune down the power level of mages for the sake of crossover or personal preference and wether such a step was necessary (for crossover I presume). You may not agree with that premise, but all that's been done here was a asking a normal question in a civil manner. There was no mage bashing, no &quot;mages <b>should</b> be weaker&quot;. You're just being defensive without anyone even showing agression toward you (or for that matter mages).<br /> <br /> Mage bashing threads may be awful, but if we actually have a discussion like this in a civil and nontoxic fashion, why try turning it into a fight? As it has been said before if the topic isn't for you, just don't respond.<br /> <br /> That said I agree that using wiches/psychics/dread powers is probably the easiest way for designing an opponent for other splats that feels magey but doesn't have the flexibility to make you think &quot;ahh damnit why isn't he just anticipating every move of theirs with his op knowledge gathering powers&quot;.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T02:28:37-07:00","id":1136120,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136086#post1136086\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1534_2.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1534\" data-vbnamecard=\"1534\">totalgit</a> Kindly, stop posting if you have nothing constructive to add.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Kindly stop pretending to be a mod.<br /> <br /> As to the general thrust of the thread - can't help you with crossovers; if you have werewolf pcs hunting storyteller character mages for god's sake dumb the mages down, same as if you're trying to run Hunter. Mummies can hold their own for a bit, Demons can until the mages realise their powers rip Demon's Cover to bits.<br /> <br /> If it's &quot;relative to the rest of the Chronicles of Darkness,&quot; and you're worried about mages being too powerful (whatever that means) for the monsters and mysteries, remember - the Awakened deal with things that would eat protagonists of other gamelines for breakfast. High-Ranking Goetia, Supernal and Abyssal entities, bizarre Lower Depths things, dimensional tears. You can make an element of setting weirdness much more dangerous in a Mage game, as the Awakened can cope with it (or not, and die to it).<br /> <br /> And their principle opponent is one another.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":10}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T09:55:35-07:00","id":1136226,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1107-maryshelly\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1107_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1107\" data-vbnamecard=\"1107\">maryshelly</a> For toning down Mages, you could as others have brought up, use one of the less potent versions, like Witches (from Hunter), or Proximi (you can give them various Mage-like features to power them back up).<br /> <br /> Other options are to add Mage-only threats (not threats only Mages can deal with, but threats than only impact Mages). Like Abyssal intrusions that only hunt down Supernal magic (you could even tie the Atrocity dice from Danse Macabre into Paradox rolls - triggering Paradox may not only add to the Scene, but the entire area). Or setting additions like a far stronger Mage-Police, or even supernatural Rules regarding magic use (No using certain forms of mind control without prior justification or you get punished, either by other Mages, or reality itself, for example). Or add in natural counter-measures against Supernal magic - like how some metals or symbols resonate with certain Paths for magical tools, maybe some materials or actions counteract some Arcana , such as blocking certain effects, or reducing potency or something (which would also help mortals against Mages).<br /> <br /> You might make Instant casting or Creative Thaumaturgy less effective. Mages can still sling their Praxis and any Rotes they know, but ad-libbing magic might <i>require</i> time-consuming Yantras, or not automatically get Arcana - 1 in Potency (Mages are still strong, and adaptable, but lose much of their power when they have to come up with spells on the fly, or those spells take precious Turns).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":7212,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","user":"Vent0","date":". 09-01-2017, 02:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T10:29:38-07:00","id":1136243,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>proindrakenzol</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1135991#post1135991\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The gameline isn't made to be balanced against other splats. They're balanced against themselves which is all that matters.</div> </div> </div> </div> Not entirely true. Mages study mysteries of the world. And mages are pretty much designed to trivialize any non-Awakened threat, which includes designing spirit and ghostly antagonists, which directly impacts a pure mage game as well as the kinds of stories you can run.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Anwyays, In my experience, there are four major kinds of crossovers:<br /> 1) supernatural antagonists (aka the war)<br /> 2) social and mental mysteries to investigate<br /> 3) casual interaction but no direct impact on plot<br /> 4) mixed party<br /> <br /> In the case of 1? Mages are generally going to win for several reasons - the big two being a better pool of powers and the fact that mages are king at gathering information, and information is the linchpin of modern wars. Case 2 heavily favors mages as well, both in terms of avoiding notice, or digging up the information trivially. In case 3, there's really no problems unless a PC starts asking an NPC mage to do things for them, in which case things go off the rail again. Case 4, we have a strong tendacy for Awakened to trivialize any non-Supernal/Awkaned/Abyssal threat, and for no one else to be able to handle a the mage-based threats.<br /> <br /> if you really want to have a less rampant witch/wizard-splat, there's really no good way to do it than to redesign the powers of the Awakened from the ground up. Even if you correct the imbalance of the Awakened being able to affect even high Potency others, that's still not going to correct the manner in which Awakened magic can be used to empower other supers to the point of putting their natural powers to shame or to trivially deal with any threat.<br /> <br /> The sheer variety of techniques per Arcana combined with the shielding and other buffing abilities is a major issue in any crossover game (other than type 3).<br /> <br /> While there are times when crossover with mages hasn't been a bad thing, that's usually from a specific attitude on part of the mage player. So, its not an absolute. In general, though? Its usually a pain in the butt. <br /> <br /> And that's not touching on a rather important issue related to the balance of Awakened mages in the first place - spell limits. Nomrally, you can only have a few spells active at once. In a crossover game, especially type 4, you have easy access to a number of sleepwalker types that can hold your spells,a cting as major force multipilers. <br /> <br /> This is why I said you really need to redesign things from the ground up, because the simple fact of the matter is that there's simply too many things that can cause issues","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 09-01-2017, 01:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T10:37:51-07:00","id":1136248,"title":"","content":"Being able to trivialize ghosts and spirits doesn't mean that Mage isn't balanced against itself. Try again.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:17:48-07:00","id":1136269,"title":"","content":"So, I don't know or care about crossover balance, but I think Mages are too powerful / unrestrained for their own game, as well. It's not an interesting power scheme for an urban fantasy game, as it's too unchallenging, and it's certainly not a magic system appropriate for a horror game. But that's a much longer rant. <br /> <br /> I don't think &quot;power&quot; is necessary the issue, but...the lack of cost? The ease of use? The flexibility? There's just no...downside to magic use other than some rare paradoxes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:20:23-07:00","id":1136271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136269#post1136269\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, I don't know or care about crossover balance, but I think Mages are too powerful / unrestrained for their own game, as well. It's not an interesting power scheme for an urban fantasy game, as it's too unchallenging, and it's certainly not a magic system appropriate for a horror game. But that's a much longer rant. <br /> <br /> I don't think &quot;power&quot; is necessary the issue, but...the lack of cost? The ease of use? The flexibility? There's just no...downside to magic use other than some rare paradoxes.</div> </div> </div> </div>If you're looking to the magic system as the only way to punish Mages for committing AoH then you're doing it wrong.<br /> <br /> In fact it's likely you've entirely missed the point of magic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 09-01-2017, 02:24 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:25:31-07:00","id":1136275,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136271#post1136271\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> If you're looking to the magic system as the only way to punish Mages for committing AoH then you're doing it wrong.<br /> <br /> In fact it's likely you've entirely missed the point of magic.</div> </div> </div> </div>As I said, it's a longer rant, but I don't think Mage is a good horror game largely because the magic isn't scary. And part of the reason it isn't scary is because it's very forgiving to both be a mage and to use magic.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 02:28 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:32:06-07:00","id":1136279,"title":"","content":"Horror is about helplessness, the horror in Mage is them compared to other people because they're the monsters, and it's also about the Exarchs in comparison to which Mages are as helpless as Sleepers are to them. Not sure why the magic itself has to be horrific.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:36:25-07:00","id":1136282,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136279#post1136279\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not sure why the magic itself has to be horrific.</div> </div> </div> </div>Because there's examples of magic systems that drive you insane, such as in Call of Cthulhu, to name an example.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:40:01-07:00","id":1136285,"title":"","content":"*looks at the abyss*<br /> <br /> *looks into the camera like he's on The Office*","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:41:19-07:00","id":1136287,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136282#post1136282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Because there's examples of magic systems that drive you insane, such as in Call of Cthulhu, to name an example.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's fine for Call of Cthulhu, but I fail to see why it's necessary in order to implement horror in Mage which has other sources of horror.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:42:10-07:00","id":1136289,"title":"","content":"I think people who have a problem with mages being strong are missing a CORE theme of Mage: Hubris. One of the major points of mage is to have both player characters and npcs be powerful so that they can abuse that power and showcase their hubris. This just highlights the problems with crossover games and the reason why the developers specifically chose not to balance the game lines against each other. Each game has its own themes and attempting to balance the games against each other instead of with themselves defeats the purpose of each game.<br /> <br /> Honestly, if you want to do something that goes specifically against game design you probably shouldn't go to that game's forums to seek the creativity to change the game. Sure, some people will help you, but if you don't know how to change the game on your own then you might not know the game well enough to appreciate why it was made the way it was made.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T11:44:56-07:00","id":1136290,"title":"","content":"I mean, to keep things in perspective to the thread, people were saying that Dr. Strange felt like a Mage movie. And rightly so! But Dr. Strange is a superhero film. The reason Mage can't crossover with, say, Vampire, is that Vampire is a horror movie and Mage is a superhero movie with horror elements. It's not so much about power level as it is about design attitude; Mage has higher scale opponents but it's not an intrinsically &quot;personal horror&quot; state to be in, unlike, say, being a vampire.<br /> <br /> So the real reason this discussion keeps cropping up is people go into the CofD expecting horror games, and Mage is kind of an outlier where it really just isn't intended to be one down to its bones. Power level is just a proxy for genre, in this instance, I think.<br /> <br /> I mean, look at the magic system itself - it's a fantasy magic system. It's a direct line from Ars Magica to Mage: The Ascension to Mage: The Awakening. It's an urban fantasy game with relatively high powered protagonists. This is not to say there isn't horror in Mage, or horror you can do with Mage, but crossover is going to inevitably run into genre contradictions.<br /> <br /> EDIT: I mean, the bottom line is, there's real truth to Mage being kind of an odd man out in the CofD generally. This sort of boils down to &quot;they should have made a different game than they made&quot; as a criticism, which is completely worthless, obviously. But you could easily envision Mage as its own standalone game a la Scion and a different game about horror-genre magic specifically in the &quot;Mage&quot; slot fitting in better with the CofD, tonally.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 02:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:02:14-07:00","id":1136294,"title":"","content":"Yeah, it is pretty central to Mage as a game line that they be powerful. The horror you find in Mage is rarely in the monsters you face. Yeah, those things are grotesque quite often, but the horror isn't caused by them; it's caused by <i>you</i>, and people like you. This is a game line that names Hellblazer as one of its inspirations, and every issue of that is a question of just how many twists John plans to put into the noose he's going to hang himself with this week.<br /> <br /> The powerlessness in Mage comes from feeling like you're a god and then realizing you're human all along; from opening the ancient chest covered with warnings because you're curious what's inside and being terrified when it all goes pear-shaped; from causing everyone you love and care about to look at you like a monster. It's from taking off the hood of your executioner and realizing you're looking at yourself.<br /> <br /> To get yourself into deep shit, as it were, you need a really big shovel. A huge part of Mages as people is that, once they've dug themselves into that hole, thinking &quot;Y'know, if I made this shovel bigger, I could dig myself out of it&quot;.<br /> <br /> My most memorable Mage moments aren't from facing off against some unspeakable horror from beyond the veil of time and space; they're where absolute certainty and conviction made a young Thyrsus nearly put an axe to her uncle's neck in a fit of rage and grief. They're from when that same Thyrsus, suddenly so full of the zeal of a thearch, realized that a young man whom she so desperately wants to help is beyond her ability to do so; not because she can't pacify his abusive father or offer financial stability or even answers to his questions, but because those things are what are driving him closer to Awakening (which she believes is worth the risk of his emotional health).<br /> <br /> If you want to be spooked by monsters, you can do that too. Been plenty of that. Rising power means the ability to seek out greater threats. It's just that, for Mage, the horror isn't that something with too many consonants and too few vowels in its name exists; it's that you can't help yourself in wanting to go poke it in the eye and ask it for a favour.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2855-axelgear","name":"Axelgear","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2855_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1450"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:04:29-07:00","id":1136296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136289#post1136289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think people who have a problem with mages being strong are missing a CORE theme of Mage: Hubris.</div> </div> </div> </div>How is that anytime that one of this thread happen, people come and start throwing the old &quot;you must not be getting it&quot; as if the option were &quot;getting it and loving it&quot; and &quot;not getting it&quot;. I think some people here forget that one can get the theme of hubris of the game and either think is not well done (me, for example) or just not subscribe to it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136289#post1136289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Honestly, if you want to do something that goes specifically against game design you probably shouldn't go to that game's forums to seek the creativity to change the game. Sure, some people will help you, but if you don't know how to change the game on your own then you might not know the game well enough to appreciate why it was made the way it was made.</div> </div> </div> </div>What place for example is there to discuss hacks to Mage with people that play mage?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>maryshelly</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line#post1135965\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I see that a common opinion is that Mages are overpowered compared to the other splats. Is this ok or will it seriously unbalance the game if mages are allowed to stomp around the World of Darkness as the most powerful beings around. Should there be some ways to bring them in line with everyone else? Here are some ideas that I just brainstormed<br /> <br /> 1/ Don't allow rotes so easily with some really powerful spells, psychic domination comes to mind. Maybe allow the rote later on with learning, but not readily available at character creation<br /> 2/ Don't allow 3 dice with a willpower point when casting a spell<br /> 3/ Allow other supernaturals to use supernatural tolerance to resist spells<br /> 4/ Don't allow 1 free potency if potency is the nature of the spell ( I recall a rule about that)<br /> <br /> Just some ideas, what are your thoughts?</div> </div> </div> </div>I think that the problem you are having is that your goal is too broad. &quot;Balance mage&quot;, thats to aimlessly of a goal to properly put into practice. Option 3 and 1 are fine but the rest are meh as balance goes. Instead start small adressing small issues.<br /> <br /> For example when i houserule mage my main issue is &quot;I wanna run urban fantasy and that mages and beyond arent the only thing threatening to mages&quot; similar to your objective that is too broad of a objective so instead i broke it into small concern and i adress them each and everyone. &quot;the difference between arcanum 2 and 3 and so on is too big of a gap&quot;, &quot;time magic being able to rewind a whole session worth of scenes without issue&quot;, &quot;the game favoring casting from your sanctum instead of face to face&quot;.<br /> <br /> So on the difference on arcanum i went with not allowing players to freely raise arcanum past a certain point in each chapter. On the time magic thing i am trying to port permanent paradox do as thing to say to the player &quot;oh sure you can go back 1 scene.....but it gonna cost you A LOT so use it wisely&quot; and for the face to face issue i make that casting symphatetic doesnt give you the exceptional successes unlike face to face but that the aiming roll can add bonus to the roll (which balances itself with the fact that the player will have small gnosis + arcanum pools). And so on.<br /> <br /> Point being, segregate your issues with the power of mage in small issues to be adressed individually. Is a longer approach than bigger all emcompassing houserules but it allows you to think them over and carefully consider each and every single one.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-01-2017, 03:09 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:05:53-07:00","id":1136297,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136282#post1136282\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Because there's examples of magic systems that drive you insane, such as in Call of Cthulhu, to name an example.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> If the best we can do is a game based on the writings of a racist hydrophobe, we're in bad fucking shape.<br /> <br /> (I *hate* Lovecraftian stuff)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"color:#000000\"><b>Dave Brookshaw</b></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/14-dave-brookshaw","name":"Dave Brookshaw","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar14_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2081"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:16:51-07:00","id":1136300,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136297#post1136297\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> If the best we can do is a game based on the writings of a racist hydrophobe, we're in bad fucking shape.<br /> <br /> (I *hate* Lovecraftian stuff)</div> </div> </div> </div>I would point to Unknown Armies and maybe Kult as better examples for how you can have full time magic-users in a horror game and not sacrifice horror. Call of Cthulhu is a bad example because magic isn't an &quot;every session&quot; part of gameplay.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:40:38-07:00","id":1136318,"title":"","content":"Again, magic does not need to have a high cost in order for horror to be present in the game. In fact if it did it would undercut the horror that Mage is actually going for.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 09-01-2017, 03:47 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:46:52-07:00","id":1136322,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136318#post1136318\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Again, magic does not need to have a high cost in order for horror to be present in the game.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Pretty much necessary if it's a magic horror game; Mage can have horror in the sense any game can have horror, by just having monsters show up. But for magic to be scary, it has to be baked into the roots with horror magic tropes and conventions, which mage generally lacks. My point is basically it's not the power level that throws off crossover, it's genre. <br /> <br /> Demons are powerful, but Demon is unambiguously a horror game. There's a common thread that makes a crossover with, say, vampire, totally viable, despite disparate power levels. But Mage is ultimately a different kind of game. It doesn't mix well. It's a square fantasy peg in the round horror hole of the CofD. This isn't a criticism, it just makes crossover a genre mix challenge rather than a power level equalization challenge.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:49:09-07:00","id":1136323,"title":"","content":"...nope, you still don't get it. Magic having a higher cost in Mage would undercut the actual horror in Mage by making it less likely that magic would be used.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:50:15-07:00","id":1136324,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136323#post1136323\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...nope, you still don't get it. Magic having a higher cost in Mage would undercut the actual horror in Mage by making it less likely that magic would be used.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's little to no actual horror in Mage. I would say it's roughly on par with, say, a darker D&amp;D monster manual section, or The Dresden Files. Maybe the Sandman Slim novels if I'm feeling generous.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 03:52 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T12:57:53-07:00","id":1136332,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136324#post1136324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There's little to no actual horror in Mage. I would say it's roughly on par with, say, a darker D&amp;D monster manual section, or The Dresden Files. Maybe the Sandman Slim novels if I'm feeling generous.</div> </div> </div> </div>Pff. Yeah, sure. The best and worst of humanity exploded into hyperfocus, all of the petty travesties and grotesque mis-decisions writ cosmic and &quot;There's little to no actual horror in Mage.&quot;<br /> <br /> Mages are terrifying-terrifying to be around and terrifying <i>to be</i>. And a significant part of that horror is the amount of people who don't catch what makes Mage horrifying.<br /> <br /> EDIT: And that's before you count all the ways that diving into magic warps you-they are default a monster.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:01:29-07:00","id":1136335,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136324#post1136324\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> There's little to no actual horror in Mage. I would say it's roughly on par with, say, a darker D&amp;D monster manual section, or The Dresden Files. Maybe the Sandman Slim novels if I'm feeling generous.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your inability to understand the horror inherent in easily abused magic is a flaw in your thinking, not in the setting or the system.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:02:04-07:00","id":1136336,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136332#post1136332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pff. Yeah, sure. The best and worst of humanity exploded into hyperfocus, all of the petty travesties and grotesque mis-decisions writ cosmic and &quot;There's little to no actual horror in Mage.&quot;<br /> <br /> Mages are terrifying-terrifying to be around and terrifying <i>to be</i>. And a significant part of that horror is the amount of people who don't catch what makes Mage horrifying.<br /> <br /> EDIT: And that's before you count all the ways that diving into magic warps you-they are default a monster.</div> </div> </div> </div>Nah. They are none of those things. The game just doesn't have enough teeth to land any of that. It's basically just the babytown frolics version of Kult, to be honest. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:05:48-07:00","id":1136339,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136335#post1136335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Your inability to understand the horror inherent in easily abused magic is a flaw in your thinking, not in the setting or the system.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'd even say it is a definite plus on the system. Your character is meant not to see anything wrong with mind controlling others and raising the dead and the lack of perceived consequences plays a big part in that i think. <br /> For me its the only way to arrive to a state of '' What have i done!!!''","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Completed campaign: Scion 2nd Edition. Les L\u00e9gendes Currently playing: Being a dad for a 3 year old daughter and a 2 years old son and now a beautiful new baby.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11312-maitrecorbo","name":"Maitrecorbo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11312_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"719"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:07:17-07:00","id":1136340,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136322#post1136322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Pretty much necessary if it's a magic horror game; Mage can have horror in the sense any game can have horror, by just having monsters show up. But for magic to be scary, it has to be baked into the roots with horror magic tropes and conventions, which mage generally lacks. My point is basically it's not the power level that throws off crossover, it's genre.<br /> <br /> Demons are powerful, but Demon is unambiguously a horror game. There's a common thread that makes a crossover with, say, vampire, totally viable, despite disparate power levels. But Mage is ultimately a different kind of game. It doesn't mix well. It's a square fantasy peg in the round horror hole of the CofD. This isn't a criticism, it just makes crossover a genre mix challenge rather than a power level equalization challenge.</div> </div> </div> </div>I agree with Mage being different, but I wouldn't say it has no actual horror. The things you can, and are liable to, do can be pretty horrific in and off themselves, and unlike some other splats, you cannot claim &quot;my inner Beast made me do that&quot; or &quot;I have no choice, I need to do this to survive&quot;. The horrible things you do? &quot;I wanted to see if I can do that&quot;. Some Rick Sanchez level of justification, there.<br /> I don't dislike systems were magic needs to be horrific in nature, and has great costs. In fact, atmospheric stuff like that is awesome, and it might even fit the CroD well, really. But I have to admit I really, really like Mage the way it is, and the Horror it can provide.<br /> And Horror there is, not just &quot;There be Monsters!&quot;, or &quot;You accidently blew your arm off trying to make coffee&quot; or even &quot;Instead of closing the wound, you channel abyss-tainted Life-force into your wife's wound, and ever-increasing cancerous growths eat her away while she lives and suffers and cries, and you are to blame because you just wouldn't be satisfied with waiting for help, and now you are truly helpless and this is all your fault&quot;.<br /> <br /> It is a cosmological Horror (not Cosmic Horror though) of Loss of what could be, of Reality fraying and coming undone, some constant threat added in for good measure, tyrannical oppression, and most of all the hubristic Horror of facing what you would be willing to do <i>if only you could</i>.<br /> <br /> I can see how it's not for everyone. Does not mean it's bad, or wrong. It's just not your cup of tea, then.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:23:58-07:00","id":1136346,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wormwood</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136340#post1136340\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree with Mage being different, but I wouldn't say it has no actual horror. The things you can, and are liable to, do can be pretty horrific in and off themselves, and unlike some other splats, you cannot claim &quot;my inner Beast made me do that&quot; or &quot;I have no choice, I need to do this to survive&quot;. The horrible things you do? &quot;I wanted to see if I can do that&quot;. Some Rick Sanchez level of justification, there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hypothetically, sure, but the game does a terrible job highlighting and emphasizing those things. I don't dispute &quot;Mages can abuse people! Abuse is scary! Therefore, Mage is scary!&quot; is at least a coherent position to take, but we are talking about a game where a fifth of all player characters can start with Kilgrave-levels of mind control that never talks about rape. We're talking about a game that declares every character is obsessed with one or more occult mysteries, but never goes into any discussion of how obsessions like that might interfere with day-to-day activities, health or sanity. A mage in Unknown Armies is obsessed; a mage in Mage is just really interested in something. It might be dangerous, sure, but so is parkour.<br /> <br /> We're talking about a game that spends most of its attention on whiz-bang magical adventures against fantastic adversaries. We're talking about a game that never once declares itself a horror game. So, yeah, you can talk about hubris and the self-destructive temptation of power, but there's no teeth to any of that. Look at the Wisdom mechanics, for example - it's actually incredibly easy to avoid the major wisdom sins and still get what you want, because the magic system is so low-cost, powerful, and flexible. Vampire made it genuinely difficult to both survive and keep your humanity; the whole axis of the game revolves around that dilemma. Mage claims something similar in its trichotomic dilemma of &quot;Engage the Mysteries, Survive, Preserve Wisdom&quot; but it doesn't really make it that hard.<br /> <br /> This is coming from personal experience, by the by, when I ran a Mage game that I deemed largely successful, I felt I had to be unusually punitive as a storyteller in emphasizing the darkness, danger, and occult consequences of the player's actions. &quot;Unusually&quot; in that I felt I was working against the game in introducing horror elements, not following its lead. In actual play, it felt a lot more like an Ars Magica game than it did Unknown Armies. That is, it felt more like a fantasy game than a horror game.<br /> <br /> Heck, Ars Magica has more baked-in with the Warping mechanic than Mage does and Ars Magica isn't remotely a horror game. There's a reason the Abyss took such a huge backseat in 2E (well, wordcount) - because it's not trying to be a horror game the way, say, Vampire is.<br /> <br /> If you look at the genre tropes, Mage fits urban fantasy. You're playing as Harry Dresden, not John Constantine. Constantine routinely pays horrific prices and dances with dangerous forces; Dresden just occasionally burns down a building. Both can be described as &quot;hubris&quot; but only one is horror.<br /> <br /> I'll say that arguably Mage tries for horror and lands on urban fantasy, but I'm not sure it's really aiming at horror to begin with. I feel like some people are seeing this and thinking I'm shitting on Mage; I'm just explaining my theory as to why it doesn't fit with the CofD in terms of crossover. It's a perfectly cromulent urban fantasy game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 04:31 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:30:44-07:00","id":1136347,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136336#post1136336\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Nah. They are none of those things. The game just doesn't have enough teeth to land any of that. It's basically just the babytown frolics version of Kult, to be honest.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The game has as much teeth as the ST is willing to give it. If you want no holds barred chronicles, the ST can come at you with a veritable shit-storm of entities you've pissed off during the course of your frolicking. Just because you're a mage doesn't mean you're equipped for any and all threats you could attract. There are tons of stories of careless, short-sighted, cocky, cavalier mages that became the impetus for an investigation.<br /> <br /> Mages have the tools to know stuff about stuff, but they don't have it all figured out. You play a game where you're obsessed by Mysteries - things that aren't solved and have the potential to be exceedingly dangerous if not approached tactfully.<br /> <br /> The ST has license to run mage like a hyper-vindictive DM, where an insta-gib trap or condition lies in wait at every turn.<br /> <br /> Horror in mage is also about knowing how limited you are. Knowing how powerful other things are or could potentially be.<br /> <br /> You just figured out you have enormous freedom to do whatever you want! But you also found out you're not the only one! You also found out you're already outclassed when you Awaken! You also found out the people who outclass you are still outclassed by others!<br /> <br /> <br /> Just as easily as you can manipulate and play with things, other entities can do the same to you.<br /> <br /> Some of the other splats work in vast networks, too. Oh, look, a vampire is no threat to you - you must have it all figured out - so you cream them and go on about your business. That couldn't possibly end up bad for you and your loved ones.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> A common misconception about power and versatility is thinking you don't have anything to lose. You likely will have things to lose. The classic example is thinking you're unstoppable, until someone reminds you that they can wear your mother's skin or turn your dear old dad into a sex-slave.<br /> <br /> You have options, but so does everything else. Except they're not necessarily tied to resources the same way you are.<br /> <br /> In mage, because you solve Mysteries, you're also acting as vanguard or frontiersman. You're trying to figure it out where either a) others couldn't or b) no one has tried before. Spoiler alert: there is always room to fuck it up.<br /> <br /> <br /> There is a whole system for descending into madness as a consequence of being careless or unlucky - there is also a system that acknowledges breaking points, or the fact that you can be bothered by specific things even though you're &quot;all-powerful.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> You're free to believe it isn't something you find personally scary, but there are plenty of reasons people do find it unsettling, terrifying, unnerving, etc.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I know if mages existed, and I met one, I'd be both hyper curious and simultaneously scared of what they could do to me intentionally or otherwise. People don't always mean what they say, or mean for the consequences of their actions. Things don't always play out in your favor.<br /> <br /> The same is true for playing mage.<br /> <br /> It is your prerogative to play Mage like it is a superhero game, but that doesn't mean it works that way.<br /> <br /> <br /> Music can also go a long way to make you unsettled, even as a mage.<br /> <br /> <br /> You do the work and mage can be scary, but if you just hand every piece of info away or make every conflict play out in a predictable or lackluster way, then of course - mage becomes &quot;not scary.&quot;<br /> <br /> That isn't the game's fault, though.<br /> <br /> You gotta give to get.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:30:46-07:00","id":1136348,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136332#post1136332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Pff. Yeah, sure. The best and worst of humanity exploded into hyperfocus, all of the petty travesties and grotesque mis-decisions writ cosmic and &quot;There's little to no actual horror in Mage.&quot;<br /> <br /> Mages are terrifying-terrifying to be around and terrifying <i>to be</i>. And a significant part of that horror is the amount of people who don't catch what makes Mage horrifying.<br /> <br /> EDIT: And that's before you count all the ways that diving into magic warps you-they are default a monster.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> This. This is the point, and it's one of the reasons why Mage plays so badly with (or next to) other splats. Their players are off worrying about problems that are truly trivial to mages, while mage players are trying to hammer their minds into the box of &quot;how utilitarian a god will I be today?&quot; All while dicing (literally) with a reality that would very much like to have a word with them...!<br /> <br /> The tonal disconnect, from splats about body horror and loss of control and the like--fairly mundane acting-out of our terrors, to one of a higher plain of awareness of humanity and how you can break it, is why Mage is both a very good game and very hard to pull off right. And there is a &quot;right&quot; to mage, as otherwise you are playing superpower sub-genres, Cthulu investigators with powers, or Kult-lite (for a few examples).<br /> <br /> The point of the game is asking &quot;you can now solve that unsolvable problem&quot; answered by &quot;well, poop&quot; after doing so, interspersed with periods of banal academic effort and making a damn fine tea with your mind.<br /> <br /> So, Mage is really weird horror, and I'll admit after 20 years to still having a hard time playing, let alone storytelling, in it.<br /> <br /> --Khanwulf <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12493-khanwulf","name":"Khanwulf","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12493_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"898"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:35:42-07:00","id":1136352,"title":"","content":"1) It does talk about things like mind control being bad, that's what AoH is for.<br /> <br /> 2). If you can't put two and two together on that point by yourself then are you sure you can actually identify how to implement horror in the game in the way the game intends?<br /> <br /> 3) As Mage has a much higher level of versatility available to its characters the ST must be allowed an equal level of versatility for employing methods of bringing horror to bear for the players. If the only method you can think of is, &quot;Magic should be hard.&quot; then you're handicapping yourself.<br /> <br /> Mages do make horrific sacrifices by being reckless they sacrifice everything around them to get what they want.<br /> <br /> They do dance with dangerous forces, they actually convince themselves regularly that the Exarchs can be beaten, the Abyss can be sealed, the Lower Depths can be expunged and that yes the Death Lords and gods of the Shadow will bow to their whims.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:49:54-07:00","id":1136358,"title":"","content":"Gnosticism being fundamental to the setting aside, Mage and Kult focus on a different kind of horror. Mage is more about the kind of characters who have the chance to become slaves of their own magnificent cosmic powers and its consequences, the kind of things that can scare them and the secrets only they can hope to discover, plus what happens when they fight one against each other. Kult is, well, more about the terrible parts of a Gnostic universe? It's not a criticism, that's something cool to focus about.<br /> <br /> I mean, it's not exclusive and they sure overlap here and there, but it's less a matter of lacking teeth and more a matter of trying to bite a whole different target.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/815620-cinder-s-comprehensive-collection-of-creations\" target=\"_blank\">Cinder's Comprehensive Collection of Creations</a> - Homebrew Hub<br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/beast-the-primordial-aa/1172470-cinder-writes-about-beast-the-primordial\" target=\"_blank\">I write about Beast: The Primordial a lot</a>","edited":{"userid":38,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder","user":"Cinder","date":". 09-01-2017, 04:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/38-cinder","name":"Cinder","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar38_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1408"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:55:38-07:00","id":1136360,"title":"","content":"If you wanted a Mage game more Lovecraftian, where magic itself is dangerous to reality as sane humans understand it (as the entirety of human understanding is fundamentally flawed and terrible naive) then completely remove Mana as a Supernatural Fuel and instead have each Mana a Mage would spend count as a Success on a Paradox Die instead. Just that simple. That would make most uses of Magic, and in particular the potent uses having serious side-effect, and affecting things in ways never intending including bringing Abyssal Horrors into existence.<br /> <br /> To be clear the setting no longer has Mana, and instead things that would require the expenditure of Mana instead generate Automatic Parodox success worth of effect equal to that would be spent.<br /> <br /> Or Make it double tough and keep Mana while removing its Paradox mitigating effect and instead insert when used it produces Paradox as previous described. Both of these are on top of normal Paradox rules.<br /> <br /> When people say &quot;cost&quot; I don't believe they mean resources expenditure, I mean a Porsche is expensive in terms of financial resources but getting one isn't a source of horror. <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/SideEffectsInclude\" target=\"_blank\">And </a><a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/WithGreatPowerComesGreatInsanity\" target=\"_blank\">what</a> <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/BlessedWithSuck\" target=\"_blank\">I</a> <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/EquivalentExchange\" target=\"_blank\">understand </a><a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/JackassGenie\" target=\"_blank\">them </a><a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/BeCarefulWhatYouWishFor\" target=\"_blank\">to</a> <a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/PowerAtAPrice\" target=\"_blank\">mean </a><a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/NecessaryDrawback\" target=\"_blank\">is </a><a href=\"http://tvtropes.org/pmwiki/pmwiki.php/Main/DangerousForbiddenTechnique\" target=\"_blank\">this</a>. (&lt;-Note each word is a separate Trope link)<br /> <br /> My suggestions are harsh but would definitely lead to moral questions about the use of reality bending power and make the thought &quot;I could do X, but is X worth the risk?&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 09-01-2017, 04:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T13:56:41-07:00","id":1136361,"title":"","content":"If anything, Mage is actually more interesting to me - because it can be as horror inducing as the situation calls for.<br /> <br /> It isn't trying to tell me what horror is. It lets me come to that conclusion on my own.<br /> <br /> I don't find Cthulhu particularly scary. Nor do I find vampires or werewolves to be scary.<br /> <br /> Scary, to me, is the stories that happen in-between. Something that resonates with me as disturbing or unsettling.<br /> <br /> <br /> I'm not scared when I run out of resources. I'm scared when I'm not sure I can handle something, or I've grossly misunderstood a situation. I'm scared when I won a moral battle and lost the moral war.<br /> <br /> <br /> I'm very particular about comedy, and hate to be told or convinced that something is funny and I should be laughing now. Comedy, like horror, should be organic.<br /> <br /> No amount of spooky skeletons and blood-sucking (or the threat of being annihilated by sunlight) can make me think vampire is scary.<br /> <br /> If anything, all that other stuff just makes me think of edgy-goth vibes.<br /> <br /> Jump-scares are cute and all, but real horror is the stuff you think about when you're alone. Real horror, to me, is the stuff that gets to you even when you're with other people.<br /> <br /> &quot;You break your finger to cast a spell! There is blood and it hurts! Scary way to cast a spell, isn't it? Isn't it scary?! Are you unsettled? Have you been disturbed, or dare I say, frightened?!&quot;<br /> <br /> vs.<br /> <br /> &quot;Yeah, I checked him out. The spirit says it'll come out peacefully if we let it bang John Doe's wife one more time.&quot;<br /> &quot;Sounds good to me.&quot;<br /> &quot;Are you serious?&quot;<br /> &quot;I have shit to do, man. Nobody is getting hurt. Just let it fuck and fuck off.&quot;<br /> <br /> The second one is much more unsettling on several layers, to me.<br /> <br /> Adding an edgy or cryptic sacrifice to every spell would just make it super pretentious - like the game is saying magic is scary BECAUSE it is costly. When in reality, that isn't what I find scary about magic (or the supernatural) at all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":11166,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","user":"kryptonitekrusher","date":". 09-01-2017, 04:59 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T14:14:28-07:00","id":1136364,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136297#post1136297\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the best we can do is a game based on the writings of a racist hydrophobe, we're in bad fucking shape.<br /> (I *hate* Lovecraftian stuff)</div> </div> </div> </div>Please, don't put words in my mouth. You can separate the artist from the medium. Critiquing Lovecraft for his racism is like critiquing the early Loony Toons for the same reason: both are a product of their age.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136335#post1136335\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your inability to understand the horror inherent in easily abused magic is a flaw in your thinking, not in the setting or the system.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's nothing wrong with making consequences to casting supernal magic more risky, more dire, beyond some philosophical notion of how wise some action was or wasn't, if only to drive home the fact you're messing with shit you shouldn't. The issue comes down when that mentality of &quot;it's alright to psychically dominate this other PC&quot; presents itself, for instance. It's nothing adults around a game table can't handle, to be sure, but the game does lend itself to forget those boundaries. And when you cross that line, I sorta want something with more kick than what the game already offers.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11166_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11166\" data-vbnamecard=\"11166\">kryptonitekrusher</a> Yet, too bad they didn't introduced a touchstone mechanic to Awakening. Like they don't need anything save their mysteries.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T14:19:39-07:00","id":1136367,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136361#post1136361\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If anything, Mage is actually more interesting to me - because it can be as horror inducing as the situation calls for.<br /> <br /> It isn't trying to tell me what horror is. It lets me come to that conclusion on my own.<br /> <br /> I don't find Cthulhu particularly scary. Nor do I find vampires or werewolves to be scary.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>What you personally find scary is just about the most irrelevant thing when determining the genre of a piece of fiction. Vampires aren't scary to me, but Requiem is obviously a horror game. Someone who is scared of dogs reading Pugmire wouldn't transform that game into a horror game, either.<br /> <br /> When I say Mage isn't a horror game because the magic isn't scary, I'm not saying &quot;I, personally, do not find this magic scary&quot;, I'm saying it's because the magic isn't intended to be scary at all. It's not built or structured the way horror magic works. Magic in Mage is scary he same way a gun is scary; the possible abuse and consequences of its use. Which ain't horror, though it might be scary.<br /> <br /> The horror genre, like any genre, is a collection of tropes organized around a particular intent. Mage lacks both the tropes and intent of the horror genre, but has the urban fantasy ones. QED.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 05:23 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T14:59:05-07:00","id":1136385,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136364#post1136364\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's nothing wrong with making consequences to casting supernal magic more risky, more dire, beyond some philosophical notion of how wise some action was or wasn't, if only to drive home the fact you're messing with shit you shouldn't. The issue comes down when that mentality of &quot;it's alright to psychically dominate this other PC&quot; presents itself, for instance. It's nothing adults around a game table can't handle, to be sure, but the game does lend itself to forget those boundaries. And when you cross that line, I sorta want something with more kick than what the game already offers.<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar11166_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"11166\" data-vbnamecard=\"11166\">kryptonitekrusher</a> Yet, too bad they didn't introduced a touchstone mechanic to Awakening. Like they don't need anything save their mysteries.</div> </div> </div> </div>I didn't say there was anything wrong with magic having costs. I said that magic having costs wouldn't benefit Mage or add anything to the horror that Mage is about. Also, when speaking of costs I mean strictly those that could be incurred in order to cast a spell. Magic in Mage has tons of other kinds of costs, but they're paid -after- the spell is cast in consequences. <br /> <br /> If the table isn't capable of remembering the what they're doing is horrific, and the ST isn't bothering to remember the rules for Wisdom, then yes the impact of the horror in the setting is weakened. That's not a flaw in the game though, that's a flaw in those playing the game by 1) Not taking the game seriously in its own context, and 2) incompetent ST who isn't utilizing all of their available tools.<br /> <br /> Also, Mages don't need anything other than their Mysteries, that's one of the things that makes them horrific.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136367#post1136367\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The horror genre, like any genre, is a collection of tropes organized around a particular intent. Mage lacks both the tropes and intent of the horror genre, but has the urban fantasy ones. QED.</div> </div> </div> </div>Your inability to see the horror right in front of you doesn't mean others are as blind to it, or that it's not there.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T15:27:12-07:00","id":1136400,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136367#post1136367\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's not built or structured the way horror magic works.</div> </div> </div> </div>Magic in Mage involves parceling a bit of the omnipresent symbolic cloud that is your power into discrete pieces and sticking them to places and things that then have reality contrive to rubber-band back to you. Some of the things you do with those agents endangers your ability to limit the scope of this rubber-banding unless they're one of the small number of spells that you've given more wiggle-room to (which themselves are more likely to go weird or wrong without additional preparation or oversight), and keeping each of those agents on-task in the long-term requires a little bit of your mystical focus or a big chunk of your personal will. Heightening your mystical insight gives you more specifically-supernatural alterations to your destiny that also serve as channels for better weakening your hold on your magic, which itself helps you heighten your mystical insight.<br /> <br /> Mages are the only splat whose power structure is built around their actively using them all the time despite the long-term consequences of doing so, and the way Wisdom and Obsessions work means that that rapidly goes downhill if they cross the wrong lines on an impulse. &quot;The rush of dragging down the heavens to do your bidding may blind you to the burning shell of your surroundings&quot; is a horror best viewed in retrospect or from outside, yes, but &quot;you got what you wanted to happen just then to happen and now everything else is dust and smoke&quot; does not lose the dust and the smoke just because you got what you wanted to happen to happen.<br /> <br /> Magic is an extension of a mage's identity \u2014 its inclination is to <i>be</i>, and without care, being precedes deliberation. The tension Mage deals with in its horror is between that all-important preparation that makes magic work most safely and effectively and the fact that there's very little to discourage you from trying over and over again in rapid succession at the start \u2014 carelessness is not only easy but encouraged in a lot of the mechanics that also incentivize stringent maintenance of your magical identity. The most basic use of Reach is saying &quot;I need this to happen in the next thirty seconds, not an hour from now,&quot; and the most high-spec Yantra available to more than one spell is &quot;this is something it makes sense for me to be able to do as Shadow-Name the Example-Mage.&quot; Mages stick bits of themselves everywhere they do magic, and they are themselves people to whom magic literally comes naturally \u2014 there's a reason the two archetypical ends of the scale are isolated hermitage and fast-track to self-destruction with extra collateral damage.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T15:30:16-07:00","id":1136401,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136364#post1136364\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The issue comes down when that mentality of &quot;it's alright to psychically dominate this other PC&quot; presents itself, for instance. It's nothing adults around a game table can't handle, to be sure, but the game does lend itself to forget those boundaries. And when you cross that line, I sorta want something with more kick than what the game already offers.</div> </div> </div> </div>Might I recommend the Atrocity and Supports systems in my homebrew 2ed conversion of Danse Macabre's <i>Atrocity</i> and <i>Hell is Other People</i> to be universally applicable to all supernaturals?<br /> <br /> Given how easy Atrocities are for Mages to get away with it becomes a bit like a noose they can tighten themselves when the metaphysicial consequences of being horrible is becoming literally more monstrous as you do more Atrocities, alienating everyone who you care about.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136385#post1136385\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your inability to see the horror right in front of you doesn't mean others are as blind to it, or that it's not there.</div> </div> </div> </div><a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Graham%27s_Hierarchy_of_Disagreement.svg\" target=\"_blank\">I think directly refuting his point, if you're able, by listing tropes with links and quoting pages would be more effective than simply stating contradicting point of view.</a> I invite you to do so. Pointing out someone's inability to see something is not particularly helpful to them or enlightening to others, and if we're not all trying to help each other what's the point of this conversation?<br /> <br /> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar111_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"111\" data-vbnamecard=\"111\">Satchel</a> This is an excellent example of a good argument for horror already in the setting, thank you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 09-01-2017, 06:43 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T15:40:02-07:00","id":1136406,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136385#post1136385\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Your inability to see the horror right in front of you doesn't mean others are as blind to it, or that it's not there.</div> </div> </div> </div>Different people like different things, different people want different things out of mage. Why this insistence that one specific way to evoke horror via mage is the &quot;right&quot; way? That's just.... weird. WoD games have allways highlighted the golden rule. If people want to play mage differently and not focus on hubris of completely unchecked power as a central theme, then why do we have to correct them? This just kind of baffles me, the thread started out somewhat well, but now it's just become a discussion about what way to evoke horror is correct, which is frankly a bit stupid for the aforementioned reasons.<br /> <br /> Another thing to keep in mind: why not both? Mages don't need to be all powerful to live out their hubris tbh. Alot of that hubris is perpetrated against sleepers or other beings that can't defend themselves anyway. Only having other mages as potential opponents (in a straight up fight anyway, I am aware of the danger of family members being assassinated and the like... though honestly time and fate magic both shit all over that kind of plot) is not that important to their hubris. For example something I personally do is change clash pools for spirits from rank (+ influence if using an influence) to rank + attribute. A mage can still bully around rank 1 and 2 spirits around and they can still infiltrate the shadow and do their various shenannigangs, but a beginning mage can at least not piss into the face of a lesser incarna anymore with a casually erected spell and expect to win most of the time. Yes mage is about the horrible consequences of your actions... but you can really have that theme without mages being unassailable through shielding spells. If I have an apprentice unwisely poke at pandoras box and release a major plague spirit I'd like that to do more than just &quot;oh look its a spirit hahaha isn't it cute, now go back into the box again.&quot; I personally don't need magic to be edgy, but I like there to be consequences beyond just the possiblity of players looking at themselves and thinking &quot;oh that's fucked up&quot;. That alone rarely drives plot (it drives plot in specific scenes but itself needs a story to fit in).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T15:56:51-07:00","id":1136412,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"5241\" data-vbnamecard=\"5241\">Flinty</a> I'm not saying they're liking Mage wrong, or that there is a &quot;right&quot; way to invoke horror in Mage. I'm saying the horror is already there, and that it takes either willful ignorance or an unfortunate blind spot in the employment of critical thinking in order to be unable to detect it. It's fine if they want to change things to go for a specific flavor of horror or whatever, what I object to is the insistence that the game does not achieve its objectives just because they fail to understand what those are. It's like trying to build a better car when you don't actually know how combustion engines work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136401#post1136401\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://&amp;quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Graham%27s_Hierarchy_of_Disagreement.svg&amp;quot;]I</a> think directly refuting his point, if you're able, by listing tropes with links and quoting pages would be more effective than simply stating contradicting point of view.[/URL] I invite you to do so. Pointing out someone's inability to see something is not particularly helpful to them or enlightening to others, and if we're not all trying to help each other what's the point of this conversation.</div> </div> </div> </div>I already did, I don't see the need to repeat my points when the fact is the only thing that remains is that they just don't get it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 09-01-2017, 07:00 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T16:01:11-07:00","id":1136414,"title":"","content":"I suppose what I am trying to say is that horror is intimately dependant on your perspective. Something that scares one person alot may not affect another at all. But yes <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2632\" data-vbnamecard=\"2632\">Mrmdubois</a>, mage does indeed hold up when it comes to horror through the lens of hubris. It's just not what everybody expects from it, I suppose. And honestly I can sort of understand where they're coming from. Hellblazer as has been pointed out earlier is quoted as an inspiration for mage for example and evokes a completely different kind of horror than a game of Awakening played by the book will.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T16:03:52-07:00","id":1136415,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <a href=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/7c/Graham%27s_Hierarchy_of_Disagreement.svg\" target=\"_blank\">I think directly refuting his point, if you're able, by listing tropes with links and quoting pages would be more effective than simply stating contradicting point of view.</a> I invite you to do so. Pointing out someone's inability to see something is not particularly helpful to them or enlightening to others, and if we're not all trying to help each other what's the point of this conversation? </div> </div> </div>Nah, don't worry about that. It wouldn't be a mage argument on the internet if it didn't have someone being rude and condescending.<br /> <br /> What made me realize I didn't care for Mage as part of the CofD (though it's not without its merits on its own, mind) is a passage in GURPS Horror, where Ken Hite describes how magic <i>in horror stories</i> is different from fantasy magic. I think it's a pretty good set of criteria that defines horror magic from fantasy magic. The principles, paraphrased, were as follows:<br /> <br /> -Magic is serious. &quot;Someone who trivially uses magic should be as scary as someone who trivially uses plutonium.&quot; Mage arguably has this already and in fact seems to be largely the response I've been getting. The rest of this bullet point is about the investment, cost, price of magical power. Mage is a bit light on this stuff for my tastes but, eh, it's a gameplay convenience. Point conceded.<br /> <br /> -Magic is dangerous. This is definitely not enough I think; for powerful magic there should be a lot more risk of physical injury, or mutation, or madness, or being sucked into a vortex or possessed or whatever. Magic is pretty reliably safe in Mage unless you go looking for paradox. Yes, mages have adversaries that are quite dangerous, but that's an issue of relative power, not intrinsic horror.<br /> <br /> -Magic is uncanny. &quot;Even in the most overt full-magic campaign, mages shouldn't truly understand the powers they toy with.&quot; I've thought since Mage 1E we got far too much reliable information about the underlying metaphysics, cosmology, and secret history. I'd love to see more uncertainty about magic in the game; the same way Requiem 2nd introduced stuff like Revenants and additional lost clans, failed embraces, etc. Let's get broken watchtowers, lost paths, a fallen Order that exists only during certain celestial events, etc. Just go nuts. Mage: The Ascension was once described as reducing magic to something as interesting as VCR repair, which is a criticism I think could be levied at Awakening just as well. It's just too...explained. Ars Magica does a good job at having Hermetic Theory be really broad and powerful, but limited in ways that mysterious other magics were not. Stuff like that would be good for Awakening.<br /> <br /> -Magic is scary. This is the big one. Magic in horror stories is Just Not Right; it has horrific spoor. Milk spoils. Candles flicker. Power surges. &quot;If you aren't scared to use it to save your life, it isn't black enough for horror.&quot; Magic in Mage is arguably a desirable, empowering thing for humanity; heck, two of the factions (three, really) are all about that.<br /> <br /> Mage magic feels like fantasy magic, not horror magic. Which is why I don't think it fits in a horror setting, because it's just not black enough magic to be That Old Black Magic.<br /> <br /> Envision an alternative, darker Mage, where the obsessions are more consuming, the magic more perilous (say you cut the Supernal Realms and all magic is the engineered use of the Abyss to violate reality for your own goals). Does that lose the hubris and abuse of powers aspect? I don't think so.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 07:08 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T16:10:59-07:00","id":1136419,"title":"","content":"Magic is Dangerous: Mages poke at shit they don't understand all the time. They're not as threatened by their own magic [Usually](as in that which a Mage personally wields), but they're certainly threatened by the magic of others and that of the Mysteries they perpetually stumble across.<br /> <br /> Magic is Uncanny will be covered in SoS, although frankly it's always been assumed that Mages don't know everything about magic. If they did then one Order would be right.<br /> <br /> Magic is scary is an obvious function of Long Term Nimbus, and Shadow Name.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T16:29:10-07:00","id":1136427,"title":"","content":"Not addressing any specific point, but I think a lot of complaints re:horror grow out of players forgetting that <i>Mages are the monsters.</i> It's an easy thing to forget, since pound for pound they're still (to a bigger degree than any other splat except probably Hunter) human, but Mages aren't the guys who are (typically) menaced by the forces they wield, any more than a vampire is menaced by drinking blood. They menace others by doing things like accidentally wiping half of their family out of the timeline while insisting it was the right choice, or drive people insane by repeatedly exposing them to arcane powers to Awaken them while insisting it's for their own good, or who carelessly release spells that they forget to check on which make an entire city block now exist in a separate dimension five minutes in the future where the air makes you think every day is like Sunday.<br /> <br /> Magic isn't usually scary to a mage in the way we usually think of scary when it comes to horror films and books. They usually see it as a holy, pure, important force. Even as everyone is screaming and their eyes are bleeding at the rock inscribed with the so-called &quot;holy&quot; symbol. That's it's horror: the things you are allowing to happen and the things you are becoming because of it.<br /> <br /> Forcing Mages to use horrifying regents or to have them always risk turning into tentacled monsters (the first of which they <i>can</i> do if they want and the second they sort of already risk if they aren't careful with Paradox) is something you can do, if you feel it fits the sort of tone for the game you're looking to run, but as a &quot;fix&quot; to a perceived &quot;problem&quot; in the game it comes across as missing the point.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Come visit our unofficial Mage: the Awakening Discord Server!<br /> <a href=\"https://discord.gg/Z42DJ9h\" target=\"_blank\">https://discord.gg/Z42DJ9h</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13241-biston","name":"Biston","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13241_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"50"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T16:32:35-07:00","id":1136429,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Biston</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136427#post1136427\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Forcing Mages to use horrifying regents or to have them always risk turning into tentacled monsters (the first of which they <i>can</i> do if they want and the second they sort of already risk if they aren't careful with Paradox) is something you can do, if you feel it fits the sort of tone for the game you're looking to run, but as a &quot;fix&quot; to a perceived &quot;problem&quot; in the game it comes across as missing the point.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not a problem that Mage isn't a horror game. WoD has always been half urban fantasy anyway, Mage just leans in that direction a lot more than the CofD generally does, which poses a genre interaction problem for crossovers.<br /> <br /> EDIT: Okay, I mean it's not a problem <i>for Mage</i>. The crossover issue is basically that Mages are coming out of a different genre, and this is reflected tonally and mechanically. The advice earlier in the thread for incorporating mages into other game lines also serve to make mage more of a horror game - making magic more dangerous, more costly, more uncertain, and less convenient also serves to reduce the mage's tendency to just solve all the problems the other characters have and then move on to punching gods in the face.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 07:37 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T16:38:34-07:00","id":1136431,"title":"","content":"All the gamelines have different themes. A crossover necessarily requires finding a compromise between them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T16:39:26-07:00","id":1136432,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136431#post1136431\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">All the gamelines have different themes. A crossover necessarily requires finding a compromise between them.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's what I've been saying!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T16:48:06-07:00","id":1136436,"title":"","content":"If that were all you were saying I wouldn't be disagreeing with you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T17:05:04-07:00","id":1136440,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"543\" data-vbnamecard=\"543\">Professor Phobos</a> I see where you are coming from, really, and I wouldn't mind seeing a hack like that making Mage a different Horror game, but I still disagree with the notion that Mage is not a Horror game, or that Magic is not dangerous/scary.<br /> We've covered the former extensively, so here's why for the latter: the magic itself needn't be the source of the horror in the first place, despite Ken Hite's opinion. The <i>usage </i>of it is plenty source for horror, already.<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Which is why I don't think it fits in a horror setting, because it's just not black enough magic to be That Old Black Magic. </div> </div> </div> I disagree: occasionally fraying reality, calling down monstrosities from beyond, potentially even contributing to the Abyss's growth in power (The Guardians had this belief that Vulgar magic strenghtens the Lie, right?)... those things make it very fitting for a Horror setting. But most of all, the Horror of the magic comes from the ease of its use! Being able to rewrite minds makes it so easy to give in and make people think the way I want them to. After all, there is little incentive in stopping me, right`? It encourages the theme of Hubris, sure, but also makes using Magic for horrific means much easier. &quot;I need to sacrifice a goat and a small child's right thumb for this spell... you know what, on second thought, I'll just go shoot him!&quot; or &quot;This might make me crazy, is it really worth it?&quot; vs. &quot;What an annoying twat! I'll make him forget his college years. Let's see how you'll do without that PhD, prick!&quot; or even &quot;Hey, hey! See that skater over there? Watch this! *imitates Force Push, guy falls down* Ahahahaha! Aw man, the look on his face!&quot; Both are valid Horror scenarios, albeit very, very different.<br /> <br /> Being able to level cities with little trouble, burn down buildings on accident and guarantee I never have to go to work via winning small lottery tickets/finding wallets etc., covering my daily expenses... those things remove me from humanity, sure, but they are so <i>damn </i>easy. Temptively easy, really. Why bother doing things the old-fashioned way?<br /> After all, spells are reliable- most of the time. But if something works most of the time, and only in 0,1% of the time goes really bad, and you fling this stuff around ten times a day, how long does it take for something to go really bad? On average, about 4 months. And this is generous: most mages are likely to use magic more than ten times a day; it is, after all, so very reliable. Especially those actively fighting, like Arrows. Thing is, you won't see it coming or expect it, BECAUSE it has been so reliable. It will most likely fail in a very unfortunate moment, too. Ever went into a situation with a tool, confident you can handle it thanks to that tool, then have the tool fail you? Now imagine this could be life-threatening, soul-shattering, reality-ripping or destiny-fraying.<br /> Imagine you relize you don't really know how to deal with situations outside of using your magic anymore.<br /> And even if it works as intended: there are so many, many horrible things done through magic, even on accident or through a misunderstanding.<br /> Is this not your brand of Horror? That is fine. But it is horror.<br /> <br /> Regarding black magic that would make you scared to use it even when you're about to die: It helps with a certain atmosphere, but I wouldn't want to play it. There would be little to no incentive to invest in something like that, imo. Unless you make it so that it's the only way to face certain threats, and then we're simply in Cthulhoid territory. Works, sure. Horrifying? Yes.<br /> But anyone who says a Merlin having to face a Morrigan is not horrifying sees the world through a different lense than I do. Their magic is beautiful, and not scary in and of itself, but in what it can do and how it can be used. If two basically Demi-gods slug it out, I do not want to be anywhere near them. Preferably, not even in that plane of existence. As was already mentioned: Power difference like that is freaking scary. People flinging around high power like that, without any real merit as to why they got it is like arming every other jack on the streets with military-grade weaponry.<br /> <br /> This is mostly incoherent and lacking a thread since it is late, I apologize for that. G'night.<br /> <br /> Edit: basically, what Biston said. Ninja'd due to slow typing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8542,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","user":"Wormwood","date":". 09-01-2017, 08:07 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T17:11:54-07:00","id":1136444,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wormwood</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136440#post1136440\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"543\" data-vbnamecard=\"543\">Professor Phobos</a> I see where you are coming from, really, and I wouldn't mind seeing a hack like that making Mage a different Horror game, but I still disagree with the notion that Mage is not a Horror game, or that Magic is not dangerous/scary.<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>You know, honestly, I can just concede this, because the difference between me thinking it's not a horror game and me thinking it's not good at being a horror game are inconsequential.<br /> <br /> I do think Mage could benefit from a flatter power curve just on its own merits, but I like low-key, grounded, local nWoD rather than high power nWoD, and that's not been the design philosophy in years. Maybe the games have just moved past me. I wanted a game about sorcerers and they made one about wizards. That's fine.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 08:14 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T17:15:17-07:00","id":1136446,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136444#post1136444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You know, honestly, I can just concede this, because the difference between me thinking it's not a horror game and me thinking it's not good at being a horror game are inconsequential.<br /> <br /> I do think Mage could benefit from a flatter power curve just on its own merits, but I like low-key, grounded, local nWoD rather than high power nWoD, and that's not been the design philosophy in years. Maybe the games have just moved past me.</div> </div> </div> </div>Point.<br /> <br /> Isn't Deviant supposed to be low-power, very body-horror focused? Might be just your thing, and is definitly a move away from high-power nWoD (well, apparently there will be high power skills, but for a terrible price e.g. going out with a blast). I know I am looking forward to that for that very reason, and seems to fit some of your requirements for a good horror game =)<br /> <br /> Also, sorry for being off topic.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T17:34:22-07:00","id":1136457,"title":"","content":"Not to derail the thread but does lack of horror get brought up in Demon? I see Demon as primarily a espionage game and maybe dont see much horror in it (being cyber demons isnt horrifying to me).<br /> <br /> What different types of horror are there? personal horror (vampire, mage), external horror (mage, hunter?), survival horror (changeling, hunters? prommies? mage again), jump scares (Beast?), probably others. Depending on the story you want to tell you can touch on all of these horror themes in almost any chronicles game its just up to the ST/players to deliver.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T17:37:19-07:00","id":1136458,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136457#post1136457\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not to derail the thread but does lack of horror get brought up in Demon? I see Demon as primarily a espionage game and maybe dont see much horror in it (being cyber demons isnt horrifying to me).</div> </div> </div> </div>Demon is an edge case in certain respects, but I count it as horror. Werewolf and Mage I count as the &quot;non-horror&quot; odd men out. Arguably Geist, but I really like Geist so I try not to argue the point.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":543,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","user":"Professor Phobos","date":". 09-01-2017, 08:40 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-01T21:10:09-07:00","id":1136485,"title":"","content":"I somehow feel compelled to reply to this thread because I had so many crossovers with my players over the years that these kinds of pondering and evaluating various splats is my bread and butter. I speak not only from the theory but from the practice too and actual play and game sessions. <br /> <br /> Before I start giving some advice (hoping they could be useful for u) some considerations :<br /> <br /> 1) 1vs 1, white room scenarios, pvp etc... ARE NOT BALANCED scenarios. What does this means? It simply means that some characters and some supernatural creatures are weaker or stronger than others in a straight up challenge on the street. Period. No beating around the bushes, a mastigos can insta kill any enemy with mind domination, and an acanthus can spoil and ruin entire game sessions with deus ex machina shifting sands. <br /> <br /> 2) GLOBAL scenarios and scenarios where strategy, time and resources are involved, allies influence etc.. CAN be balanced with planning. <br /> Notice the emphasis on the can factor and I didn't use must. <br /> <br /> Simply put, where others might shine in sheer potency and might, maybe others shine in.. Deceiving and numbers. Keep in mind that the only games I really know are the main three, vampire mage and werewolf. I tend to focus on these because in my setting usually other splats are not that common or are politically irrelevant to the crossover (I know I might be missing out and other games are fantastic but it was a choice we made as a group, pronetheans and beasts Geist etc... are weird and rare creatures that are very reclusive dangerous and misterious to find) <br /> <br /> <br /> That having been said, I will try to give my best two cents. I had to master a battle between a local coterie and a small cabal of mages and I had to think about ways to interfere with mages affair and how that war would play out. Here are some thoughts on vampires and wolves specifically that can help u balance settings:<br /> <br /> -in my games, vampires have the political, mortal strings of anything in the city, and they can and will use it to their advantage_even when we are talking about ordo Dracul weirdos, Lance a sanctum knights etc.. I tend to portray the undead society with that feeling of omnipotence and manipulation over entire cities political entities and authorities. Police, newspapers, politicians, mafia mobs... Anything can be bought used and corrupted to attack the group. Only elder mages in the silver ladder or seers can hope to compete with the kind of influence a political coterie can pull with small effort. If u go at war with vampires and they know about it, u will face tens of ghouls armed, police and corrupted investigators, hunters and the entire human society in the city. They will try to ruin ur reputation or make up accuses to make u a terrorist or a wanted criminal. They will hunt and arrest kill or kidnap parents friends and lovers.. Or maybe turn them into ghoul. <br /> <br /> -vampires have the numbers. In my setting mages usually are rare and cabals are smaller than coteries or covenant. Even when they are at peace, vampires have higher rates of recruitment than mages. The city is full of ghouls and servants, elite characters who are ready to be embraced if necessary and are not simply cannon fodder they start as good vampire characters like players would do. If they have to resort to it even the Prince will start embrace new candidates to fight the wizards menace. Usually the vampires outnumbers the mages and will outnumber them if they have to fight a long war with small losses. Usually I have vampires outnumber mages 3 to 1.<br /> <br /> -&quot;the enemy of my enemy....&quot;_ let's be honest, do u think that if out there people crazy powerful like mages walked the night , social predators like vampires wouldn't know how to deal with them during the centuries? Vampires are not stupid and will have contacts, strings and allies in the awakened society. If they have to face a cabal, they will know someone who know someone .... And will find another cabal, order or crazy sclelestus abyssal mage that can be useful to them. Also they might as well have some mage dominated enslaved or straight up open allied with them. Vampires can be formidable manipulators and can grant u many many favors. After all, even the most hardened adamantine arrow can fall in love with a daeva innocent young girl.... <br /> In practical terms, vampires can access to allies, be it wolves, mages or entities that can counter the mages power. Usually in my city they are allied with the seers and will call them in case of assistance. <br /> <br /> -if vampires have to face mages, I give a small buff and emphasis on blood magic, occult and lore. What does it mean? Occult is very powerful, and the most alien and Witchery covenants and coteries will have knowledge on how to counter magic and other powers. I also allow for scales, rituals, sorcery and devotions that can imitate or counter specific awakened magic. Like a theban sorcery or devotion combo that can allow for a clash of wills against an acanthus trying to predict the future of the coterie, or open a scrying window... Thing is, I don't downplay occult lore, I enhance it and use it as a powerful vampire tool. <br /> Those weird undead might possess primitive blood magic, but it can be used against awakened magic! (shock on mages) <br /> <br /> -obfuscate can overpower even mage sight, remember it folks. Spies and assassins can cut mages throat on the street without even them realizing. And vampires will never ever play it fair. <br /> <br /> Small appendix on our fur friends called werewolves :<br /> <br /> -honestly werewolves are pretty strong in a fight, and when they fall into rage they can even resist mind control and activate devastating power at will without essence. Ina straight up fight without preparation they are very scary. <br /> <br /> -I really try to put the emphasis on the fact that wolves aren't simply brutes ignorant who go around biting enemies, they are strong cohesive apex predator who work as a Navy seal team and a family or tribe. They will use smart tactics, spirits as fodder cannons and careful study of the situation and the prey before acting. Don't just throw them around like wild animals. <br /> <br /> <br /> -small factor that I usually showcase_ &quot;dear thyrsus, u might have the theory and the numbers but ithaeurs and bone shadows have the blood&quot; what does it mean? <br /> Wolves will enjoy contacts, allies and spirit influences that other mages can only dream of or reach only if they are very powerful. They enjoy spiritual connection and affinity for the umbra that few thyrsus can match. Totem wise, a pack is very powerful. <br /> <br /> -don't ever underestimate spirits. Influence can be literally anything. A spirit with the concept of time or fate can counter the abilities of an acanthus , and a psychiatry or mental sickness spirit can protect and counter even the worst nightmare of a mastigos power. If the wolves understand the basic of the powers they are facing, they will seek for a spirit ally that will counter those powers and challenge them in their influence. <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I know that these advices and little changes won't change the overall power difference between single individuals, but they can change the overall tone of the setting, and maybe can help u reevaluate other splats capability and resources, and feel less the awakened menace.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4247-lasombra","name":"Lasombra","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4247_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"179"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T03:50:16-07:00","id":1136533,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136444#post1136444\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> You know, honestly, I can just concede this, because the difference between me thinking it's not a horror game and me thinking it's not good at being a horror game are inconsequential.<br /> <br /> I do think Mage could benefit from a flatter power curve just on its own merits, but I like low-key, grounded, local nWoD rather than high power nWoD, and that's not been the design philosophy in years. Maybe the games have just moved past me. I wanted a game about sorcerers and they made one about wizards. That's fine.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Isn't this wanting your cake and eating it, too?<br /> <br /> By that I mean there are plenty of ways to roleplay a &quot;magic/supernatural ability user&quot; within CofD and remain low-key or less capable than mages. Hurt Locker and the 2e Mage corebook give info on supernatural merits and splats that operate as essentially mini-mages.<br /> <br /> Why not just do that? (This is aimed at the OP as well.) <br /> <br /> From what I recall, they can still be somewhat powerful without having anywhere near as much freedom as mages.<br /> <br /> This might be a case of applying the wrong tool for the job. Mage isn't intended to be strictly low-key or restrained - the overarching themes are freedom and consequence.<br /> <br /> I doubt you run into the same issues as a supernatural merit-based splat or Proximi in a troupe of other CofD splats.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T04:01:54-07:00","id":1136537,"title":"","content":"<b>I would simply took out Instant Casting - ANY magic need to be ritual.</b> This way magic is more colorful - you do ritual for anything - and other monster can exploit greatest weakness of Awakened ( in this hack ) - being weakest when surprised. Simple solution to all crossover issues. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T04:05:18-07:00","id":1136538,"title":"","content":"No, see, then everyone would just need time magic and have to prepare their spells in advance like a DnD wizard using Hung Spell.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T04:29:29-07:00","id":1136541,"title":"","content":"that would still limit every mage to having to have Time, though.<br /> Still, I prefer Kryptonitekrusher's approach here.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T04:47:34-07:00","id":1136548,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136538#post1136538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, see, then everyone would just need time magic and have to prepare their spells in advance like a DnD wizard using Hung Spell.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, only those who need magic <i>now</i> would need to Hang spells - Mages-as-researchers wouldn't need immediate access to magic in many cases. And the restriction to Hung Spells would limit the havoc they can get up to.<br /> <br /> Still, Ritual time and/or default Duration would need some tinkering.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T05:13:45-07:00","id":1136556,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136248#post1136248\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Being able to trivialize ghosts and spirits doesn't mean that Mage isn't balanced against itself. Try again.</div> </div> </div> </div> Not what I said. I'm objecting to the idea that mage v. mage balance is all that matters. This idea that a mage v. mage balance being all that matters is untrue for many, because it does matter in terms of what kinds of stories you get to tell.<br /> <br /> I'm sorry, but even without crossover, mages being the magical heavy weights they are has a massive impact on the amount and types of stories you tell. This is something direct experiences tells us as well as various comments by the writers. <br /> <br /> You cannot say it doesn't matter, because it very much does matter. There are definitive impacts on the game. You might like the end result, and you know what? More power to you. If you like that kind of game, awesome. But as a cost, there are other types of stories that you cannot engage in. And that does matter in the long run in terms of a pure mage game. This isn't even crossover, because spirits and ghosts are a part of core mage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136289#post1136289\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think people who have a problem with mages being strong are missing a CORE theme of Mage: Hubris.</div> </div> </div> </div> No, they're not missing the theme. The problem is that many people feel that hubris should have a stronger effect associated with it. Or better mechanics. Which is a very valid claim to make.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136332#post1136332\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> terrifying <i>to be</i>. And a significant part of that horror is the amount of people who don't catch what makes Mage horrifying.</div> </div> </div> </div> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">Your inability to understand the horror inherent in easily abused magic is a flaw in your thinking, not in the setting or the system.</span> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"font-family:inherit\">There's a reason why there's so many people make claims that mages aren't monsters with completely straight faces. This is not something that can be brushed off by saying that people &quot;don't get it.&quot;<br /> <br /> Invariably, every reader's interpretation of a material won't always match up to the writers' intentions. If its only a handful, then that's only inevitable. If it starts to reach a notable portion of the reader base? Then it becomes less about the people and becomes an issue with the presentation. Its ironically hubris to assume the problem here is always on the readers when there are multiple factors at play here. </span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 09-02-2017, 08:25 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T05:31:49-07:00","id":1136560,"title":"","content":"You might lose certain stories, but you gain others. So there is a trade-off. It isn't just a net loss, I don't think. You could interpret it that way, but I just don't see it that way.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;Better mechanics&quot; is relative. Many would argue they're fine. So nobody is gaining any ground on that issue other than labeling preferences.<br /> <br /> What constitutes a notable portion of the reader base? What is the data set we're using? Forum posts? People that own/play Mage vs. people that post on reddit/Onyx Path forums?<br /> <br /> Do we opt to change everything in favor of the minority we cannot demonstrably quantify?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I'll level with you and admit that saying &quot;You don't get it because you don't get it&quot; is self-serving and doesn't do anything to move the discussion forward. I will also grant you that it is possible for implementation or design of products to fail; hence, one of the major reasons that design theory exists.<br /> <br /> But I'm not sure how to interpret your claim that a &quot;notable portion of the reader base&quot; isn't interpreting the writer's work as intended. What is notable about it other than it being a number of complaints greater than 1 and less than some non-descript variable (50% of the total)?<br /> <br /> At what point does presentation need to be addressed? More specifically, why should it be addressed if we are to believe most people who take it upon themselves to play Mage aren't having this issue?<br /> <br /> Of course, my basis for believing the majority is retaining the writer's intentions is simply the lack of thread topics (with respect to all other forum topics) pertaining to the issue of whether or not Mage constitutes horror. Sure, they exist. But what about it?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T06:06:54-07:00","id":1136567,"title":"","content":"The notion that horror requires strong mechanics to showcase itself is completely missing the point. ALL the game lines have their own forms of horror and how well the storyteller displays this has absolutely nothing to do with the mechanics of the game. It has everything to do with how the storyteller describes situations and circumstances in the game. In Mage, it's the storyteller's job to get the players to be disgusted with their own choices and thereby showing how mages are monsters. It isn't the job of the developers of the game to scare you, that's your job. It isn't the job of the developers to make you disgusted with your own choices, that's your job. Furthermore, attempting to place more stringent mechanics on the setting to enforce the horror of the setting actually detracts from said horror when the type of horror you're trying to present is that of how people hurt other people by being selfish! You don't get players to be horrified by what they make their characters do by placing stronger mechanics that detract from their capability to do horrific things. Nor do you get players to be horrified by what they make their characters do by introducing mechanics that &quot;present horror.&quot; Trying to enforce horror through mechanics simply is a losing battle. Mechanics are like facts whereas horror is emotional. If you're trying to get your players to be horrified (no matter where that horror is coming from), you're shooting yourself in the foot by trying to do so with mechanics instead of vivid descriptions, plot twists, and graphic details.<br /> <br /> If showcasing the consequences of horrific actions isn't your cup of tea, fine. But please don't suggest that there isn't horror within Mage or that it isn't actual horror. Normal people doing evil things is quite horrific as can be seen whenever you look long enough at history.<br /> <br /> As to the topic on hand, yes, I believe the op has it wrong. He has it wrong within the very title of his post. There is no line for Mage to be brought into. That implies that the developers of the game went rogue. Chronicles of Darkness is specifically designed to not have game lines that are balanced against each other. Suggesting that one game line needs to be strengthened or nerfed to bring it into line implies that you don't understand the intent of the developers and what the games are actually trying to do. Every game has its own themes and moods and any time anyone decides to play a crossover game doing so will explicitly detract from at least one of those themes or moods, which goes directly against game design.<br /> <br /> Now maybe you want to go against game design. Maybe you really do want to have that crossover game. Sometimes that is fun, and honestly, the point of these games is to have fun. So you decide to have a crossover game. Ok. Fine. Great even. Here's my point though: if you don't know how to change the abilities of one splat to make them weaker or stronger you probably haven't played the game long enough to appreciate it for its own merits. Now, it's perfectly possible that you just don't like a game for its own merits. However, by the time you've played a game long enough that you COULD appreciate it for its own merits (presuming you don't have a natural inclination against said game, which of course just begs the question of &quot;Why did you want to play this game again?&quot;), you should be familiar enough with its mechanics that you can easily see how strengthen or weaken the splat. And THAT isn't even considering how ALL these games use xp to purchase higher dots of abilities (i.e. just use a weaker mage, or a weaker werewolf, or a weaker vampire, etc).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7825,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","user":"Falcon777","date":". 09-02-2017, 09:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T07:10:07-07:00","id":1136583,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2102_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2102\" data-vbnamecard=\"2102\">MCN</a> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2757_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2757\" data-vbnamecard=\"2757\">proindrakenzol</a> said that Mage is balanced against itself as a game line and not against other game lines. Then you quoted him, said that wasn't entirely true, and then went off on an irrelevant tangent about spirits and ghosts being trivialized by Mages. I pointed out your error, then you quoted me said I was wrong, and went off on another irrelevant tangent about the fact that since Mages best opponents are other Mages this reduces the number of available stories. This is me pointing out your error again, Mage is balanced against itself. This has nothing to do with what kinds of stories you're able to tell or the relative power levels of ephemeral entities inside the game line unless you believe that if the game was balanced against itself it would be able to tell any kind of story. Which would be a patently false assumption anyway because this is Mage and therefore you wouldn't be able to tell the story of Sue the mortal ghost hunter that likes finding cold spots no matter how the game was balanced. Well, you could, but you can do that with the game as is, but it raises the question of why you're bothering with Mage at that point in either case.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T07:13:24-07:00","id":1136585,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136567#post1136567\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The notion that horror requires strong mechanics to showcase itself is completely missing the point. ALL the game lines have their own forms of horror and how well the storyteller displays this has absolutely nothing to do with the mechanics of the game. It has everything to do with how the storyteller describes situations and circumstances in the game. In Mage, it's the storyteller's job to get the players to be disgusted with their own choices and thereby showing how mages are monsters. It isn't the job of the developers of the game to scare you, that's your job. It isn't the job of the developers to make you disgusted with your own choices, that's your job. Furthermore, attempting to place more stringent mechanics on the setting to enforce the horror of the setting actually detracts from said horror when the type of horror you're trying to present is that of how people hurt other people by being selfish! You don't get players to be horrified by what they make their characters do by placing stronger mechanics that detract from their capability to do horrific things. Nor do you get players to be horrified by what they make their characters do by introducing mechanics that &quot;present horror.&quot; Trying to enforce horror through mechanics simply is a losing battle. Mechanics are like facts whereas horror is emotional. If you're trying to get your players to be horrified (no matter where that horror is coming from), you're shooting yourself in the foot by trying to do so with mechanics instead of vivid descriptions, plot twists, and graphic details.<br /> <br /> If showcasing the consequences of horrific actions isn't your cup of tea, fine. But please don't suggest that there isn't horror within Mage or that it isn't actual horror. Normal people doing evil things is quite horrific as can be seen whenever you look long enough at history.<br /> <br /> As to the topic on hand, yes, I believe the op has it wrong. He has it wrong within the very title of his post. There is no line for Mage to be brought into. That implies that the developers of the game went rogue. Chronicles of Darkness is specifically designed to not have game lines that are balanced against each other. Suggesting that one game line needs to be strengthened or nerfed to bring it into line implies that you don't understand the intent of the developers and what the games are actually trying to do. Every game has its own themes and moods and any time anyone decides to play a crossover game doing so will explicitly detract from at least one of those themes or moods, which goes directly against game design.<br /> <br /> Now maybe you want to go against game design. Maybe you really do want to have that crossover game. Sometimes that is fun, and honestly, the point of these games is to have fun. So you decide to have a crossover game. Ok. Fine. Great even. Here's my point though: if you don't know how to change the abilities of one splat to make them weaker or stronger you probably haven't played the game long enough to appreciate it for its own merits. Now, it's perfectly possible that you just don't like a game for its own merits. However, by the time you've played a game long enough that you COULD appreciate it for its own merits (presuming you don't have a natural inclination against said game, which of course just begs the question of &quot;Why did you want to play this game again?&quot;), you should be familiar enough with its mechanics that you can easily see how strengthen or weaken the splat. And THAT isn't even considering how ALL these games use xp to purchase higher dots of abilities (i.e. just use a weaker mage, or a weaker werewolf, or a weaker vampire, etc).</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Well said.<br /> <br /> I think the topic would be less controversial if it, instead, asked for a homebrew method. Ultimately, OP may think the distinction is irrelevant (homebrew vs. a question about nerfing a splat in canon) - but many, including myself, balk at the notion of &quot;nerfing&quot; mages.<br /> <br /> Again, the topic reads as though mages might need to be nerfed in canon - making everyone that likes the current setup homebrew or option rule &quot;buffs&quot; into the game so they return to where they currently sit.<br /> <br /> They don't need to be nerfed. The people that don't like the current iteration will find better luck in making their own homebrew or trying another splat/game-type. Calling for canon nerfs for something that doesn't seem to be a huge deal is kind of selfish.<br /> <br /> I don't need mage to play like Constantine. If I want to play Constantine, I'll either play another splat or make a lesser mage from the supplements and merits.<br /> <br /> I don't get the idea of nerfing what most people like even though I essentially have the tools to get what I want already in the book by way of Proximi, Sleepwalkers, and other supernatural-merit templates.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T09:00:02-07:00","id":1136602,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136585#post1136585\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think the topic would be less controversial if it, instead, asked for a homebrew method. Ultimately, OP may think the distinction is irrelevant (homebrew vs. a question about nerfing a splat in canon) - but many, including myself, balk at the notion of &quot;nerfing&quot; mages.<br /> <br /> Again, the topic reads as though mages might need to be nerfed in canon - making everyone that likes the current setup homebrew or option rule &quot;buffs&quot; into the game so they return to where they currently sit.<br /> <br /> They don't need to be nerfed. The people that don't like the current iteration will find better luck in making their own homebrew or trying another splat/game-type. Calling for canon nerfs for something that doesn't seem to be a huge deal is kind of selfish.<br /> <br /> I don't need mage to play like Constantine. If I want to play Constantine, I'll either play another splat or make a lesser mage from the supplements and merits.<br /> <br /> I don't get the idea of nerfing what most people like even though I essentially have the tools to get what I want already in the book by way of Proximi, Sleepwalkers, and other supernatural-merit templates.</div> </div> </div> </div>As you said I'm pretty sure the distinction never occured for the op. I doubt anyone expects a thread like this to make an impact upon the games offical rules. I guess if a really really really lot of people complained it may get changed for 3e... but that is far off and if that's the case anyone who disagrees can just keep playing 2e.<br /> <br /> Anyhow developing homerules to achieve this was basically what the first few pages of the thread were about. Then the whole discussion became really defensive of mage and its brang of horror. We're just really moving in a circle by now. Some mages like the idea more that mages basically have no competition, others like it if other supernatural entities (whatever those may be) are more dangerous. Personally belong to the latter camp, because the sorcerors apprentice calling upon thing he cannot contain is alot more interesting a story to me than the sorcerors apprentice calling upon powerful spirits noticing it doesn't work out the way he wants to and just casually slapping away the minor god he summoned with a casual flick of his wrist. Sure there is a certain kind of horror in the latter szenario to, I just don't prefer it myself as a Storyteller.<br /> <br /> Now personally I'm comfortable enough with the rules system to simply make a few small changes that allow me to tell stories that way (changing a few spells a bit, making spirits better at clashes of wills) without having to compromise mages themes too much (or so I feel, anyhow). But not everyone is. I'm feeling kind of sorry for the op that the thread completly devolved into a discussion about horror in mage (and I know I haven't helped).<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T09:41:14-07:00","id":1136615,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136538#post1136538\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">No, see, then everyone would just need time magic and have to prepare their spells in advance like a DnD wizard using Hung Spell.</div> </div> </div> </div>They still need to cast those spells with Hung Spell in the first place. Biggest problem with mages in crossovers is their versality - if mage need to spend 2 days in advance to prepate his Hung Spells, he still can easily be raid in this time by other forces.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T10:32:53-07:00","id":1136628,"title":"","content":"It would be interesting to know how many people would continue to want to play a mage in a crossover game if they were nerfed like this. I continue that wanting to play anything else rather than X in a X game is selfish, wanting to play a mage in a Y game the person obv wants to play a mage to be versatile and dominating the chronicle might be part of that.<br /> <br /> If a ST says ok this game is open to any splat then its the other players fault for not picking mage, they cant moan about the power of a mage when they have chosen something weaker. Its like choosing ranger in 5e dnd and moaning about how rubbish they are compared to the druid.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T11:04:36-07:00","id":1136640,"title":"","content":"Perhaps the trick is to make sure your mage has abilities outside the purview of the other PCs. <br /> <br /> Like, as tempting as it may be, <i>don't</i> play a Thyrsus in a game with werewolves.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T11:15:05-07:00","id":1136644,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136628#post1136628\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would be interesting to know how many people would continue to want to play a mage in a crossover game if they were nerfed like this. I continue that wanting to play anything else rather than X in a X game is selfish, wanting to play a mage in a Y game the person obv wants to play a mage to be versatile and dominating the chronicle might be part of that.<br /> <br /> If a ST says ok this game is open to any splat then its the other players fault for not picking mage, they cant moan about the power of a mage when they have chosen something weaker. Its like choosing ranger in 5e dnd and moaning about how rubbish they are compared to the druid.</div> </div> </div> </div>Wanting to play Mage in a Crossover Games isn't wanting to play Y in an X game. It is fully in the spirit of the game's premise. You can, additionally, want to play a Mage for thematic or concept reasons other than dominating the Chronicle through sheer power. And none of the &quot;just don't play a Mage, then&quot; suggestions eliminate a Mage's versatility, merely reign in when or how easy it is to apply.<br /> <br /> And bringing up the obvious power imbalance isn't really helping your position, here.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T11:19:31-07:00","id":1136647,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136640#post1136640\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Perhaps the trick is to make sure your mage has abilities outside the purview of the other PCs.<br /> <br /> Like, as tempting as it may be, <i>don't</i> play a Thyrsus in a game with werewolves.</div> </div> </div> </div>Really its the same thing in single splat game. In vampire i wouldnt allow 2 players to make a ventrue invictus specialising in dominate. There are enough options in the games for each player in the group to have what they want and not step on other players toes.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Completed campaign: Scion 2nd Edition. Les L\u00e9gendes Currently playing: Being a dad for a 3 year old daughter and a 2 years old son and now a beautiful new baby.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11312-maitrecorbo","name":"Maitrecorbo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11312_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"719"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T11:27:15-07:00","id":1136653,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136628#post1136628\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It would be interesting to know how many people would continue to want to play a mage in a crossover game if they were nerfed like this.</div> </div> </div> </div>Plenty in my experience, i run mage-only games while heavily nerfing them and i get players to buy in. Thing is, one gotta be upfront about it before the game starts.<br /> <br /> Many people dont think when wanting to play a mage &quot;I wanna play a game about hubris and unlimited power that is delivered with the tropes of mages&quot;, but instead think &quot;i wanna play a witch who throw bad luck, fireballs or a modern druid, etc etc&quot; The power level of mage is not part of the premise that interest them.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136628#post1136628\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I continue that wanting to play anything else rather than X in a X game is selfish, wanting to play a mage in a Y game the person obv wants to play a mage to be versatile and dominating the chronicle might be part of that.</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say it takes a very problematic player to approach a croosver game with the idea of dominating the chronicle. Unless everyone is 100% on board on being the player flunkies.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-02-2017, 02:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T12:51:55-07:00","id":1136687,"title":"","content":"Started separate topic for my <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1136684-2e-hack-no-instant-casting-%E2%80%93-for-crossover-games\"><b>No Instant Casting</b></a> Hack, to hammer details and consequence for the 2E Mage \u2013 for making mages more bearable for crossover games with other monsters. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T15:19:37-07:00","id":1136732,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136556#post1136556\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">There's a reason why there's so many people make claims that mages aren't monsters with completely straight faces. This is not something that can be brushed off by saying that people &quot;don't get it.&quot;<br /> <br /> Invariably, every reader's interpretation of a material won't always match up to the writers' intentions. If its only a handful, then that's only inevitable. If it starts to reach a notable portion of the reader base? Then it becomes less about the people and becomes an issue with the presentation. Its ironically hubris to assume the problem here is always on the readers when there are multiple factors at play here. </span></div> </div> </div> </div>I suppose my assertion is a little simplistic, and upon reading this response it occured to me that I have been meaning two seperate things in one go. Now, that's not to imply I haven't heavily been relying on the first of the two meanings, as over a decade of dealing with this particular fanbase has made it abundantly clear to me that People Not Getting It is a deep and afflicting malady, and I will actually stand by it against your assertion that this can't be brushed off with saying &quot;people don't get it&quot; because too much of my experience to the contrary makes me skeptical. I could be wrong though, so now it's time to examine that second thing I've been saying.<br /> <br /> As mentioned, over the years of dealing with this fanbase, I've come across this iteration of problem quite a few times, and what occured to me on reading the response above is that the question is not &quot;What is the horror of this game?&quot;-at least, not directly-but more &quot;How is this horrifying?&quot;, and that has interesting implications. Like, even when questions are phrased as the former, it's often with an intonation with the latter, a searching for something they missed because what they found evokes a very strong &quot;What, that's it?&quot;<br /> <br /> Because, yes, it would be crass to assume everyone who asks these sorts of questions simply can't see it, this leads to the natural conclusion that they have seen the branches of Mages horror, recognized them for what they were...but didn't actually comprehend <i>how</i> that was supposed to be horrifying. And, at it's heart, that's the most dehumanizing and monstrous part of Mage, that &quot;so what&quot; reaction. It belies a disregard for other people who have to suffer through your nimbus and the consequences of your spells, successful or not, it belies a disregard for the self-mutilation and warping that will invariably happen starting with Awakening and getting worse down the line, it belies a disregard for the world they exist in as they casually wreck any number of things in flow, all in favor of the obsession with knowing things just to know and exercising power for their own benefit, which often includes mere convenience or amusement. And all of this gets magnified because it happens within a &quot;false space&quot;, it's not like any of this has reprecusions on the &quot;true world&quot;, as though we are not responsible for the entertainment we create or ingest without even some small consideration.<br /> <br /> And putting aside the spiritual-to-physical ways that Mages are monsters, that's the main way of how Mages are Monsters. It's people power exercising power without even the slightest regard for how that power spills out, the 1% approach to and lack of care for power, all because they don't even see how any of the bad stuff is actually bad stuff-not because they don't see it, but because they don't actually care about their consequences.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T15:27:59-07:00","id":1136736,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136732#post1136732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Because, yes, it would be crass to assume everyone who asks these sorts of questions simply can't see it, this leads to the natural conclusion that they have seen the branches of Mages horror, recognized them for what they were...but didn't actually comprehend <i>how</i> that was supposed to be horrifying. And, at it's heart, that's the most dehumanizing and monstrous part of Mage, that &quot;so what&quot; reaction. It belies a disregard for other people who have to suffer through your nimbus and the consequences of your spells, successful or not, it belies a disregard for the self-mutilation and warping that will invariably happen starting with Awakening and getting worse down the line, it belies a disregard for the world they exist in as they casually wreck any number of things in flow, all in favor of the obsession with knowing things just to know and exercising power for their own benefit, which often includes mere convenience or amusement. And all of this gets magnified because it happens within a &quot;false space&quot;, it's not like any of this has reprecusions on the &quot;true world&quot;, as though we are not responsible for the entertainment we create or ingest without even some small consideration.<br /> <br /> And putting aside the spiritual-to-physical ways that Mages are monsters, that's the main way of how Mages are Monsters. It's people power exercising power without even the slightest regard for how that power spills out, the 1% approach to and lack of care for power, all because they don't even see how any of the bad stuff is actually bad stuff-not because they don't see it, but because they don't actually care about their consequences.</div> </div> </div> </div>In vampire, werewolf, etc you play as a monster. In mage you are the monster.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Completed campaign: Scion 2nd Edition. Les L\u00e9gendes Currently playing: Being a dad for a 3 year old daughter and a 2 years old son and now a beautiful new baby.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11312-maitrecorbo","name":"Maitrecorbo","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11312_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"719"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T16:16:19-07:00","id":1136744,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136732#post1136732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Some interesting point on mage.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bAs <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2102_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2102\" data-vbnamecard=\"2102\">MCN</a> said each player table playstyle and personalities are also critical. For example i know my group wont take on the hubris and abuse of power themes of awakening. Mostly because we think WtA handles them better for us but also because:<br /> <br /> For one the Share your horror stories thread and the comparison with hellblazer have pointed out that for mage to work player have to play recklessly, abuse their power or the DM have to twist the situation for it to have any effect. <br /> <br /> The comparison with Hellblazer is apt because for Hellblazer to work John Constantine needs to be the massive shortsighted shithead that he is. Even a history covers that in which he meets his dead twin brother whom John himself killed in the womb (yes, Hellblazer comics get weird) and show him an alternative timeline in which the brother was the one to be born and he was basically a force for good and leading a happy live with the same powers. <br /> <br /> Same on my group who are very moral (in our weird morality) and tend no to play neither shortsighted people nor abusers of power. Same with some elements of the setting like the Seers, &quot;they are symphatethic and compelling because people in the real world also sell out&quot; the game and its fanbase say, however for me and my group that fact is irrelevant because the secretary of a tobacco company (as an example of the selling out) is not compelling just because she is real. That fact plus the fact that with that much power (as all mages are heavy hitters) and work to make the world a shittier place and isnt a by product but the end of their actions makes them pose a danger and should be shoot on sight by out morality means that we find them neither compelling nor symphatethic bad.<br /> <br /> I also have deal a lot with this fanbase and something i always find in my experience is fans tunnel vision of people &quot;not getting it&quot;. While forgetting the option that someone might get what the game is going for and just think is not well done and/or just dont subscribe to its theme but still wants to play mage. <br /> <br /> And before anyone post the old &quot;why dont use this other system/splat&quot; those fans also forget that the history and success of WW/OPP is predicated a lot in people ignoring/disregarding the authors intended themes for their games. Maybe those people like lots of elements of the mechanics/setting of mage. Other splats arent what they are looking for. Supernatural merits are too weak or hundreds of other reason that make just nerfing mages for their games a better option.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T20:13:20-07:00","id":1136803,"title":"","content":"You're not playing Mage if you just toss out its core theme. You're just borrowing its mechanics at that point. And, honestly, if that is what you want then you should probably familiarize yourself with those mechanics since they are, after all, what you want. And if that is what you want, why bother with asking how to alter them? Seriously, it's not that hard. I did it for years before appreciating Mage's setting and themes.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T20:22:17-07:00","id":1136805,"title":"","content":"So, were there other recommendations for adjustments or explainations how they aren't actually necessary? Or was it all off-topic, thread-crapping, or &quot;you're playing wrong (read: not my way)&quot;?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Malkydel: &quot;And the Machine dictated; let there be adequate illumination.&quot;<br /> Yossarian: &quot;And lo, it was optimal.&quot;","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7212-vent0","name":"Vent0","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7212_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"5184"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T21:02:29-07:00","id":1136814,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Vent0</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136805#post1136805\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, were there other recommendations for adjustments or explainations how they aren't actually necessary? Or was it all off-topic, thread-crapping, or &quot;you're playing wrong (read: not my way)&quot;?</div> </div> </div> </div>Personally i gave the OP my recomendation on how to approach the issue on page 3 and still waiting for an answer on the OP but yes, a lot of offtopic and thread-crapping by people rushing to the aid of mage<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136803#post1136803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're not playing Mage if you just toss out its core theme. You're just borrowing its mechanics at that point. And, honestly, if that is what you want then you should probably familiarize yourself with those mechanics since they are, after all, what you want. And if that is what you want, why bother with asking how to alter them? Seriously, it's not that hard. I did it for years before appreciating Mage's setting and themes.</div> </div> </div> </div>People have been doing that since Vampire the Masquerade with most of the gamelines. Keeping the setting/some of all the mechanics but ignoring/replacing the themes. Also not the OP and not everyone is you, whats so bad in helping a person who ask for help?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-03-2017, 12:32 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T21:02:49-07:00","id":1136815,"title":"","content":"\u2026Anyway.<br /> <br /> Some notional tweaks of no particular endorsement level or intended synergy:<br /> <br /> \u2022 Replace the three starting rotes with a free two-dot Grimoire; Increase the Experience cost of Rotes and/or require breakthroughs similar to those that accompany raising Gnosis on a smaller scale before a character can internalize knowledge of a Rote.<br /> \u2022 Make accessing more than one die from a given Yantra cost Mana or add a Paradox die.<br /> \u2022 Change the base casting pool to just Arcanum unless it uses your Dedicated Tool or takes place in your Sanctum.<br /> \u2022 Add your Gnosis to an unwilling target's Withstand if you don't use an appropriate Sympathy Yantra and/or have a sympathetic connection with them.<br /> \u2022 Require Willpower expenditure to prevent unsafe relinquishment of one of your spells at the end of a chapter.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T21:30:54-07:00","id":1136819,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Threadcrapping is a rules violation, stop doing it. Darksider.</span>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11-darksider","user":"Darksider","date":". 09-03-2017, 01:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-02T21:38:25-07:00","id":1136824,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136819#post1136819\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">OP isnt even replying, ya'll been baited.</div> </div> </div> </div>Inane &quot;baiting&quot; has never been a part of the forum culture here. Kindly do not attempt to make it so.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T05:12:39-07:00","id":1136894,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136803#post1136803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're not playing Mage if you just toss out its core theme. You're just borrowing its mechanics at that point. And, honestly, if that is what you want then you should probably familiarize yourself with those mechanics since they are, after all, what you want. And if that is what you want, why bother with asking how to alter them? Seriously, it's not that hard. I did it for years before appreciating Mage's setting and themes.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, two things. First of all.... the core themes of Mage are The World is a Lie and Addicted to Mysteries. Power and hubris aren't actually listed as themes of mage, but it comes up enough that its a third theme, lets say. That still leaves two thirds of the game that you can focus on if you toss out the focus on hubris. That's a lot of room to play in. Cries of someone not playing the same as you not playing Mage just means you're subscribing to a One True Way to game, and that's just.... wrong.<br /> <br /> Secondly, from reading LokiRaven's post, they actually are playing with the theme of hubris. Or, rather, they're playing high-Wisdom mages who understand that magic can be harmful and go out of their way to understand their limits. And that's fine. I personally default to high Integrity-equivalent types for most of my characters, simply because that's the type of person I am and, unless I am deliberately making a different style of character for the sake of pushing boundries, its the type of character I have the most fun playing. Just because I'm not usually engaging in the fall of Integrity-equivalents and play moral monsters doesn't mean I'm &quot;borrowing mechanics.&quot; I'm not playing superheroes with fangs/fur/fae/etc (though, I will say that is a valid playstyle as well if people want to do it.)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T05:34:14-07:00","id":1136901,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136732#post1136732\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*snip*</div> </div> </div> </div> The problem I have with assertion is that it assumes monstrocity and horror are somehow a standard everyone agrees to. Now, I've made cases in the past where the monstrocity and horror of mage is tied to their Nimbus, their Obsessions, with paradox/dissonace as a side thing. But here's the thing. These arguments are countered by people pointing out that you can take Obsessions like raising your Wisdom (and there are entire valid schools of thought that Wisdom is a moral code, not just magic control) or other non-environment affecting studies. And, even then, Obsessions are entirely carrot - and a carrot that can be easily ignored. A number of people consider the ease that you can avoid affecting Sleepers with your Nimbus to make it negligable. And I think that paradox is laughable; the only time that I've seen it become relevant is in combat, and I don't run combat heavy games. You can't say that mages will abuse humans with their magic, because that's not how human psychology works, and Awakened psychology is close enough to humanity's that its still relevant.<br /> <br /> So, from a certain point of view, mages are only monsters if they CHOOSE to be. Its not that mages are a monster because they are mages, its because someone is being a bad person. Individuals can be horrific. But it doesn't mean an entire race is horrific, or monstrous. Which, following that line of thought, mean mages aren't really monsters as a whole.<br /> <br /> So, its not that people are really going &quot;so what?&quot; in that what these careless mages isn't bad. Its that they see the problems, and deciding that they are entirely managable in a virtuous manner. Which, again from a certain point of view, is possible. There's no disregard for the people suffering through the nimbus, its that the players feel that no one is being affected with the nimbus in the first place! Its like taking Promethean, and making it so that Disquiet, Wastelands and Torment are all trivially easy to ignore. Mage is insanely forgiving towards that kind of thing. That's not something that I think is really something that should be happening.<br /> <br /> And, to be complete, there's likely a lot of people carrying impressions of 1e forwards, and 1e did not include anything like an obsession or a magical-radiation Nimbus. There was nothing other than mages being able to cast spells, and an assumption they'll be jerks with said magic - a very poor assumption to make. So, starting with this, moving into 2e where the &quot;monstrous&quot; part of Mages appears to be entirely managable, is it any surprise that people claim mages aren't monsters or horrific?<br /> <br /> And earlier, Arcane? You said that the horror of mages is humanity writ large. I'm going to flat out and say that, for many people, humanity writ large does not a monster make. Is it terrible? Sure. But familiarity breeds contempt, and someone who's basically an evil wizard version of Wayne LaPierre isn't scary. Terrible, sure. But not something that necessarily invokes shock and fear. Disguist, absolutely, but a lot of people equate horror with shock and fear more than disgust, and casual English is not known for its strict adherance to exact definitions.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Now, my personal belief is that Mage does have a lot of decent ideas, but a large chunk of the mechanics need to be sharpened and streamlined. Mage mechanics are all over the place still. Some are too good, some are just terrible. Paradox is still laughable outside combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2102,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","user":"MCN","date":". 09-03-2017, 08:48 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T06:13:10-07:00","id":1136907,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136901#post1136901\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Paradox is still laughable outside combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>And if you want to make it laughable inside of combat for at least your own PC, you simply relegate magic to shielding and counterspelling while using non-magical means for offense.<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T06:20:34-07:00","id":1136911,"title":"","content":"If you don't think cults are scary I don't know what to tell you.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T06:43:56-07:00","id":1136920,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136901#post1136901\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now, my personal belief is that Mage does have a lot of decent ideas, but a large chunk of the mechanics need to be sharpened and streamlined. Mage mechanics are all over the place still. Some are too good, some are just terrible. Paradox is still laughable outside combat.</div> </div> </div> </div>What mechanics in particular? Have you actually played 2e and tried the new paradox or are you just making up your mind about it without actually using it?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can't say that mages will abuse humans with their magic, because that's not how human psychology works, and Awakened psychology is close enough to humanity's that its still relevant. </div> </div> </div>That old saying: &quot;Absolute power corrupts absolutely&quot; is a thing. Im not ashamed to say if given the power of a mage, i would abuse the hell out of it maybe to the detriment of people around me, esp ones that might have it coming.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1534,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","user":"totalgit","date":". 09-03-2017, 09:51 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1534-totalgit","name":"totalgit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1534_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"742"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T07:11:43-07:00","id":1136923,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136920#post1136920\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That old saying: &quot;Absolute power corrupts absolutely&quot; is a thing. Im not ashamed to say if given the power of a mage, i would abuse the hell out of it maybe to the detriment of people around me, esp ones that might have it coming.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is a thing sure but isnt absolute and even less so when we are talking about a game. Assuming that everyone who plays mage will abuse their power or be recklessly is a mistake. Is like saying &quot;everyone who plays vampire will spiral down their PCs humanity to 1&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136911#post1136911\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you don't think cults are scary I don't know what to tell you.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a little offtopic (but this thread has been derailed a long time ago anyway) but is there any resource to read on Cults? I mean the books throws them as something that mages do but in my experience few players do because on one there isnt much info on how they function or form beyond movies (eyes wide shut) or unfortunate RL events like &quot;heavens gate incident&quot; and not many wanna explore that kind of things.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-03-2017, 10:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T07:45:33-07:00","id":1136930,"title":"","content":"I think the implication was more around the fact that all Orders are cults. There used to be some info on Antagonists , including brainwashing and deprogramming rules, but is the earliest first ed we are talking about.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":195,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","user":"Thorbes","date":". 09-03-2017, 10:47 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T07:45:51-07:00","id":1136931,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>totalgit</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136920#post1136920\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What mechanics in particular? Have you actually played 2e and tried the new paradox or are you just making up your mind about it without actually using it?</div> </div> </div> </div> First, let me say that accusing someone of not playing the game is in -very- bad taste. Yes, I have played the game, and found it lacking. Paradox is designed to be an issue inside of a face paced environment, which is generally goign to be combat. There are the occasional exception, but those are very rare, in my experience. Given that I don't like combat heavy games, either to play in or run, that makes paradox kind of iffy.<br /> <br /> As for what I have issues with? Paradox is a joke. Obsession needs a stick. Nimbus is a pain in the butt to track in combat, meaningless out of combat. Scrutiny is too time consuming and needs to be group oriented isntead of individual. Some order merits need revamping. Too much number crunching. Active spell limits are too forgiving. I don't like having mages and the abyss be the only real threat to PC mage cabal. I don't like how so much of the game seems to be balanced around combat. And that's just off the top of my head -there are others.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> That old saying: &quot;Absolute power corrupts absolutely&quot; is a thing. </div> </div> </div> And has been proven grossly false by the scientific community. It is, to put it bluntly, a Lie. Some people might have the inclination towards corruption, but others will not. Those latter who Awaken will not become corrupt.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, the type of corruption the saying is refering to (rulership) has <i>nothing </i>to do with mage hubris. Mage hubris is a result of Reach, risking 'dox and carelessness with magic, with a touch of &quot;mind control and murder bad,&quot; which, given the mage toolset, is generally easy to avoid. You can be a corrupt despot, have Enlightened Wisdom and not fall into hubris. <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">If you don't think cults are scary I don't know what to tell you.</div> </div> </div> </div> Some are, some aren't. Like all organizations, you have to take each one individually and see if its scary/bad or not. Generalization is generally (ha!) bad.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T10:02:47-07:00","id":1136954,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136931#post1136931\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> First, let me say that accusing someone of not playing the game is in -very- bad taste. Yes, I have played the game, and found it lacking. Paradox is designed to be an issue inside of a face paced environment, which is generally goign to be combat. There are the occasional exception, but those are very rare, in my experience. Given that I don't like combat heavy games, either to play in or run, that makes paradox kind of iffy.<br /> <br /> As for what I have issues with? Paradox is a joke. Obsession needs a stick. Nimbus is a pain in the butt to track in combat, meaningless out of combat. Scrutiny is too time consuming and needs to be group oriented isntead of individual. Some order merits need revamping. Too much number crunching. Active spell limits are too forgiving. I don't like having mages and the abyss be the only real threat to PC mage cabal. I don't like how so much of the game seems to be balanced around combat. And that's just off the top of my head -there are others.<br /> <br /> And has been proven grossly false by the scientific community. It is, to put it bluntly, a Lie. Some people might have the inclination towards corruption, but others will not. Those latter who Awaken will not become corrupt.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, the type of corruption the saying is refering to (rulership) has <i>nothing </i>to do with mage hubris. Mage hubris is a result of Reach, risking 'dox and carelessness with magic, with a touch of &quot;mind control and murder bad,&quot; which, given the mage toolset, is generally easy to avoid. You can be a corrupt despot, have Enlightened Wisdom and not fall into hubris.<br /> <br /> Some are, some aren't. Like all organizations, you have to take each one individually and see if its scary/bad or not. Generalization is generally (ha!) bad.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm not understanding how Paradox is a joke. From my reading, Paradox is as big an issue as the ST is willing to make it. Meaning the ST can add more modifiers or generate circumstances that make Paradox a bigger issue.<br /> <br /> I don't understand why &quot;obsession needs a stick.&quot; Does it really need anything? It works as a lead or direction tool. Nimbus can still transmit conditions and communicate influence outside of combat. Don't get why scrutiny is &quot;too time consuming&quot; or &quot;needs to be group oriented,&quot; by that I mean, why does it &quot;have&quot; to be that way - when it has already been established and has reasons for working the way it currently does?<br /> <br /> Too much number crunching seems highly subjective. Don't crunch as many numbers? I think we're still better off than 1e and where Exalted was (Pretty sure current Exalted resolved those issues, but I haven't played it to figure out if it has less crunching than Mage currently has.) What makes you think there are too many numbers to crunch in the current system? I found it to be vastly reduced from the previous iteration, let alone the chaos of Classic WoD. I suppose it has less crunching than D&amp;D's &quot;choose a spell, roll the dice, do the damage&quot; formula - but even then those games can be pretty complex if the DM sets up the challenges appropriately.<br /> <br /> Active spell limits are too forgiving? These all seem like personal issues that don't necessarily equate to a less horrifying game. You might personally feel that way or want a grittier game, but it seems like you're free to make it grittier based on your own understanding of the rules and what you want out of a game.<br /> <br /> Why are mages and the abyss the only real threats to PCs? Is your ST not utilizing other splats or creatures? Your ST is literally able to design any bizarre or enigmatic nightmare and pump them full of abilities and countermeasures. Your ST could even throw them within a magical deadzone and have them exist as supernaturals outside of magic. Other splats can come at you in complex waves of political intrigue and resource management. Again, it seems like you aren't challenging people as an ST, or you aren't being challenged enough by your ST.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The whole Power Corrupts thing is sort of a misnomer. Corruption either needs to be defined within the context of the law, and even then some people can be said to be &quot;corrupt&quot; as a consequence of exceptions or overriding the current conception. It could literally mean anything from money misappropriation to willfully not fulfilling campaign promises to thirst for war to commanding the execution of others, etc. Who gets to use the label of corrupt? Often times it is some set of observers, not you. Meaning you could understand your actions as corrupt, but you could also understand them as permissible. At the end of the day this depends on how you personally feel about stuff and how your ethics work, that doesn't make you any less or more corrupt in the eyes of observers. Your ST can put pressure here on your actions based on the perceptions of NPCs, politics, and established Orders or groups.<br /> <br /> Your ethical behavior is not necessarily a resolved issue because it can be influenced by so many things within the context of CofD. There is tons about &quot;doing the right thing in spite of the consequences&quot; when talking about virtuousness.<br /> <br /> The scientific community has shown that people are more likely to take advantage of things when they aren't accountable. Whether or not this translates into them being &quot;good or bad&quot; is highly subjective, though. People can also be influenced by their environments to commit decisions they otherwise wouldn't, as is the case with the Milgram experiment (or the culture of mage society the ST has set out for the PCs.) In CofD, people can be influenced by supernatural circumstances or beings to commit actions they otherwise wouldn't. Those acts don't translate into nothing. They could very well land within the context of breaking points.<br /> <br /> Crime statistics show most people that steal are not necessarily rational thinkers, but opportunists who see something and take it in the moment: a rush, the thrill, intrigue, what-if, etc. Rational-thought thefts are a thing, but they aren't the majority of all theft related crimes. The same can be said for many violent crimes or manslaughter cases of murder (which make up the majority of murder.)<br /> <br /> People unintentionally hurt each other or kill each other often. This is often in spite with the knowledge that, typically, these people are perfectly &quot;well-adjusted&quot; or non-murdering types.<br /> <br /> People don't always rationally think through the processes and instead are many times accidental contributors. These are examples against rational choice theories, or the belief people are bad or making poor decisions intentionally. This says to me that &quot;corruption&quot; is liable to surface without intent. It could even surface directly within the context of NOT trying to be corrupt.<br /> <br /> What does this translate into gamewise? An ST staying on top of your decisions and making it hard to make the right choice. Or, that the right choice has unintended consequences.<br /> <br /> It seems like you're looking at the game from a purely mechanical standpoint, but I don't think that does it justice because the ST can lend a lot to make it more forgiving or less. Mage, in and of itself, probably works fine for people that aren't calculating all the ways to ignore penalties and reduce the mechanics to what you've outlined as your gripe with Hubris degeneration.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":11166,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","user":"kryptonitekrusher","date":". 09-03-2017, 01:06 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T10:44:39-07:00","id":1136963,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136814#post1136814\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">People have been doing that since Vampire the Masquerade with most of the gamelines. Keeping the setting/some of all the mechanics but ignoring/replacing the themes. Also not the OP and not everyone is you, whats so bad in helping a person who ask for help?</div> </div> </div> </div>The op posited that Mage might need to be brought into line. That presumes that there is a &quot;line&quot; to be brought into, despite the fact that the developers designed ALL the games (minus Beast, just because) to be balanced against themselves. Now, the op hasn't responded at all, but it's quite irritating to see people try to come to the defense of &quot;Anyone can play the game however they want, don't push your way of playing&quot; when the way the op talks about the topic implies that he doesn't understand the game design. The idea that the op probably doesn't understand the design of Mage is strengthened by the fact that he mentions that potency is something he's heard of. A basic principle, one of the spell factors no less, of spell casting in Mage is just something he's &quot;heard of.&quot; That doesn't sound like someone that has a basic (let alone solid) grasp of the mechanics (let alone the themes) of Mage. So yeah, it's NOT that hard to jump to the conclusion that the op doesn't &quot;get&quot; the game.<br /> <br /> Furthermore, for those of us that understand it AND appreciate it (I say the word and because you and MCN say that you understand the design but purposefully ignore said design for your own reasons) we want to bring people to our position. Explaining how there indeed is horror in the game (horror being one of the draws to the Chronicles of Darkness), how each game is balanced around non-crossover play, how mages are designed to be powerful to engage both the mysteries and the horror; doing all of this gives us the opportunity to help others understand the game. At any point anyone can say that they don't want to play the way we play, the way we think the game was designed for. But if someone doesn't understand the design and he's told to play however he wants, then it's much more difficult to help that person appreciate a game for its own merits. We want to share the experience of the game, but that is partially undercut when people criticize the developers for not placing horror in the game or for doing a terrible job at it, or saying that it's perfectly fine to have a crossover game despite it being against nearly all game designs (beast excluded).<br /> <br /> Given the fact that there are those of us that believe horror is in the game, that it's a good thing to keep the game lines separate, that prefer the &quot;sticks&quot; of horror and hubris and obsession to be role play based instead of mechanically based, it's not really surprising that you find us getting offended (pretty minor offense for me, this is a game after all) when people argue for &quot;play however you want and don't push for your understanding of the game.&quot; Regardless of how well or poorly the developers made the game to fit their vision of it, we enjoy the game and want others to enjoy it for its own merits as well. This isn't necessarily easy, especially for Mage. Mage is complicated and weird. But to us, both playing the game and helping others to understand it are worth every hour.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T11:07:51-07:00","id":1136968,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136901#post1136901\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">And, even then, Obsessions are entirely carrot - and a carrot that can be easily ignored.</div> </div> </div> </div>Obsessions are Aspirations and thus are accordingly difficult to talk a mage out of fulfilling. If you are ignoring Aspirations and having your character ignore them without that being a meaningful story element then elements of the game falling flat from that neglect is not the fault of the game itself.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> You can't say that mages will abuse humans with their magic, because that's not how human psychology works, and Awakened psychology is close enough to humanity's that its still relevant. </div> </div> </div>Again: Acts of Hubris give your character Conditions that mess with their Obsessions in ways that stick and risk further degeneration, these being indiscriminate use of magic or the excessive assertion of the self above others. Magic itself serves as an extra Vice through its exceptional success mechanics in such a way that characters who spend a lot of Willpower have reason to cast often. Obsessions are mechanically identical to the basic game mechanic of &quot;things which your character wants to do or which the world pushes your character towards accomplishing&quot; except with added magic feedback juice. All of these things are aspects of the game's presentation of Awakened psychology regardless of whether you opt to ignore them or not.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, from a certain point of view, mages are only monsters if they CHOOSE to be. </div> </div> </div>Yes. That choice is called the Awakening. It's a fairly long process with plenty of opportunity to turn back or get sidetracked. It's not an instantaneous flick of the Cosmic Enlightenment Switch to become Awakened.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Its not that mages are a monster because they are mages, its because someone is being a bad person. </div> </div> </div>I realize that conflating definitions of monstrosity is <i>de rigueur</i> for this class of conversation, but no, mages are monsters because a mage is a type of monster. They attract the supernatural and readily slide into egomania if they are not instead outcasts and liars by necessity, which are not generally elements of the ground state of human nature in either sense even without the ability to warp reality around themselves.<br /> <br /> &quot;The characters aren't scary if the Storyteller makes no push toward the themes of the game - the themes that the world your characters inhabit is disquietingly less real than they used to think and that your characters are driven toward that which begs to be explored and understood regardless of the danger or morality of that pursuit&quot; is not an indictment of the game in any stronger sense than lack of explicit hard moves on the players' allies, resources, and loved ones being built into the basic nature of a starting character with a functioning division from their Shadow Name \u2014 &quot;The game doesn't work as intended if the Storyteller is asleep at the wheel and/or the players aren't playing characters host to all-important obsessions with things that melt normal people's self-image to stay around&quot; is narrower than &quot;The game doesn't work as intended.&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mage 2e Core, &quot;An Introduction to Storytelling Games,&quot; p14</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The ideal group size for Mage is around two to five players, taking on one main character each. You\u2019ll make decisions for your character \u2014 what Mystery she\u2019s obsessed by, how and when she uses magic, how she reacts to betrayal, love, or anger.<br /> <br /> One player, <b>the Storyteller, is responsible for</b> portraying characters who don\u2019t belong to specific players, and <b>presenting fictional situations that challenge the other players\u2019 characters.</b> Think of these as the supporting cast \u2014 both ongoing characters who help or oppose the core cast, and one-off antagonists who turn up to cause unique kinds of trouble.<br /> <br /> As for challenging the player characters, <b>it\u2019s the job of the Storyteller to come up with scenes where the players have to make decisions fraught with conflict and danger.</b> The Storyteller narrates a situation, then the other players say how their characters respond. The most important question a Storyteller can ask is, \u201cWhat do you do now?\u201d<br /> <br /> [\u2026]<br /> <br /> The Storyteller is responsible for\u2026<br /> \u2026bringing the setting to life through description.<br /> \u2026deciding where scenes start and what\u2019s going on.<br /> \u2026portraying characters who don\u2019t belong to other players.<br /> \u2026involving each player and her character in the ongoing story.<br /> <b>\u2026putting players\u2019 characters in tough spots, encouraging interesting decisions. </b><br /> <b>\u2026facilitating the actions players\u2019 characters take, while making sure there are always complications. </b><br /> \u2026making sure that poor dice rolls affect but don\u2019t stop the story.<br /> <br /> The players are responsible for\u2026<br /> \u2026creating their own individual characters as members of the cast.<br /> \u2026deciding what actions their characters take.<br /> <b>\u2026making decisions that create drama and help keep the story moving.<br /> \u2026highlighting their characters\u2019 strengths and weaknesses.<br /> \u2026confronting the problems the Storyteller introduces. </b><br /> \u2026developing their characters\u2019 personalities and abilities over time, telling personal stories within the overall story of the game.<br /> <br /> Everyone is responsible for\u2026<br /> \u2026giving other players chances to highlight their characters\u2019 abilities and personal stories, whether that\u2019s by showing them at their strongest or weakest.<br /> \u2026making suggestions about the story and action, while keeping in mind the authority of players over their characters and <b>the responsibility of the Storyteller to occasionally make trouble.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":111,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","user":"Satchel","date":".  Salient quotation. 09-03-2017, 03:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T11:28:17-07:00","id":1136976,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136954#post1136954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Why are mages and the abyss the only real threats to PCs? Is your ST not utilizing other splats or creatures? Your ST is literally able to design any bizarre or enigmatic nightmare and pump them full of abilities and countermeasures. Your ST could even throw them within a magical deadzone and have them exist as supernaturals outside of magic. Other splats can come at you in complex waves of political intrigue and resource management. Again, it seems like you aren't challenging people as an ST, or you aren't being challenged enough by your ST.</div> </div> </div> </div>From the horses mouth<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Dave Brookshaw</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136120#post1136120\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">As to the general thrust of the thread - can't help you with crossovers; if you have werewolf pcs hunting storyteller character mages for god's sake dumb the mages down, same as if you're trying to run Hunter. Mummies can hold their own for a bit, Demons can until the mages realise their powers rip Demon's Cover to bits.<br /> <br /> If it's &quot;relative to the rest of the Chronicles of Darkness,&quot; and you're worried about mages being too powerful (whatever that means) for the monsters and mysteries, remember - the <b><u>Awakened deal with things that would eat protagonists of other gamelines for breakfast. High-Ranking Goetia, Supernal and Abyssal entities, bizarre Lower Depths things, dimensional tears.</u></b> You can make an element of setting weirdness much more dangerous in a Mage game, as the Awakened can cope with it (or not, and die to it).<br /> <br /> <b><u>And their principle opponent is one another.</u></b></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136954#post1136954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What does this translate into gamewise? An ST staying on top of your decisions and making it hard to make the right choice. Or, that the right choice has unintended consequences.<br /> <br /> It seems like you're looking at the game from a purely mechanical standpoint, but I don't think that does it justice because the ST can lend a lot to make it more forgiving or less. Mage, in and of itself, probably works fine for people that aren't calculating all the ways to ignore penalties and reduce the mechanics to what you've outlined as your gripe with Hubris degeneration.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2102_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2102\" data-vbnamecard=\"2102\">MCN</a> and me were saying, the game depends A LOT more on the group and DM in question to function. If i dont wanna play monkey paw to the actions of the PCs and they play it smart/moral then it would fall flat. Not saying is a big design flaw, when you get a group that fully embraces that go for it but it shouldnt be a surprise that there is also people who dont subscribe to that.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136963#post1136963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> but it's quite irritating to see people try to come to the defense of &quot;Anyone can play the game however they want, don't push your way of playing&quot; when the way the op talks about the topic implies that he doesn't understand the game design.</div> </div> </div> </div>As irritating as people coming to the &quot;defense&quot; of mage posting endlessly &quot;you dont get it, if you just could get it. You would see there is 100% perfect as it is&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136963#post1136963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"> Furthermore, for those of us that understand it AND appreciate it (I say the word and because you and MCN say that you understand the design but purposefully ignore said design for your own reasons) we want to bring people to our position.</div> </div> </div> </div>And i want to bring people to mine, so tough luck thats the nature of debate. We stuck with each other. And if you bothered to read i am not rejecting the fact that people like mage. I challenging the binary notions of &quot;WoD games are play as intended by everyone&quot; and &quot;There 2 kind of people, people who dont get mage and people who get mage and thus love it&quot;. Not even challenging that there is horror in mage but instead pointing out that it will depend on the group and DM dynamic for it to have an effect.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136963#post1136963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Explaining how there indeed is horror in the game (horror being one of the draws to the Chronicles of Darkness), how each game is balanced around non-crossover play, how mages are designed to be powerful to engage both the mysteries and the horror; doing all of this gives us the opportunity to help others understand the game. At any point anyone can say that they don't want to play the way we play, the way we think the game was designed for. But if someone doesn't understand the design and he's told to play however he wants, then it's much more difficult to help that person appreciate a game for its own merits. We want to share the experience of the game, but that is partially undercut when people criticize the developers for not placing horror in the game or for doing a terrible job at it, or saying that it's perfectly fine to have a crossover game despite it being against nearly all game designs (beast excluded).</div> </div> </div> </div>So let me get this clear? You want to shove your opinion on other people but dont want others opinions to oppose yours? Wow thats a pretty shitty argument. I dont have to help you in any way or form to make your argument for you, if your arguments requires other people to not challenge them then you lack the ability to properly defend it and shouldnt complain.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136963#post1136963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given the fact that there are those of us that believe horror is in the game, that it's a good thing to keep the game lines separate, that prefer the &quot;sticks&quot; of horror and hubris and obsession to be role play based instead of mechanically based, it's not really surprising that you find us getting offended (pretty minor offense for me, this is a game after all) when people argue for &quot;play however you want and don't push for your understanding of the game.&quot; Regardless of how well or poorly the developers made the game to fit their vision of it, we enjoy the game and want others to enjoy it for its own merits as well. This isn't necessarily easy, especially for Mage. Mage is complicated and weird. But to us, both playing the game and helping others to understand it are worth every hour.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again i dont care about how you feel or other feels on that matter when the mere fact that their opinions get challenged upset them. Were to be discussing social or political issues that people in the real world die or suffer from, maybe that argument would have a leg to stand on. But if we talking about how people criticise the game you enjoy and you get mad because they dare to do so? Sorry that not how discussion works.<br /> <br /> Are you, and this other posters characters so weak that the declaration of opinion, not fact mind you because (at least me) said &quot;<u><b>I think </b></u>Mage is not a good game about hubris&quot; instead of saying a declaration like &quot;mage is a bad game about hubris, thats a fact&quot; so sensible that the idea of dissent offends you?<br /> <br /> And you keep citing the OP but honestly if you had bothered to read past the title, is obviously that the houserules that the OP posted are intended for crossover. You dont think crossover is part of the game, fine i dont either, but i dont go posting to threadcrap as you have been doing, because thats all your post so far. Not even a constructive cristicism of the houserules just a &quot;crossovers are bad by design&quot; rince and repeat.<br /> <br /> Also you keep claiming that people arguing against you make it hard for you to &quot;help&quot; the OP but you arent helping the OP on his rules to do crossover, you are just saying dont do it, crossovers arent intended you should appreciate mage as solo game. And while i do share that opinion putting it out constantly (as you have been doing) is not helpful to the person asking.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-03-2017, 02:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T11:59:35-07:00","id":1136981,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136976#post1136976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> From the horses mouth<br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> So then your main gripe is that the ST needs to be able to meet your expectations? Again, not trying to be dismissive but the example of &quot;monkey paw&quot; vs. &quot;meticulous group&quot; seems to illustrate the weakness of not being challenging enough on its own.<br /> <br /> Mages are primarily threats to one another, I think, because they mostly deal with one another. Given that they need to communicate with each other to gain and pass knowledge, or fight over resources. This more or less doesn't address the fact that an ST is perfectly capable of structuring chronicles away from Mage interactions and push you into the unknown.<br /> <br /> I will concede that Mage can be problematic if the ST and group don't agree on what is to be expected. If the game is approached without agreeing on stuff, you're liable to disagree about stuff.<br /> <br /> I don't think it's a negative. The game opens on neutral ground and can be scaled up or down with input.<br /> <br /> I know the game is perfectly fine when I play with certain friends, but it can get out of hand if I don't scale up the difficulty against my more D&amp;D (optimization-based friends) minded friends.<br /> <br /> Some of my friends have zero interest in dying and would much rather explore a world and partake in stories with less DEFINITIVE penalties. They rather be mangled and disfigured, robbed or mind-raped than start a new character after spending so much time in character creation. Other friends would feel robbed or like it was a waste of time if they couldn't just get sniped from a rooftop or hit with a suicide spell.<br /> <br /> I don't think the game needs to be moved to harsher territory when it can be scaled up with a pre-chronicle meeting on expectations and outcomes.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T13:15:33-07:00","id":1137005,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136976#post1136976\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> This is what <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar2102_3.png');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"2102\" data-vbnamecard=\"2102\">MCN</a> and me were saying, the game depends A LOT more on the group and DM in question to function. If I don't want to play monkey paw to the actions of the PCs and they play it smart/moral then it would fall flat. Not saying it is a big design flaw, when you get a group that fully embraces that go for it but it shouldn't be a surprise that there are also people who don't subscribe to that. <b>Yeah, it depends upon the group more by design. This isn't dnd where its ok and expected for people to &quot;roll up&quot; a character and throw some dice around. Descriptions and details are the life of the game. </b><br /> <br /> <br /> As irritating as people coming to the &quot;defense&quot; of mage posting endlessly &quot;you don't get it, if you just could get it. You would see there is 100% perfect as it is&quot;. <b>More irritating. If we wish to have our perspective presented, we either have to hope for the person to read and re-read more of the books and forum posts, or we have to address every new person that decides to bring the topic up because people don't take the time to search for topics that have already been discussed. You have the luxury of simply ignoring the thread since &quot;play how you want&quot; is already in the core rule book. </b><br /> <br /> <br /> And I want to bring people to mine, so tough luck that's the nature of debate. We are stuck with each other. And if you bothered to read I am not rejecting the fact that people like mage. <b>You are, however, saying that it isn't ok for us to bring the legitimate claim that someone might not understand the game.</b> I challenge the binary notions of &quot;WoD games are play as intended by everyone&quot; and &quot;There 2 kinds of people, people who don't get mage and people who get mage and thus love it&quot;. I'm not even challenging that there is horror in mage but instead pointing out that it will depend on the group and DM dynamic for it to have an effect. <b>So you're acknowledging that the game is a role playing game. Group and DM dynamic is everything to getting any role playing game to work well. Furthermore, none of us that have been arguing for playing &quot;as intended&quot; have said that that is the only way to play. We typically go out of our way to remind people that the rule books talk about throwing out what you want when this topic is brought up (yes, this is my reminder). </b><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> So let me get this clear? You want to shove your opinion on other people but don't want others opinions to oppose yours? Wow that's a pretty shitty argument. I don't have to help you in any way or form to make your argument for you, if your arguments requires other people to not challenge them then you lack the ability to properly defend it and shouldn't complain. <b>No. I'm not interested in SHOVING my opinion on others. Your word, not mine. I'm interested in presenting the FACT that horror is designed into the game. I'm interested in presenting the FACT that the game is designed to be balanced against itself. <u>Most importantly, I'm interested in doing what I can to present the vision of the developers to people that come to the game so that they can decide for themselves what they want to do</u>. Mage isn't easy to grok or appreciate. Therefore I don't appreciate people saying that we shouldn't defend the game just because people can play how they want. Congratulations, you've presented THE basic principle of all gaming (playing how you want so long as it's fun). Once you're done with that, we would like to show that this game does have its own niche to present. </b><br /> <br /> <br /> Again I don't care about how you feel or others feel on that matter when the mere fact that their opinions get challenged upset them. <b>The existence of these themes isn't an opinion, it's fact. It's in the game. Stating it isn't in the game is tantamount to calling both us and the developers liars. THAT is offensive (as is calling my arguments shitty). THAT is why we get upset when people say we should stop explaining the design of the game. You might not be making the claim that these themes aren't in the game, but you ARE making the claim (by saying that it's irritating whenever we jump to the defense of the game and its design) that we shouldn't explain our position to those that might not understand it.</b><br /> <br /> Are you, and these other posters character so weak that the declaration of opinion, not fact mind you because (at least I) said &quot;<u><b>I think </b></u>Mage is not a good game about hubris&quot; instead of saying a declaration like &quot;mage is a bad game about hubris, that's a fact&quot; so sensible that the idea of dissent offends you? <b>Character attack aside, no dissent doesn't offend me. Dissent that states that we irritate someone just for presenting our side offends me. If this were politics or economics, I could understand how our position could be inherently offensive to some people, and thus causing people to give dissent. However, this is us explaining GAME design. As in, &quot;if you don't understand the game, you might be missing out on something fun.&quot;</b><br /> <br /> And you keep citing the OP but honestly if you had bothered to read past the title, it's obvious that the house rules that the OP posted are intended for crossover. You don't think crossover is part of the game, fine i don't either, but i don't go posting to thread crap as you have been doing, because that's all your post so far. Not even a constructive criticism of the house rules just a &quot;crossovers are bad by design&quot; rinse and repeat. <b>Cross overs go against game design, they are not necessarily bad by design (since the design of such is in the hands of the ST, not the developers). This just means it will take more effort on the part of the ST to create a good design (not easy to do given the sheer number of hours put in just to create a game line). Also, my constructive criticism has been there, regardless of your notice of it. I quite clearly mentioned that understanding how to change the game comes with understanding the mechanics of the game. Understanding the mechanics of the game comes with playing it. I also implied that it is easier to understand the mechanics of the game by making an effort to play the game according to game design. I may be presenting a basic concept here (understanding a game through and through allows a person to change the game more readily to what said person wants), but I've noticed that this concept gets forgotten. Ergo, constructive criticism expressed.</b><br /> <br /> Also you keep claiming that people arguing against you make it hard for you to &quot;help&quot; the OP but you aren't helping the OP on his rules to do crossover, <b>Sure I am. Stating that someone shouldn't be so quick to throw something out means that that person has more material to work with should they use the game. </b>you are just saying don't do it, crossovers aren't intended you should appreciate mage as solo game.<b> Perhaps I didn't get this across very well. My apologies, that's my fault (not being sarcastic here). I'm saying do these things first. And do them plenty. THEN if you want to do crossover and/or alter the game do it to your heart's content. This way you have the chance to appreciate the game, and if not, you're more prepared to change it as needed. </b> And while I do share that opinion<b> O_o ? Really? You really do share the opinion that someone should try to appreciate the game for its own merits first? Maybe you do, but I didn't get the impression from anything you have said.</b> putting it out constantly (as you have been doing) is not helpful to the person asking.</div> </div> </div> </div>There, that's my rebuttal.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T14:20:27-07:00","id":1137036,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There, that's my rebuttal.</div> </div> </div> </div>Indeed, and you put it all in quotes, how dishonest of you.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>You are, however, saying that it isn't ok for us to bring the legitimate claim that someone might not understand the game.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Because your claim isnt legitimate at all. The claim i am contesting is the binary view of &quot;there is 2 kinds of people, people who dont get mage and people who do get mage and love it&quot;<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Furthermore, none of us that have been arguing for playing &quot;as intended&quot; have said that that is the only way to play. We typically go out of our way to remind people that the rule books talk about throwing out what you want when this topic is brought up (yes, this is my reminder). </b></div> </div> </div> </div>The hypocrisy in this comment is specially sweet considering<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136803#post1136803\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u><b>You're not playing Mage if you just toss out its core theme</b></u>. You're just borrowing its mechanics at that point. And, honestly, if that is what you want then you should probably familiarize yourself with those mechanics since they are, after all, what you want. And if that is what you want, why bother with asking how to alter them? Seriously, it's not that hard. I did it for years before appreciating Mage's setting and themes.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>No. I'm not interested in SHOVING my opinion on others. Your word, not mine. </b></div> </div> </div> </div>No, actually your words<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136963#post1136963\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">We want to share the experience of the game, but that is partially undercut when people criticize the developers for not placing horror in the game or for doing a terrible job at it, or saying that it's perfectly fine to have a crossover game despite it being against nearly all game designs (beast excluded).</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>I'm interested in presenting the FACT that horror is designed into the game. I'm interested in presenting the FACT that the game is designed to be balanced against itself. <u>Most importantly, I'm interested in doing what I can to present the vision of the developers to people that come to the game so that they can decide for themselves what they want to do</u>. Mage isn't easy to grok or appreciate. </b></div> </div> </div> </div>Things that arent A) Part of the OP original request.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Therefore I don't appreciate people saying that we shouldn't defend the game just because people can play how they want. </b></div> </div> </div> </div>And B) I dont appreciate shoving the &quot;one true way&quot; relentlestly and assuming that everyone should get behind you little bandwagon.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>The existence of these themes isn't an opinion, it's fact. It's in the game. Stating it isn't in the game is tantamount to calling both us and the developers liars. THAT is offensive (as is calling my arguments shitty). THAT is why we get upset when people say we should stop explaining the design of the game. You might not be making the claim that these themes aren't in the game, but you ARE making the claim (by saying that it's irritating whenever we jump to the defense of the game and its design) that we shouldn't explain our position to those that might not understand it.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>First of all saying one believes that the game doesnt properly do a theme it isnt calling the developers a liar but instead criticiscim their product. As for<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>but you ARE making the claim (by saying that it's irritating whenever we jump to the defense of the game and its design) that we shouldn't explain our position to those that might not understand it.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>You are claiming that is bad when i say the argument of &quot;If you only could get mage you will love it&quot; is irritating but at the same time say that when people challenge it.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><i>but it's quite irritating to see people try to come to the defense of &quot;Anyone can play the game however they want, don't push your way of playing&quot; when the way the op talks about the topic implies that he doesn't understand the game design.</i></div> </div> </div> </div>thats is an hypocrite argument.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Character attack aside, no dissent doesn't offend me. Dissent that states that we irritate someone just for presenting our side offends me. If this were politics or economics, I could understand how our position could be inherently offensive to some people, and thus causing people to give dissent. However, this is us explaining GAME design. As in, &quot;if you don't understand the game, you might be missing out on something fun.&quot;</b></div> </div> </div> </div>Is not character attack questioning that you get &quot;offended&quot; by other people disagreeing with you, is not a mature way to handle discussions.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Also, my constructive criticism has been there, regardless of your notice of it. I quite clearly mentioned that understanding how to change the game comes with understanding the mechanics of the game. </b></div> </div> </div> </div>Just in case i might be wrong i went back and read your contribution and your &quot;constructive criticism&quot; boils down to &quot;dont do crossover and get gud&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137005#post1137005\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>O_o ? Really? You really do share the opinion that someone should try to appreciate the game for its own merits first? Maybe you do, but I didn't get the impression from anything you have said.</b></div> </div> </div> </div>No, i share your opinion that Crossover games arent good. However unlike you i dont go threadcrapping on people wanting to do crossover.<br /> <br /> TLDR: The issue is of time and place, you wanna defend mage as written? Do so in a &quot;sell me on mage&quot; thread or in a &quot;Tell me why you love mage&quot; thread. But coming to a &quot;help me rework mage&quot; thread to defend mage is irritating because nothing gets done. No interesting idea gets posted.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136981#post1136981\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So then your main gripe is that the ST needs to be able to meet your expectations? Again, not trying to be dismissive but the example of &quot;monkey paw&quot; vs. &quot;meticulous group&quot; seems to illustrate the weakness of not being challenging enough on its own.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you are discussing 2 different points. The part i quoted on dave is answering to your comment of &quot;<i>Why are mages and the abyss the only real threats to PCs? </i>&quot; which even Dave admit thats pretty much it. Supernal/abyss stuff, other mages and maybe lower depth things. That other splats are outclassed agaisnt mage.<br /> <br /> On the &quot;Meticulous group&quot; and &quot;monkey paw&quot; is referring as to why for some groups the horror and hubris aspect of mage might fall flat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-03-2017, 05:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T14:23:04-07:00","id":1137039,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136954#post1136954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm not understanding how Paradox is a joke.</div> </div> </div> </div> Show, don't tell. Personal experience has shown that its not nearly living up to the hype as proivided in the book in a lot of situations outside combat.<br /> <br /> On the matter of number crunching - I know WoD players that are driven away from the game because the complexity and math intimdiate them, or they find it unfun, etc. That hurts if you don't make the game approachable.<br /> <br /> As for the rest? Thank you for telling me that the mechanical issues I feel can be improved upon and tightened up are all wrong. Have a nice day!<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136968#post1136968\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*snip*.</div> </div> </div> </div> Between your attempts to tell the &quot;right way&quot; to play the game and yoru arguments that hinge on a lack of player agency over their character, I'm just going to disagree with you in the strongest terms. Your entire argument is flawed from the beginning. <br /> <br /> You want to play that way, go ahead. Have fun. Goodbye.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2102-mcn","name":"MCN","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2102_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3479"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T14:49:34-07:00","id":1137051,"title":"","content":"So, do you just, like, play as recklessly as possible? All the reach all the time, cast in front of sleepers, inure yourself to trivial stuff? <br /> <br /> Cos if you don't I think paradox is having more of an effect on your game than you think.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T15:07:30-07:00","id":1137055,"title":"","content":"So, I'm gonna give some advice for making mage a bit more horror-esque.<br /> <br /> My advice is roll for Acts of Hubris more often. The conditons for acts of hubris are really bad, and they often have stuff to do with the obsessions. I know I have issues with acts of hubris because there aren't a lot of examples, but I'm sure you can consult people on the forums for sone help with that.<br /> <br /> Another thing, remember that for each successive spell you cast you get another paradox die for the scene. Which doesn't sound so bad at first, but I am fairly certain that is entirely the point; it doesn't seem so bad at first, until you are on spell six and you just summoned an Abyssal entity.<br /> <br /> Also, when it comes to supernatural experimentation, always remember that the other splats have friends and families. How can you not look at yourself like a monster when you've had some vampire locked in your basement for three weeks, and their friend came in guns blazing to save them. Of course, that situation will probably happen to people with low wisdom and those who have a condition from an Act of Hubris.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12436-ever-professional","name":"Ever Professional","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12436_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T15:10:21-07:00","id":1137057,"title":"","content":"I mean, if you think about it, if you ever relinquish control of a spell, that's a willpower dot you lost to the fear of paradox. If it was really a joke you could just ignore spell control and walk around with 26 spells on the go.<br /> <br /> Mages are Hopper, and paradox is the ants from Bugs Life. You might <i>claim</i> you're not afraid of it, but we all know if you don't keep it in line it'll come down like an avalanche.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":3166,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","user":"Elfive","date":". 09-03-2017, 06:12 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T15:11:46-07:00","id":1137059,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137039#post1137039\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Between your attempts to tell the &quot;right way&quot; to play the game and yoru arguments that hinge on a lack of player agency over their character, I'm just going to disagree with you in the strongest terms. Your entire argument is flawed from the beginning.<br /> <br /> You want to play that way, go ahead. Have fun. Goodbye.</div> </div> </div> </div>Lack of Player Agency? It's called buy in and playing in accordance to it. If you play a game about noir detectives and complain about how it's not facilitating your superhero, you done messed up. If you're playing Promethean and complain that it's not working as an expression for your transhumanistic and/or misanthropic desires, you done messed up. And if you ignore all the ways in which mages are monsters and then complain that it's not horror, <u><i>you done messed up</i></u>. None of that is on the game.<br /> <br /> Games have buy-ins, and with them comes a refinement on what expected game play is. I don't get to try and make friends with psychos trying to chop my face off in Borderlands 2 either, but you don't hear me saying the game isn't working.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":6}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T15:42:15-07:00","id":1137078,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ever Professional</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137055#post1137055\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, when it comes to supernatural experimentation, always remember that the other splats have friends and families.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> When I crush insects, I care not at all about their purported insect friends and family.<br /> <br /> The insignificant feelings of a few lab animals are nothing compared to unlocking the very secrets of reality.<br /> <br /> Besides, it's not like I'm hurting real people...<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/618-branford","name":"branford","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar618_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1904"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T15:44:51-07:00","id":1137079,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>branford</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137078#post1137078\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> When I crush insects, I care not at all about their purported insect friends and family.<br /> <br /> The insignificant feelings of a few lab animals are nothing compared to unlocking the very secrets of reality.<br /> <br /> Besides, it's not like I'm hurting real people...<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /></div> </div> </div> </div>I was about to be really confused, then I saw the winky face.<br /> <br /> Also, for groups who tend away from this sort of play, why not introduce an NPC that is like this. Show them what they can easily become, like how you'd introduce a Draugr to a vampire game, or werewolves of each end of Harmony in werewolf.<br /> <br /> EDIT: <br /> It'd help with a bit of introspective horror, or you can take them to a magic deadzone to show them how much they've changed since the awakening.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":12436,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12436-ever-professional","user":"Ever Professional","date":".  Didn't post my full thought. 09-03-2017, 06:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12436-ever-professional","name":"Ever Professional","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12436_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T15:44:57-07:00","id":1137080,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137036#post1137036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I think you are discussing 2 different points. The part i quoted on dave is answering to your comment of &quot;<i>Why are mages and the abyss the only real threats to PCs? </i>&quot; which even Dave admit thats pretty much it. Supernal/abyss stuff, other mages and maybe lower depth things. That other splats are outclassed agaisnt mage.<br /> <br /> On the &quot;Meticulous group&quot; and &quot;monkey paw&quot; is referring as to why for some groups the horror and hubris aspect of mage might fall flat.</div> </div> </div> </div>&quot;Mages and the abyss are the only real threats&quot; isn't supported by the quote you cited. Dave states many things can be a threat to mages in that very quote. He ends it with &quot;Mages are principally antagonists for each other&quot; - but that doesn't say Mages are the only thing, among the abyss, that concerns mages.<br /> <br /> He specifically cited various other creatures and circumstances that can challenge and defeat mages - he also acknowledged &quot;They can cope with it, or not, and die.&quot;<br /> <br /> His statement didn't really state mages outclass all other splats, he cited situations where they can prevail. Mages need to be dumbed down for the same reason most antagonists in tabletop get dumbed down. I'm sure werewolves and vampire NPCs, played hyper-aware and with as much fore-thought as tactical PCs could give mage PCs a run for their money.<br /> <br /> Any time you make a Mage vs Splat thread you immediately get an avalanche of posts detailing the pointlessness of whiteroom fights. There is obviously a reason for that.<br /> <br /> <br /> Your second point is something I already acknowledged. If the group hasn't agreed on what is to be expected, particularly if they find they aren't being challenged enough as Mages, then it kind of falls back on what the ST is doing to challenge them. I already acknowledged it's somewhat of a neutral setting, but my counterpoint is I'm not seeing why it shouldn't sit where it currently does.<br /> <br /> Why should things be more fucked up if the amount of fuckery can be increased with the simple agreement with ST and group prior to game session?<br /> <br /> You're pointing out a &quot;flaw,&quot; sure, but you're not convincing me why your &quot;flaw&quot; is worth changing the game for. This is essentially as much a flaw as me saying &quot;Well, you can't really play Mage as a Vampire Awakened Werewolf, if you wanted to. So that's kind of a problem.&quot; Okay, you're right, you can't (technically, you can if that's your deal) - but what about it?<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:02:22-07:00","id":1137090,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ever Professional</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137079#post1137079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Also, for groups who tend away from this sort of play,</div> </div> </div> </div>As a rule, everyone should play however they like. They goal is to enjoy yourself, and there's no fear that the badwrongfun police will come knocking at your door.<br /> <br /> Nevertheless, Mage, just like every other rpg, definitely has clearly intended themes and setting, with a rules set to emphasize these priorities. Hubris, privilege and the corruption of power are the foundation and fundamentals of Mage.<br /> <br /> Given the vast number of rpg choices available, I admit to being a little confused why people who don't actually like or want to embrace Mage's setting and themes would actually choose or want to play it. If you want to role-play humanitarian, heroic or just generally &quot;nice&quot; pc's in Mage, it will almost certainly feel like fitting a square peg in a round hole.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":618,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/618-branford","user":"branford","date":".  typos 09-03-2017, 07:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/618-branford","name":"branford","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar618_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1904"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:04:29-07:00","id":1137092,"title":"","content":"*Totally once ran a vampire game where the antagonists were the characters from Twilight for humour purposes*","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:11:12-07:00","id":1137097,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137059#post1137059\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Lack of Player Agency? It's called buy in and playing in accordance to it. If you play a game about noir detectives and complain about how it's not facilitating your superhero, you done messed up. If you're playing Promethean and complain that it's not working as an expression for your transhumanistic and/or misanthropic desires, you done messed up. And if you ignore all the ways in which mages are monsters and then complain that it's not horror, <u><i>you done messed up</i></u>. None of that is on the game.<br /> <br /> Games have buy-ins, and with them comes a refinement on what expected game play is. I don't get to try and make friends with psychos trying to chop my face off in Borderlands 2 either, but you don't hear me saying the game isn't working.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, you know I'm firmly a member of the &quot;mages are monsters&quot; camp. But I think that, for some people, the issue is that the ways in which Mages are monsters <i>is ignorable</i>. A noir detective game doesn't give you the tools to play a superhero. Promethean's humanist themes are inseparably integrated into the mechanics. Mage presents mages as monsters in the fiction, and it gives you the tools to play a monstrous mage. But it doesn't do anything to discourage you from ignoring those tools. People read that Mages are supposed to be monsters, then they play the game and don't experience any compulsion towards monstrosity. They are given the option of playing the game the way it is presented in the fiction, but not prevented from playing it in a different way, so they question the fiction. It's an issue of ludonarrative dissonance. This is, unfortunately, a natural consequence of the carrots over sticks design philosophy.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:13:38-07:00","id":1137100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137080#post1137080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Mages and the abyss are the only real threats&quot; isn't supported by the quote you cited. Dave states many things can be a threat to mages in that very quote. He ends it with &quot;Mages are principally antagonists for each other&quot; - but that doesn't say Mages are the only thing, among the abyss, that concerns mages.<br /> <br /> He specifically cited various other creatures and circumstances that can challenge and defeat mages - he also acknowledged &quot;They can cope with it, or not, and die.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Except that those threats that Dave says the mages can cope or die from are mostly mage exclusive (High ranking goetia, Supernal/Abyssal entities, Lower Depths things, dimensional tears)**<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137080#post1137080\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're pointing out a &quot;flaw,&quot; sure, but you're not convincing me why your &quot;flaw&quot; is worth changing the game for. This is essentially as much a flaw as me saying &quot;Well, you can't really play Mage as a Vampire Awakened Werewolf, if you wanted to. So that's kind of a problem.&quot; Okay, you're right, you can't (technically, you can if that's your deal) - but what about it?</div> </div> </div> </div>I am not? Me, pointing out the monkey paw DM and the &quot;Meticulous group&quot; are to answer as to &quot;Why people dont immediately get the horror in mage?&quot; by answering you need to be in a very specific headspace both mentally and gameplay wise to get it and even then is no garantee you like it or think is well done.<br /> <br /> As for the antagonists, i am no saying that the game (as a product) should be changed for crossover but that mages as written overclass other splats by a mile.<br /> <br /> **Maybe lower depth things are also Mummy/geist/beast things? I am not familiar with those gamelines.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:14:41-07:00","id":1137102,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137092#post1137092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*Totally once ran a vampire game where the antagonists were the characters from Twilight for humour purposes*</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Sparkly vampires definitely serve the Lie! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/618-branford","name":"branford","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar618_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1904"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:16:35-07:00","id":1137104,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Elfive</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137092#post1137092\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">*Totally once ran a vampire game where the antagonists were the characters from Twilight for humour purposes*</div> </div> </div> </div>I like it. Did you use the Players bloodline for added funny?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12436-ever-professional","name":"Ever Professional","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12436_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:19:00-07:00","id":1137106,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137100#post1137100\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> **Maybe lower depth things are also Mummy/geist/beast things? I am not familiar with those gamelines.</div> </div> </div> </div>Mummies are from the Lower Depths.<br /> <br /> Also, Strix, the new primary antagonist and big bad in Requiem, are from the Lower Depths.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/618-branford","name":"branford","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar618_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1904"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:20:49-07:00","id":1137107,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137100#post1137100\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">**Maybe lower depth things are also Mummy/geist/beast things? I am not familiar with those gamelines.</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe the Duat counts as a Lower depth, and the Duat comes from Mummy.<br /> <br /> Vampires also get in on the Lower Depth action by being able to use Cruac to summon shadowy entities from a realm that is essentially oblivion to non-vampires (vampires can't enter it), and the strix realm is a lower depth.<br /> <br /> A few other splats can get in on the fun, I just don't remember which ones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12436-ever-professional","name":"Ever Professional","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12436_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:25:24-07:00","id":1137110,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>branford</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137090#post1137090\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Given the vast number of rpg choices available, I admit to being a little confused why people who don't actually like or want to embrace Mage's setting and themes would actually choose or want to play it. If you want to role-play humanitarian, heroic or just generally &quot;nice&quot; pc's in Mage, it will almost certainly feel like fitting a square peg in a round hole.</div> </div> </div> </div>Plenty of reasons. One might like the setting but no the themes. Liking the flexibility of the magic system if not their power. Or like certain part of the mechanics. Being confortable with the system itself. Or lack of decent alternative.<br /> <br /> Speaking only for myself, i like plenty of elements of mage (Yantra system, legacies, 90% of the setting) but a few things i dislike: The themes, Wisdom, power level, the seers as written and i am confortable enough with the system so its a matter of tweaking until i get what i want (in my case a game about fireballs in dark alleys). As for other systems, not many alternatives as i dislike apocalypse world and FATE/Dresden, Unknown armies bent on madness is not exactly what i am looking for and Ascension system is awful to me. And GURPS ascension requires me to find, read and convert GURPs. <br /> <br /> If Dracula makes right to his promise to make WoD great again (which i doubt) maybe that option will open up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:37:58-07:00","id":1137116,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137036#post1137036\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Indeed, and you put it all in quotes, how dishonest of you.<br /> <br /> <br /> Because your claim isnt legitimate at all. The claim i am contesting is the binary view of &quot;there is 2 kinds of people, people who dont get mage and people who do get mage and love it&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> The hypocrisy in this comment is specially sweet considering<br /> <br /> No, actually your words<br /> <br /> Things that arent A) Part of the OP original request.<br /> <br /> And B) I dont appreciate shoving the &quot;one true way&quot; relentlestly and assuming that everyone should get behind you little bandwagon.<br /> <br /> First of all saying one believes that the game doesnt properly do a theme it isnt calling the developers a liar but instead criticiscim their product. As for<br /> <br /> You are claiming that is bad when i say the argument of &quot;If you only could get mage you will love it&quot; is irritating but at the same time say that when people challenge it.<br /> <br /> thats is an hypocrite argument.<br /> <br /> Is not character attack questioning that you get &quot;offended&quot; by other people disagreeing with you, is not a mature way to handle discussions.<br /> <br /> Just in case i might be wrong i went back and read your contribution and your &quot;constructive criticism&quot; boils down to &quot;dont do crossover and get gud&quot;.<br /> <br /> No, i share your opinion that Crossover games arent good. However unlike you i dont go threadcrapping on people wanting to do crossover.<br /> <br /> TLDR: The issue is of time and place, you wanna defend mage as written? Do so in a &quot;sell me on mage&quot; thread or in a &quot;Tell me why you love mage&quot; thread. But coming to a &quot;help me rework mage&quot; thread to defend mage is irritating because nothing gets done. No interesting idea gets posted.</div> </div> </div> </div>There's nothing dishonest about how I did my rebuttal. It's quite clear when I'm speaking and when you are speaking.<br /> <br /> The two aren't mutually exclusive. Saying &quot;You aren't playing Mage by throwing out a core theme&quot; doesn't contradict the statement of &quot;play how you want.&quot; It simply means that you're missing out on potential fun if you throw something critical out. Which is a circular way of simply restating the same thing. So no, there isn't anything hypocritical about what I said.<br /> <br /> Forums are places where things get off topic. But frankly, defending Mage from people like you and explaining it to people like the op is VERY related to the topic. It goes like this: &quot;Hey, these games are DEFINITELY set in the same world and this splat is CLEARLY over powered. I want to change this game, here's some ideas, what do you think?&quot; Other people see the tone of the thread and its title as well as the clear misunderstanding and go &quot;Oh....hey um. Ok, so what I think is that your thread is presuming a few things that the design doesn't actually present. Maybe you should take a moment to see what it IS presenting? I mean, here's some pretty obvious ways to change the game, but you're definitely not going to give yourself the chance to have all the fun you might have if you don't take a little bit of time to at least understand the design first.&quot;<br /> <br /> Once again, you're word, not mine. You have an extreme antithesis towards the concept that there might be a designed way to play and that it's ok for people to promote that design.<br /> <br /> NO. Pointing out that you're making a character attack is not an immature way of handling the discussion. It is showing your poor arguing skills because character attacks are logical fallacies. People with poor character can still make good arguments.<br /> <br /> As to the claim that I thread crapped: once again, false. I didn't just say &quot;don't do crossover and get gud.&quot; I also gave a VERY obvious idea: &quot;Hey, maybe if you think mages are overpowered, why don't you try using a weaker mage? All mages have to purchase dots like other splats, try using one that isn't a master or an adept.&quot; I may not have used all of those words (more specifically the second sentence), but the intent is clearly there. But since that idea isn't good enough for you, here's a few more:<br /> <br /> 1. Require all casting of spells to spend either mana or willpower.<br /> 2. Increase the cost of purchasing gnosis and arcana<br /> 3. Ambush the mage. Like, seriously, ambush the crap out of that prideful sob.<br /> 4. Don't give the mage all the knowledge. Let him work for it. Yeah, he can figure stuff out, but he can't do it all immediately.<br /> 5. Give distractions to the mage. They all have their obsessions. Push the fact that it really is an obsession that must be pursued.<br /> 6. Give yourselves shadow names. No need to let mages know who you are.<br /> 7. Make allies with other mages.<br /> 8. Bring a horde of sleepers.<br /> 9. Bring in political pressure. It doesn't matter if they've got a head start, getting in at least one solid political punch can put them on the defensive.<br /> 10.Make sure paradox isn't just baring it's teeth. It needs to have it's breath hot on the necks of these sob mages. This can include things like doubling the cost of reducing paradox dice or doubling the amount of paradox dice produced by anything that does.<br /> 11. Automatically take away one dice from wisdom checks for acts of Hubris. This becomes especially vicious if you say that there is an epidemic of mages that have the vice of wrath. Killing with magic is a very quick way to lose wisdom. This becomes compounded by the two conditions granted for losing a dot of wisdom (yeah, no extra check there, you just get the condition).<br /> 12. And a bonus one: do some more research on the topic. This topic has been brought up quite a few times, so there are other ideas that have been presented in other threads. You might be interested in them.<br /> <br /> <u>Your claim that my claim isn't legitimate is obviously false. You don't get to make the claim that you can understand Mage and don't buy into it's premises and also say that it's perfectly fine to push anyone and everyone away from said premises instead of explaining them. THAT is dishonest. You're not affording others the benefit of the doubt that you claim to have. Especially when you say something like this: &quot;</u>i am confortable enough with the system so its a matter of tweaking until i get what i want&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":7825,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","user":"Falcon777","date":". 09-03-2017, 07:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7825-falcon777","name":"Falcon777","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"490"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:41:43-07:00","id":1137117,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137100#post1137100\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Except that those threats that Dave says the mages can cope or die from are mostly mage exclusive (High ranking goetia, Supernal/Abyssal entities, Lower Depths things, dimensional tears)**</div> </div> </div> </div>That's still quite a lot of things. I'm also left wondering why the worst of the Awakened haven't subjugated the rest of the splats; or at least relegated them to cute things to experiment on and crack open.<br /> <br /> I mean, there is a reason Mages still have to take care when dealing with other splats. They're not entirely the top of the food chain. This also ignores relativistic comparisons of top ranking vampires and werewolves dealing with fledgling or slightly seasoned mages.<br /> <br /> I remember hearing even Changeling can cause serious hurt for Mages.<br /> <br /> I also recall Mummies being serious shit, but can't recall why. I've never played it or read anything about the line other than a few power descriptions that sounded like end of times shit.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:47:54-07:00","id":1137120,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137110#post1137110\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Plenty of reasons. One might like the setting but no the themes. Liking the flexibility of the magic system if not their power. Or like certain part of the mechanics. Being confortable with the system itself. Or lack of decent alternative.<br /> <br /> Speaking only for myself, i like plenty of elements of mage (Yantra system, legacies, 90% of the setting) but a few things i dislike: The themes, Wisdom, power level, the seers as written and i am confortable enough with the system so its a matter of tweaking until i get what i want (in my case a game about fireballs in dark alleys). As for other systems, not many alternatives as i dislike apocalypse world and FATE/Dresden, Unknown armies bent on madness is not exactly what i am looking for and Ascension system is awful to me. And GURPS ascension requires me to find, read and convert GURPs.<br /> <br /> If Dracula makes right to his promise to make WoD great again (which i doubt) maybe that option will open up.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> With all due respect, while it appears that you certainly like Mage's crunchy mechanical magic rules (but not its power level), you dislike almost everything else, virtually the entire setting that makes Mage, well.., Mage.<br /> <br /> I cannot imagine that small tweaks to Mage will ever feel satisfying to you or anyone who feels similarly, and the attempt will be an exercise in frustration and anger.<br /> <br /> My issue is that so many people complain about Mage for being Mage and not some totally different game that suits their unique tastes and preferences. That's an impossible standard. Requesting suggestions for minor houserules or setting tweaks is one thing, and most of us use them, but effectively demanding an entirely new game unsurprisingly is bound to irritate the many fans who like Mage on its own merits.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/618-branford","name":"branford","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar618_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1904"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T16:48:40-07:00","id":1137121,"title":"","content":"Mummy's power stat goes backwards.They start at 10 and slowly wind down the longer they've been awake.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-03T17:46:54-07:00","id":1137138,"title":"","content":"Falcon777, i have to ask. Are you an english speaker? Because i think you are missreading 90% of what am saying and discussing.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137116#post1137116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><u>Your claim that my claim isn't legitimate is obviously false. You don't get to make the claim that you can understand Mage and don't buy into it's premises and also say that it's perfectly fine to push anyone and everyone away from said premises instead of explaining them. THAT is dishonest. You're not affording others the benefit of the doubt that you claim to have. Especially when you say something like this: &quot;</u>i am confortable enough with the system so its a matter of tweaking until i get what i want&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Uhm, thats no the claim that i contest? The claim i contest is &quot;2 kinds of people, people who dont get mage and people who do and thus love it&quot; and by my very existence thats claim is false as i dont fall in any of those categories.<br /> <br /> And i not saying people cant like mage just that defending mage shouldn't come up in a thread about house-ruling mage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137116#post1137116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">NO. Pointing out that you're making a character attack is not an immature way of handling the discussion. It is showing your poor arguing skills because character attacks are logical fallacies. People with poor character can still make good arguments.</div> </div> </div> </div>What? No seriously i think you are confuse here on what i meant. You said people get defensive/offended when others (me, for example) critique mage. And i said that a) i dont care about how you feel. And b) Feeling offended by others opinion of a game is immature. Nothing less, nothing more.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137116#post1137116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Forums are places where things get off topic. But frankly, defending Mage from people like you and explaining it to people like the op is VERY related to the topic. It goes like this: &quot;Hey, these games are DEFINITELY set in the same world and this splat is CLEARLY over powered. I want to change this game, here's some ideas, what do you think?&quot; Other people see the tone of the thread and its title as well as the clear misunderstanding and go &quot;Oh....hey um. Ok, so what I think is that your thread is presuming a few things that the design doesn't actually present. Maybe you should take a moment to see what it IS presenting? I mean, here's some pretty obvious ways to change the game, but you're definitely not going to give yourself the chance to have all the fun you might have if you don't take a little bit of time to at least understand the design first.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>or you could read his ideas and actually critique them? Or propose your own, it took you 5+ post to actually start posting something relevant here:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Falcon777</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137116#post1137116\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1. Require all casting of spells to spend either mana or willpower.<br /> 2. Increase the cost of purchasing gnosis and arcana<br /> 3. Ambush the mage. Like, seriously, ambush the crap out of that prideful sob.<br /> 4. Don't give the mage all the knowledge. Let him work for it. Yeah, he can figure stuff out, but he can't do it all immediately.<br /> 5. Give distractions to the mage. They all have their obsessions. Push the fact that it really is an obsession that must be pursued.<br /> 6. Give yourselves shadow names. No need to let mages know who you are.<br /> 7. Make allies with other mages.<br /> 8. Bring a horde of sleepers.<br /> 9. Bring in political pressure. It doesn't matter if they've got a head start, getting in at least one solid political punch can put them on the defensive.<br /> 10.Make sure paradox isn't just baring it's teeth. It needs to have it's breath hot on the necks of these sob mages. This can include things like doubling the cost of reducing paradox dice or doubling the amount of paradox dice produced by anything that does.<br /> 11. Automatically take away one dice from wisdom checks for acts of Hubris. This becomes especially vicious if you say that there is an epidemic of mages that have the vice of wrath. Killing with magic is a very quick way to lose wisdom. This becomes compounded by the two conditions granted for losing a dot of wisdom (yeah, no extra check there, you just get the condition).<br /> 12. And a bonus one: do some more research on the topic. This topic has been brought up quite a few times, so there are other ideas that have been presented in other threads. You might be interested in them.</div> </div> </div> </div>1. Would this be on top of the already required mana expenditure for casting outside of your path arcanums? Or maybe when you cast path arcanums you need to spend WP and when outside of those Mana.<br /> 2. Could work thought in my experience is best to just put a cap on arcanums per chapter but that requires a dm wanting to do time skips. So is not bad.<br /> 4. You mean that knowing spells dont work as intended?<br /> 6. Thats applicable to supernatural? Or you mean humans?<br /> 7. Brings the problem back to mages power and strain credibility.<br /> 8. Wouldn't that also cock-block other supernaturals? Maybe an alternative is making sleeper penalties an area of effect thing (kinda like crossing over to the shadow penalties) so in heavily populated areas they get a paradox dot. So in that case even in a place that is hidden from humans and the other supernaturals can cut loose, the mage still get penalties.<br /> 12. Are you talking to me or the OP because i am not yet asking for help in houseruling mage.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>branford</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137120#post1137120\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">With all due respect, while it appears that you certainly like Mage's crunchy mechanical magic rules (but not its power level), you dislike almost everything else, virtually the entire setting that makes Mage, well.., Mage.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I honestly disagree i dont think those 4 things (The themes, Wisdom mechanic, power level, the seers as written) is &quot;everything else&quot; in mage. Considering there still the orders, abyssal, legacies, alchemy, mage society, ochematas, etc etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>branford</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137120#post1137120\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I cannot imagine that small tweaks to Mage will ever feel satisfying to you or anyone who feels similarly, and the attempt will be an exercise in frustration and anger.</div> </div> </div> </div>As i said to the OP the trick is starting small and tackle each problem on their own. The setting ones are easier. Switching the themes of mage from hubris/abuse of power to the &quot;the 5 orders are sitting on a table, the necronomicon drops, what they do?&quot; AKA &quot;the approach of the orders to magic&quot; and the nature of one soul, how a mage starts generic and slowly acquires their own understanding of their magic forged by orders, yantras and legacies.<br /> <br /> The seers was the easiest, just drop the pretension that people would find them symphathetic and is a kill on sight policy as my players already do.<br /> <br /> Wisdom is trickier before i started with vampire i was working on porting the resonance mechanic from Ascension to Awakening. Which i feel is better fit as it isnt good or bad but instead just makes the mage better at the magic they already do at expenses of other kinds. Issue so far was to see if i keep nimbus or combine them into one (in which player dont get to choose a nimbus but gets assigned one by his actions)<br /> <br /> Power levels is basically an ants jobs. First, caps on arcanums (this chapter max arcanum is 2, next chapter is 3, etc). Then is making sympathetic magic worse than face to face that i do by removing exceptional success from sympathetic casting (casting from afar is its own reward) and making the result of an aiming roll put extra dice (or potency, have to play test that one yet). Another issue is porting permanent paradox rule for those spells i want pcs to cast but not so much. I am thinking of a condition. Like &quot;You wanna go back in time? You can, its gonna be permanent paradox condition&quot;, &quot;want a permanent effect on you?&quot; that doable, but a permanent paradox condition&quot;. Its a matter of finding that sweet spot of &quot;costly but worth it&quot;<br /> And then is just a matter or checking each spell and modify them or ban them.<br /> <br /> But that, for me, will wait until Signs of Sorcery is released. But so far is 3 issues that need work for my houserules to be good. &quot;Permanent paradox condition&quot;, Aiming roll gives potency or extra dice and checking, modifiying and/or banning spells.<br /> <br /> And those of those are porting mechanics from ascension, neither particularly frustrating or anger inducing (except finding the complete resonance rules in a city book for ascension of all places)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-03-2017, 11:05 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T06:40:27-07:00","id":1137276,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MCN</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1136894#post1136894\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, two things. First of all.... the core themes of Mage are The World is a Lie and Addicted to Mysteries. Power and hubris aren't actually listed as themes of mage [snip]</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm sorry, but how isn't &quot;The World is a Lie&quot; connected to Hubris? I mean...<br /> <br /> Amy: &quot;Yup, reality as you know it is just made up of some symbols I figured out after I became *really* obsessed with that other thing, shame you can't see what I'm talking about, but I'm totally right, and everyone else is wrong.&quot;<br /> Billy: &quot;Reality isn't real? Ooookay then.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4854-justin-sane","name":"Justin Sane","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T07:32:48-07:00","id":1137291,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justin Sane</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137276#post1137276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm sorry, but how isn't &quot;The World is a Lie&quot; connected to Hubris? I mean...<br /> <br /> Amy: &quot;Yup, reality as you know it is just made up of some symbols I figured out after I became *really* obsessed with that other thing, shame you can't see what I'm talking about, but I'm totally right, and everyone else is wrong.&quot;<br /> Billy: &quot;Reality isn't real? Ooookay then.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>The problem is that in mage, the mage is objectivly right. Sure being to sure about it in character may be presumptious and hubristic, but the theme the world is a lie is not really hubristic in and of itself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T07:47:12-07:00","id":1137296,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Flinty</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137291#post1137291\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The problem is that in mage, the mage is objectivly right. Sure being to sure about it in character may be presumptious and hubristic, but the theme the world is a lie is not really hubristic in and of itself.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is he [objectively right], tho? I mean, yes, we, as players/storytellers, have access to information about he cosmology of the setting no character could ever hope to get. And yet, characters assert &quot;The World is a Lie&quot; with as much certainty as we do.<br /> <br /> <div style=\"margin: 5px 20px 5px 20px;\"> <div style=\"margin-bottom:2px; font-size: 80%;\"> <input type=\"button\" value=\"Spoiler\" style=\"width:45px;font-size:10px;margin:0px;padding:0px;\" onClick=\"if (this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display != '') { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = ''; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Hide'; } else { this.parentNode.parentNode.getElementsByTagName('div')[1].getElementsByTagName('div')[0].style.display = 'none'; this.innerText = ''; this.value = 'Show'; }\" /> </div> <div style=\" margin: 0px; padding: 6px; border: 3px groove blue; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px; -webkit-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); -moz-box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75); box-shadow: 1px 1px 5px rgba(50, 50, 50, 0.75);\"> <div style=\"display: none;\"><div style=\"background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.8); padding: 6px; -webkit-border-radius: 10px; -moz-border-radius: 10px; border-radius: 10px;\">Also, if they are, and the 10 Arcana can then explain all aspects of reality, how do the Lower Depths fit into that worldview?</div></div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4854-justin-sane","name":"Justin Sane","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"319"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T08:00:22-07:00","id":1137306,"title":"","content":"Lower depths are worlds that are fallen even further than the regular one. They lack something, from an entire arcarna to more specific, narrower concepts. <br /> <br /> And maybe there's one relatively benign one that doesn't have the colour blue. Who knows?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3166-elfive","name":"Elfive","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"8226"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T09:09:15-07:00","id":1137329,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justin Sane</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137296#post1137296\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">. And yet, characters assert &quot;The World is a Lie&quot; with as much certainty as we do.</div> </div> </div> </div>I mean... do they? Can only speak for my personal games, but in them some believed in it more feverently than others. I tend to treat mages as a spectrum of people, some accept the dominant paradigm of the world being a lie, but even with the supernal reinforcing that idea it's still alot to believe. Even a mage who knows its technically true won't realistically start treating everything around him as imaginary figments, at least not immediatly.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1131211-flintys-homebrew-hub-2e\" target=\"_blank\">My Mage 2e Homebrew</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5241-flinty","name":"Flinty","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"793"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T09:37:32-07:00","id":1137338,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Justin Sane</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137276#post1137276\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm sorry, but how isn't &quot;The World is a Lie&quot; connected to Hubris? I mean...</div> </div> </div> </div>I would think the more germane contradiction in the snipped sentence is the entire tail-end of the &quot;Addicted to Mysteries&quot; writeup being about hubris.<br /> <br /> By the same token, the Lie is not &quot;reality isn't real&quot; so much as &quot;reality is bigger than most characters are willing and able to recognize.&quot; The Awakened toolkit gives them access to multiple contexts that just aren't all available to mortals and other monsters, which tends to put weird pressures on their priorities even outside the context of &quot;by the way, several fundamental aspects of the universe don't want the public to know about these things.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T10:29:36-07:00","id":1137358,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137338#post1137338\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would think the more germane contradiction in the snipped sentence is the entire tail-end of the &quot;Addicted to Mysteries&quot; writeup being about hubris.<br /> <br /> By the same token, the Lie is not &quot;reality isn't real&quot; so much as &quot;reality is bigger than most characters are willing and able to recognize.&quot; The Awakened toolkit gives them access to multiple contexts that just aren't all available to mortals and other monsters, which tends to put weird pressures on their priorities even outside the context of &quot;by the way, several fundamental aspects of the universe don't want the public to know about these things.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah. On a day to day level, &quot;The Lie&quot; doesn't really change much about how a Mage goes about their day. They go to the same Starbucks, worry about the same tattooed cultists with magical knives hoping to harvest their spleens to fuel dark rituals, get annoyed by the same ghost pounding on the subway tracks every morning, get annoyed when their netflix subscription auto-renews even though they haven't sent back that DVD of Tango &amp; Cash in two months, etc. What knowing that there's more to reality than the mechanistic world of human perception does on a practical level is just give you more rope to hang yourself with. <br /> <br /> Plus, &quot;The Lie&quot; isn't about the nonexistence or irrelevancy of the material world, it's about the place of humanity in the cosmos. Neither without power, nor singularly important.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/543-professor-phobos","name":"Professor Phobos","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"237"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T11:58:43-07:00","id":1137390,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Professor Phobos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137358#post1137358\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Yeah. On a day to day level, &quot;The Lie&quot; doesn't really change much about how a Mage goes about their day. They go to the same Starbucks, worry about the same tattooed cultists with magical knives hoping to harvest their spleens to fuel dark rituals, get annoyed by the same ghost pounding on the subway tracks every morning, get annoyed when their netflix subscription auto-renews even though they haven't sent back that DVD of Tango &amp; Cash in two months, etc. What knowing that there's more to reality than the mechanistic world of human perception does on a practical level is just give you more rope to hang yourself with.<br /> <br /> Plus, &quot;The Lie&quot; isn't about the nonexistence or irrelevancy of the material world, it's about the place of humanity in the cosmos. Neither without power, nor singularly important.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> While I agree with most of your post, let's be clear about two things:<br /> <br /> 1. Starbucks is obviously a Seer front, and every mochachino you buy Serves the Lie.<br /> <br /> 2. One can master all the Arcana and spend their entire life studying Tango &amp; Cash, and still not reveal all its hidden Wisdom. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":618,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/618-branford","user":"branford","date":". 09-05-2017, 07:27 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/618-branford","name":"branford","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar618_2.gif","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1904"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T14:46:37-07:00","id":1137456,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137097#post1137097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> So, you know I'm firmly a member of the &quot;mages are monsters&quot; camp. But I think that, for some people, the issue is that the ways in which Mages are monsters <i>is ignorable</i>. A noir detective game doesn't give you the tools to play a superhero. Promethean's humanist themes are inseparably integrated into the mechanics. Mage presents mages as monsters in the fiction, and it gives you the tools to play a monstrous mage. But it doesn't do anything to discourage you from ignoring those tools. People read that Mages are supposed to be monsters, then they play the game and don't experience any compulsion towards monstrosity. They are given the option of playing the game the way it is presented in the fiction, but not prevented from playing it in a different way, so they question the fiction. It's an issue of ludonarrative dissonance. This is, unfortunately, a natural consequence of the carrots over sticks design philosophy.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I agree a lot with this. In the end, I don't think that I've had a single player character in our group act &quot;like a Monster&quot; - there is no push, no need, no motivation to do so. Lot of problems can be solved in non-monsterous ways, and both setting and mechanics actually seem to punish monstrous behavior - Wisdom is a thing losing which sucks and can be a &quot;game over&quot; for you if you lose enough of it, and apparently there are mechanisms of the peer pressure and social regulation that actively punish and restrict monstrous behaviors (abusing sleepers, &quot;dangerous magic&quot;, even stuff like unsafely relinquising spells are apparently either straight out illegal, or leading to social ostracism). &quot;Evil&quot; magics - like sacrifices or soul stealing and other stuff - currently seem pointless, because...why bother? You can get same or better results using &quot;legit&quot; means. And you won't have to rebuy Wisdom or explain yourself to your peers! <br /> <br /> I can take a genuinely ok or even good person, and then transform her into a Vampire, and the game will naturally put her into a horror position of becoming a monster, and having to be a monster in order to continue her nightly existence. The very idea of turning a well meaning, &quot;equality for everyone&quot; girl into a Ventrue with major dots in Dominate makes me smile about the struggles to uphold her humanity. The same character in Mage?<br /> Wisdom 7 all the day, without very much to challenge that.Turning that into a horror game requires about as much effort on my part as turning a DND adventure into one, with the note that I don't feel like either game gives me proper tools for that at all, so I'm mostly doing it myself from the scratch.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9102-whw","name":"WHW","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9102_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2542"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T15:09:59-07:00","id":1137466,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137456#post1137456\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> I agree a lot with this. In the end, I don't think that I've had a single player character in our group act &quot;like a Monster&quot; - there is no push, no need, no motivation to do so. Lot of problems can be solved in non-monsterous ways, and both setting and mechanics actually seem to punish monstrous behavior - Wisdom is a thing losing which sucks and can be a &quot;game over&quot; for you if you lose enough of it, and apparently there are mechanisms of the peer pressure and social regulation that actively punish and restrict monstrous behaviors (abusing sleepers, &quot;dangerous magic&quot;, even stuff like unsafely relinquising spells are apparently either straight out illegal, or leading to social ostracism). &quot;Evil&quot; magics - like sacrifices or soul stealing and other stuff - currently seem pointless, because...why bother? You can get same or better results using &quot;legit&quot; means. And you won't have to rebuy Wisdom or explain yourself to your peers!<br /> <br /> I can take a genuinely ok or even good person, and then transform her into a Vampire, and the game will naturally put her into a horror position of becoming a monster, and having to be a monster in order to continue her nightly existence. The very idea of turning a well meaning, &quot;equality for everyone&quot; girl into a Ventrue with major dots in Dominate makes me smile about the struggles to uphold her humanity. The same character in Mage?<br /> Wisdom 7 all the day, without very much to challenge that.Turning that into a horror game requires about as much effort on my part as turning a DND adventure into one, with the note that I don't feel like either game gives me proper tools for that at all, so I'm mostly doing it myself from the scratch.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> There is kind of a discrepancy in what is meant by active and passive monsters. By that I mean, the designation of being &quot;monstrous&quot; can either refer to malicious behavior or a derivative quality of merely existing as a thing that is far beyond the capability of humans.<br /> <br /> Angels are decidedly monstrous, but much of culture understands them to be benevolent. Granted, they're emissaries of the God Machine - but they're perhaps more monstrous because of their capabilities. I believe they're more monstrous than regular human cultists, to some degree.<br /> <br /> It could be argued that it doesn't matter if you're the most benevolent, or neutral, or evil mage. Your designation as mage makes you terrifying to behold because for humans you're an anomaly. From the perspective of those within the Lie, presumably all of us in the real world, mages are exceedingly strange and mysterious. They're monstrous simply by virtue of being, not necessarily acting. They become more monstrous if they're actively making life hell for humans (or whatever is perceiving them.)<br /> <br /> I'm sure, to vampires and werewolves, mages are bizarre and creepy even if they don't mean to be.<br /> <br /> You can be as nice as you want to be, but I know more or less how much your kind can do, and how can I trust you to be benevolent when your kin twist and manipulate things all the time? We don't know how you work or what your goals are as individuals. We've seen plenty of your bad apples. How do we (humans/vampires/werewolves/etc.) know you're not just part of some greater up-fuckery? How do we know you're not just a more organized version of all the horrors that exist in the world of darkness?<br /> <br /> Since the World of Darkness thrives on ambiguity, it's easy to see how mages could warrant caution, distrust, hostility, and so on.<br /> <br /> This, of course, relies on a definition of monstrous as more synonymous with awe-inducing or possibly terrifying.<br /> <br /> I'm sure we have plenty of fables and legends about otherwise benign entities that are more or less designated as monsters simply because they're not like people.<br /> <br /> Heck, I know of plenty of demons, demi-gods, and spirits that walk the line between horror and friendly. Depending on who is telling the story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":11166,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","user":"kryptonitekrusher","date":". 09-04-2017, 07:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T15:39:04-07:00","id":1137482,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>WHW</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137456#post1137456\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I agree a lot with this. In the end, I don't think that I've had a single player character in our group act &quot;like a Monster&quot; - there is no push, no need, no motivation to do so.</div> </div> </div> </div>It is explicitly the job of the Storyteller to put pressure on the player characters and encourage choices that result in dramatic outcomes that lean on their flaws. People being hesitant to make the push that the game calls for them to make is a different problem from that push not existing in the game.<br /> <br /> &quot;The same character in Mage&quot; as a well-meaning Ventrue that specced as far into Dominate as a starting character can manage is a character with three dots in Mind and all their starting Rotes and Praxes devoted to mind control and memory alteration and probably an Obsession relating to social hierarchy in some fashion; the fastest way for her to branch out from a casual ability to violate the sanctity of another character's mind with a thought and a gesture is to gamble with her Wisdom in service to her Virtue, suffer intense failures in spellcasting, take Conditions on exceptional spells, and contain Paradoxes to garner Arcane Beats with which to broaden the scope of her specialties. She can certainly coast her way to that point for most things that aren't Wisdom dots or extra Praxes, but her more driven contemporaries will outpace her quite rapidly if she rests on her laurels in a notionally meritocratic society like the average Caucus or Consilium.<br /> <br /> The world exists outside the characters, and the nature of Awakening inclines characters to want to keep up with at least some parts of it; the mechanics alone cannot provide all of that drive without turning it from a drive to an inarguable and objective need, at which point the game would be more about being A Kind Of Monster than it would be about being a Mage with a view of the cutthroat world of the enthralling supernatural.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T17:57:07-07:00","id":1137527,"title":"","content":"Monstrous bodies are monstrous because they transgress, they break laws and taboos. They are enticing because they speak to our hidden desires, and at the same time show what is acceptable or not, a sort of 'border control' at the limit of the cultural body we are part of.<br /> Let's map that to Mages, shall we?<br /> Playing with the foundations of reality? Well, people go all kinds of apeshit if you want to clone other people, so let's just assume this is a taboo, shall we?<br /> Hidden Desires? Hell, who would not like to be able to call down lightning, teleport, read minds and heal people?<br /> Is it acceptable to wipe people's minds, make a battery out of their soul - treat them like things to use? Is it acceptable to look down upon a whole class of people who were less fortunate than you?<br /> <br /> And these are harmless, as far as mage games go. Mages are without a doubt monstrous. They are, to me, also horrific monsters because crazy things are so easy for them. They are set apart by their abilities and knowledge.<br /> <br /> I can take a genuinely good person, make them a mage, and they will go <i>down the slippery slope like nobody's business</i>, because you know what? &quot;The road to hell is paved with good intentions.&quot;A good person would want to enact good change. Try to help people. Try to improve the world. After all, all this power needs to be put to good use, right?<br /> Mage's can't have good things. Trust me on this - someone or something will fuck it up. If not my Cabal or myself, it's gonna be the ST. That's your job as ST. Make the characters suffer. (exaggerated, but you know what I mean.)<br /> <br /> This is similar to people misunderstanding what the horror in Cosmic Horror is. It is NOT tentacles. Those are just obvious horror/disgust.<br /> It is the sensation (not knowledge) of an uncaring universe in which nothing has meaning, and nothing lasts.You need to be in a special headspace for that, no one denies that.<br /> <br /> The former horror is easy. The latter is far harder to pull off.<br /> Guess which one keeps me awake at night?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T18:54:58-07:00","id":1137544,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wormwood</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137527#post1137527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Monstrous bodies are monstrous because they transgress, they break laws and taboos. They are enticing because they speak to our hidden desires, and at the same time show what is acceptable or not, a sort of 'border control' at the limit of the cultural body we are part of.<br /> Let's map that to Mages, shall we?<br /> Playing with the foundations of reality? Well, people go all kinds of apeshit if you want to clone other people, so let's just assume this is a taboo, shall we?<br /> Hidden Desires? Hell, who would not like to be able to call down lightning, teleport, read minds and heal people?<br /> Is it acceptable to wipe people's minds, make a battery out of their soul - treat them like things to use? Is it acceptable to look down upon a whole class of people who were less fortunate than you?<br /> <br /> And these are harmless, as far as mage games go. Mages are without a doubt monstrous. They are, to me, also horrific monsters because crazy things are so easy for them. They are set apart by their abilities and knowledge.<br /> <br /> I can take a genuinely good person, make them a mage, and they will go <i>down the slippery slope like nobody's business</i>, because you know what? &quot;The road to hell is paved with good intentions.&quot;A good person would want to enact good change. Try to help people. Try to improve the world. After all, all this power needs to be put to good use, right?<br /> Mage's can't have good things. Trust me on this - someone or something will fuck it up. If not my Cabal or myself, it's gonna be the ST. That's your job as ST. Make the characters suffer. (exaggerated, but you know what I mean.)<br /> <br /> This is similar to people misunderstanding what the horror in Cosmic Horror is. It is NOT tentacles. Those are just obvious horror/disgust.<br /> It is the sensation (not knowledge) of an uncaring universe in which nothing has meaning, and nothing lasts.You need to be in a special headspace for that, no one denies that.<br /> <br /> The former horror is easy. The latter is far harder to pull off.<br /> Guess which one keeps me awake at night?</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> I'm kind of wondering why STs aren't producing more tough choices. By reading some of the scenarios of being able to stay relatively undisturbed or changed by the world of darkness at large, you would think the game doesn't cover the basis of dramatic storytelling, scenarios without easy answers, and all types of modifiers and conditions or supernatural abilities to force bad things to unfold. I'm just now re-reading Chronicles/Mage 2e and just picked up Night Horrors. End up seeing a lot of repetition about tough choices, drama, horror/despair, unclean world, boogeymen in the night and just a real sensation of ugly disturbing shit festering around every corner.<br /> <br /> Really strange.<br /> <br /> I was sitting here thinking being good and benevolent was some sort of achievement in spite of all the nightmares unfolding around you. Yet I'm hearing it's actually pretty easy.<br /> <br /> I am taking note, though. I don't want my players to think it's too easy to be good.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T19:23:43-07:00","id":1137551,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wormwood</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137527#post1137527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I can take a genuinely good person, make them a mage, and they will go <i>down the slippery slope like nobody's business</i>, because you know what? &quot;The road to hell is paved with good intentions.&quot;A good person would want to enact good change. Try to help people. Try to improve the world. After all, all this power needs to be put to good use, right?<br /> Mage's can't have good things. Trust me on this - someone or something will fuck it up. If not my Cabal or myself, it's gonna be the ST. That's your job as ST. Make the characters suffer. (exaggerated, but you know what I mean.)</div> </div> </div> </div>Playing the monkey paw/adversarial DM aside, its not an absolute. Implying that the needs of this theoretical &quot;good person&quot; to help people will over-ride his better judgement, instead of being a &quot;I will help people as long as i dont cross X line&quot; (like batmans, i wont kill the joker no matter how much that would improve the world) or even meddling with peoples lives with magic could be a moral issue in itself. When i play mages they are usually slackers that dont care about improving the world with magic as magic is not for meddling with human society, nor gaining power (beyond the slow accumulation of xp) kind of a zero sum for the world (they dont make it shittier nor better).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Wormwood</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137527#post1137527\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">This is similar to people misunderstanding what the horror in Cosmic Horror is. It is NOT tentacles. Those are just obvious horror/disgust.<br /> It is the sensation (not knowledge) of an uncaring universe in which nothing has meaning, and nothing lasts.You need to be in a special headspace for that, no one denies that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Its a matter of taste, is my personal belief in all of that but i dont find it horrific in anyway. Just the &quot;is how the world work, nothing to it&quot;.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137544#post1137544\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm kind of wondering why STs aren't producing more tough choices.</div> </div> </div> </div>Depends on the DM style i suppose, some people dont like to force a tough choice in everysingle scenario. Others want to put some &quot;no matter what i do, you loose something&quot; while others go with the &quot;if you play you cards right there is a happy ending&quot;.<br /> <br /> And those are the extremes, and there is a whole range from those extremes. Personally I prefer the &quot;there are intended consequences for things you do but they must follow a logical path to it, I wont play monkey paw DM. And while difficult, there is a perfect-ish ending if you can find it&quot;<br /> <br /> However i dont run global &quot;lets change society game&quot;, the setting will remain the setting it is and nothing the PC do will change it (meaning you can make local changes but anything beyond that is a no) so when you introduce mage meddling with politics or big time reality stuff i guess that you gotta have more and more unintended consequences.<br /> <br /> EDIT:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Charlaquin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137097#post1137097\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, you know I'm firmly a member of the &quot;mages are monsters&quot; camp. But I think that, for some people, the issue is that the ways in which Mages are monsters <i>is ignorable</i>. A noir detective game doesn't give you the tools to play a superhero. Promethean's humanist themes are inseparably integrated into the mechanics. Mage presents mages as monsters in the fiction, and it gives you the tools to play a monstrous mage. But it doesn't do anything to discourage you from ignoring those tools. People read that Mages are supposed to be monsters, then they play the game and don't experience any compulsion towards monstrosity. They are given the option of playing the game the way it is presented in the fiction, but not prevented from playing it in a different way, so they question the fiction. It's an issue of ludonarrative dissonance. This is, unfortunately, a natural consequence of the carrots over sticks design philosophy.</div> </div> </div> </div>This point + a style of GMing which isnt about finding unintended consequences to most of the PCs action and/or no engineering though choices or no-total-win scenarios might result in not happening unless the Players go looking for them.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-04-2017, 10:41 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T20:00:43-07:00","id":1137561,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137551#post1137551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Depends on the DM style i suppose, some people dont like to force a tough choice in everysingle scenario. Others want to put some &quot;no matter what i do, you loose something&quot; while others go with the &quot;if you play you cards right there is a happy ending&quot;.<br /> <br /> And those are the extremes, and there is a whole range from those extremes. Personally I prefer the &quot;there are intended consequences for things you do but they must follow a logical path to it, I wont play monkey paw DM. And while difficult, there is a perfect-ish ending if you can find it&quot;<br /> <br /> However i dont run global &quot;lets change society game&quot;, the setting will remain the setting it is and nothing the PC do will change it (meaning you can make local changes but anything beyond that is a no) so when you introduce mage meddling with politics or big time reality stuff i guess that you gotta have more and more unintended consequences.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes, but the book emphasizes tough choices for the sake of dramatic storytelling. It even rewards failure. So even when you lose, you're not really losing, unless you're dead. You're still telling a story about a character that could have, on paper (or as far as probability and dice rolls are concerned), won - but lost.<br /> <br /> Just briefly looking through the list of breaking point stuff, all the supernatural abilities and entities (known or crafted with special rules), there are so many ways to shove a character down the rabbit hole.<br /> <br /> You're repeatedly told to avoid easy answers and being straight forward.<br /> <br /> You're not really playing &quot;Monkey Paw&quot; DM, since you're not controlling how they play, you're controlling the scenarios they play in - which is what you're supposed to do anyway. You design the world, the inhabitants, the challenges, the mysteries. You propose the choices. You're not telling them how to choose. You're just presenting the scenarios and letting them approach - or in some instances you're presenting some forms of pressure and constraints by way of time, resources, or certain ultimatums.<br /> <br /> Monkey Paw DM sounds more like railroading people or even playing characters for people. Telling them &quot;No, you can't do that because we're gonna do this instead.&quot; When the thing they want to do is a perfectly viable freewill option.<br /> <br /> The point I'm trying to make is that even Mage emphasizes tough choices. If you aren't happy with producing tough choices that don't have clear cut &quot;This is the good ending&quot; &quot;This is the bad ending&quot; scenarios, then how is it Mage's fault for not emphasizing horror and characters doing intentional or unintentionally horrible things?<br /> <br /> You can also sit there and say &quot;Well, I don't believe it was horrible, it was perfectly rational how I handled that situation and everything is fine. Moving on with the story.&quot; But that doesn't mean your character won't be perceived by other PCs or NPCs as tactless, inexperienced, inept, foolish, apathetic, deranged, etc.<br /> <br /> <br /> Where are the villains that tell you, &quot;Either you let me rip your spine out and eat your soul, or your family gets a vivisection in the next minute.&quot;<br /> <br /> Where are the scenarios where you need to help a ghost pass on, but their only desire is to torture and kill their husband's mistress?<br /> <br /> Where is the plight to save a group of children from some terrible monster in the woods, only to find out they're being used as batteries and a tool to recruit more children. Furthermore, separation from the monster will cause the immediate death of the child. Also, the entity proposes a trade - one child, unharmed, per subdued adult you bring to it (because it wants more able-bodied servants.)<br /> <br /> Of course the situations themselves can be tweaked for the group in question, but it shouldn't always be easy to discern what the &quot;optimal choice&quot; is. Or, the best choice for your character may be immediately apparent, but the consequences can play at many different themes and elements pertaining to how the character sees the world or expected things to unfold.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T20:12:28-07:00","id":1137564,"title":"","content":"One example from D&amp;D, which admittedly annoyed me because I wasn't expecting it to be so grim - as I didn't really go in with that understanding.<br /> <br /> I was playing a heroic archer. He wanted to be good and do good things. Charitable, friendly, charismatic.<br /> <br /> He even opted to resolve things with non-lethal combat or diplomacy.<br /> <br /> He did that for a group of bandits and even established them as the defense force of the town - set up a method of getting them training and employment via the mines they recently cleared of goblins.<br /> <br /> The bandits agreed to it and so my party went off to clear another area two days from town.<br /> <br /> When we came back a bunch of villagers were dead because the bandits couldn't shake their old ways and didn't particularly care to work when they could just steal.<br /> <br /> <br /> This kind of shit happened numerous times during the adventure and ultimately my character retired an apathetic dragon-slayer who became leader of a mercenary group.<br /> <br /> <br /> I thought I was making good and right choices. I thought I was being heroic. The world played out differently and those choices did not resonate as heroic.<br /> <br /> <br /> That isn't to say I couldn't have made it as a heroic archer! But it would have been pretty difficult to maintain that energy given the unforgiving world. Basically, making the whole &quot;heroic archer&quot; thing all the more heroic for those that can manage it.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T20:29:57-07:00","id":1137570,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137561#post1137561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You're not really playing &quot;Monkey Paw&quot; DM, since you're not controlling how they play, you're controlling the scenarios they play in - which is what you're supposed to do anyway. You design the world, the inhabitants, the challenges, the mysteries. You propose the choices. You're not telling them how to choose. You're just presenting the scenarios and letting them approach - or in some instances you're presenting some forms of pressure and constraints by way of time, resources, or certain ultimatums.<br /> <br /> Monkey Paw DM sounds more like railroading people or even playing characters for people. Telling them &quot;No, you can't do that because we're gonna do this instead.&quot; When the thing they want to do is a perfectly viable freewill option.</div> </div> </div> </div>Thats not what Monkey paw Dming is, Monkey paw dming is more tied to unintended consequences of even benign actions for the sake of &quot;drama&quot; that from a certain POV might strain credibility. Is the old meme of &quot;no matter what you do in call of Cthulhu, your character will end up either dead or insane&quot;, warhammer 40k &quot;you cant change the setting for the better no matter what you do&quot; or &quot;Mage's can't have good things. Trust me on this - someone or something will fuck it up&quot;<br /> <br /> Or if we wanna go more topical example, on season 6 of Game of thrones when Tyrion...........<br /> <br /> <br /> *********SPOILERS ALERT JUST IN CASE***********<br /> <br /> has to keep the peace in Meereen. All his ideas and plan are very logical and should have worked but they failed just because (the narrative answer is that they needed to fail to make Daenerys return seem more dramatic). Thats monkey paw dming, twisting the logic just to create drama.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137561#post1137561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The point I'm trying to make is that even Mage emphasizes tough choices. If you aren't happy with producing tough choices that don't have clear cut &quot;This is the good ending&quot; &quot;This is the bad ending&quot; scenarios, then how is it Mage's fault for not emphasizing horror and characters doing intentional or unintentionally horrible things?</div> </div> </div> </div>I would say the flaw falls on relying on a specific dming style for the game. However there are such niche game in the market today like FIASCO that also relies on specific DM styles to work with. However maybe because WOD is more &quot;mainstream&quot; feels odd that one of the 3 main lines (mage, werewolf, vampire) requires such niche dming style to work.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137561#post1137561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You can also sit there and say &quot;Well, I don't believe it was horrible, it was perfectly rational how I handled that situation and everything is fine. Moving on with the story.&quot; But that doesn't mean your character won't be perceived by other PCs or NPCs as tactless, inexperienced, inept, foolish, apathetic, deranged, etc.</div> </div> </div> </div>When i said that? Or said thats a problem with mage? I never said that mage society/setting is <span style=\"color:#545454\"><span style=\"font-family:arial\">desensitized </span></span>to characters action, or are you referring to another poster?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137561#post1137561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where are the villains that tell you, &quot;Either you let me rip your spine out and eat your soul, or your family gets a vivisection in the next minute.&quot;.</div> </div> </div> </div>And you kill said villain. If you are using the Wisdom mechanic you inure yourself to killing people. And call it a day? While there is grey cases of killing is wrong, thats seems a pretty much black and white scenario.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137561#post1137561\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Where are the scenarios where you need to help a ghost pass on, but their only desire is to torture and kill their husband's mistress?</div> </div> </div> </div>Now this one i can work with. This is a difference on DMing style, i dont subscribe to those kind of scenarios. Allowing the ghost to torture that way is a character defining moment and i would never say &quot;do the morally disturbing option or you loose&quot;. The PC can just destroy the anchor and the ghost is lay to rest, at best i would make the &quot;allowing the ghost to torture&quot; as the lazy option with finding the anchor the &quot;more work&quot; option.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137564#post1137564\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> A whole D&amp;D story<br /> <br /> That isn't to say I couldn't have made it as a heroic archer! But it would have been pretty difficult to maintain that energy given the unforgiving world. Basically, making the whole &quot;heroic archer&quot; thing all the more heroic for those that can manage it.</div> </div> </div> </div>Two things. First, was that a published adventure? Because i swear to god played the same situation when my group was playtesting D&amp;D next. Was the village a cheese making one? The exception was that we got a 20 on the dice and the thieves didnt kill the villages and everything work as intended. Though we did set up a some safeguard against the thieves.<br /> <br /> Secondly again thats DMing style, i dont subscribe that kind of style. However my style and is not objectively better than others that are different.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 09-04-2017, 11:53 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T20:52:58-07:00","id":1137577,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137570#post1137570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thats not what Monkey paw Dming is, Monkey paw dming is more tied to unintended consequences of even benign actions for the sake of &quot;drama&quot; that from a certain POV might strain credibility.</div> </div> </div> </div>Not having all the problems on a character's plate form an orderly line and wait to get worse from inattention is frequently all that you need to put pressure on them to consider the more expedient route \u2014 these discussions have brought up the general arc of Dresden's problems being a lack of infinite prep and recovery time before and there's a reason for that.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-04T21:02:50-07:00","id":1137585,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137577#post1137577\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Not having all the problems on a character's plate form an orderly line and wait to get worse from inattention is frequently all that you need to put pressure on them to consider the more expedient route \u2014 these discussions have brought up the general arc of Dresden's problems being a lack of infinite prep and recovery time before and there's a reason for that.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is a issue of time management and distances. How to handle time in a game is an issue to deal with most dming, i have run and seen dms run into player complains that they &quot;shouldnt have loose that much time doing that one scene&quot; or if doing lots of prep stuff (that doesn't take that much time) between scenes. Coupled with Chrods laughable recovery time for bashing damage and fatigue. Finding that sweet spot between &quot;the characters dont do everything in one day&quot; and &quot;the players dont feel cheated on the passage of time&quot;.<br /> <br /> However i didnt think about that issue until you mentioned however and thinking about it, it could be handle with a condition (until the mage remove their need to rest from themselves, i guess or you are playing werewolf)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T01:56:57-07:00","id":1137626,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137570#post1137570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now this one i can work with. This is a difference on DMing style, i dont subscribe to those kind of scenarios. Allowing the ghost to torture that way is a character defining moment and i would never say &quot;do the morally disturbing option or you loose&quot;. The PC can just destroy the anchor and the ghost is lay to rest, at best i would make the &quot;allowing the ghost to torture&quot; as the lazy option with finding the anchor the &quot;more work&quot; option.</div> </div> </div> </div>If the Ghost Anchor is the very mistress you're proper screwed.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12191-malus","name":"Malus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12191_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1562"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T03:53:35-07:00","id":1137641,"title":"","content":"Having unintended consequences doesn't have to be an adversarial role the ST takes. In my last session the Obrimos decided to move a ley line in the hopes of jump starting a conveniently located but dormant Hallow. He was informed when he did it that he'd be changing the metaphysical landscape for the next year as a result of that action. He was cool with it. He made an informed decision, and now if anything happens as a result of it then it won't be because the ST is being a jerk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T07:37:48-07:00","id":1137669,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137551#post1137551\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Playing the monkey paw/adversarial DM aside, its not an absolute. Implying that the needs of this theoretical &quot;good person&quot; to help people will over-ride his better judgement, instead of being a &quot;I will help people as long as i dont cross X line&quot; (like batmans, i wont kill the joker no matter how much that would improve the world) or even meddling with peoples lives with magic could be a moral issue in itself. When i play mages they are usually slackers that dont care about improving the world with magic as magic is not for meddling with human society, nor gaining power (beyond the slow accumulation of xp) kind of a zero sum for the world (they dont make it shittier nor better).<br /> <br /> Its a matter of taste, is my personal belief in all of that but i dont find it horrific in anyway. Just the &quot;is how the world work, nothing to it&quot;.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div>I never said you have to go adversial. Ever tried helping people and do the right thing? Soon enough you will end up in a conflict of interests. Look at Ned Stark or Jon Snow to see how that can go. Even Batman cannot be considered a healthy hero - he is essentially just doing his very own temper tantrum therapy. Also, the debate whether Batman is responsible for all the people the joker killed after his umpteenth time escaping the prison has been done many, many times. Becoming Batman is one way of doing good - Daredevil is another, and Punisher yet another. Hell, even the Kingpin tried to be good. Good people tend to start to juggle too many balls at once at some point, and will have to make a harsh decision at some point to keep juggling. If those decisions keep coming, the character will have problems.<br /> And I never meant to say that good mages HAVE to go down the slippery slope, just that they are no more save from it than vampires.<br /> <br /> Knowledge =/= Sensation. Most of the time, my insignificance and the coming heat death of the universe do not bother me. However, sometimes, thanks to the right story or experience triggering it, I experience an extreme sense of horror about it. It is a special headspace.<br /> <br /> <br /> Regarding the missapliance of my &quot;Mages can't have good things&quot; quote: that is personal experience, not a general rule. Hence &quot;trust me on this&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8542-wormwood","name":"Wormwood","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8542_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1198"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T08:12:33-07:00","id":1137678,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137641#post1137641\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Having unintended consequences doesn't have to be an adversarial role the ST takes. In my last session the Obrimos decided to move a ley line in the hopes of jump starting a conveniently located but dormant Hallow. He was informed when he did it that he'd be changing the metaphysical landscape for the next year as a result of that action. He was cool with it. He made an informed decision, and now if anything happens as a result of it then it won't be because the ST is being a jerk.</div> </div> </div> </div>But thats why i said &quot;This point + a style of GMing which isnt about finding unintended consequences to most of the PCs action and/or no engineering though choices or no-total-win scenarios might result in not happening <b><u>unless the Players go looking for them</u></b>.&quot; <br /> <br /> If i said to the player, &quot;hey, you gonna mess things up if you do A. And they still do A.&quot; then thats not monkey paw, that just the player making a descision for his character.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T08:28:04-07:00","id":1137685,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137678#post1137678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">But thats why i said &amp;quot;This point + a style of GMing which isnt about finding unintended consequences to most of the PCs action and/or no engineering though choices or no-total-win scenarios might result in not happening <b><u>unless the Players go looking for them</u></b>.&amp;quot; <br /> <br /> If i said to the player, &amp;quot;hey, you gonna mess things up if you do A. And they still do A.&amp;quot; then thats not monkey paw, that just the player making a descision for his character.</div> </div> </div> </div>My bad, you seemed to be arguing that unintended consequences of a pc's use of magic is a sign of a bad ST.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T08:52:14-07:00","id":1137690,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137678#post1137678\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> But thats why i said &quot;This point + a style of GMing which isnt about finding unintended consequences to most of the PCs action and/or no engineering though choices or no-total-win scenarios might result in not happening <b><u>unless the Players go looking for them</u></b>.&quot;<br /> <br /> If i said to the player, &quot;hey, you gonna mess things up if you do A. And they still do A.&quot; then thats not monkey paw, that just the player making a descision for his character.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> How isn't that making things too easy for them, though? You're literally letting them avoid horror by telling them, more or less, that it can be avoided - in a game about horror. World of Darkness games aren't &quot;You live in an okay world with some bad stuff on the side.&quot; It's more like &quot;You thought you lived in an okay world, but really it's a crap-sack.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's not really adversarial if the basic premise of playing Chronicles of Darkness (WoD games) is gritty and unforgiving reality. It isn't Disney's Halloweentown, where you could be spooked if you feel like it. This doesn't mean EVERY decision should wind up in horrible despair and tough choices, but the ST should be pressing the drama from time to time and making it hard to see how you benefit from the situation.<br /> <br /> In the case I gave you earlier: &quot;<i>Either you let me rip your spine out and eat your soul, or your family gets a vivisection in the next minute.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> I forgot to mention the choice isn't between killing or not killing the enemy. It is about KILLING the enemy, AND LOSING your family. Or letting the enemy kill you. You would have to imagine an enemy that has managed to separate you and your family from each other - and confronted you in a separate location. People can be crazy. Monsters can be unyielding and brutal.<br /> <br /> Either you let the enemy kill you and hopefully they honor their promise, or you kill them - knowing your family probably just died.<br /> <br /> It doesn't even have to be about your personal choices. It could be your reaction to the stupid shit people do around you.<br /> <br /> Another example? A person you've been pursuing for morally reprehensible crimes agrees to help you with something by giving you extremely pertinent information or helping you with a task that would let you deal with another more pressing matter - in exchange, they want you to vouch for them when their punishment is being decided so they don't suffer as badly. Let's say they can help you remove a deadly condition from someone you care about.<br /> <br /> Problem? One of your NPCs, or even one of your players, has suffered loss at the hands of this person you just made a deal with. Somehow, they end up killing your only lead before you get anything out of it.<br /> <br /> How do you respond? Is your character just going to eat this loss without any difficulty?<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> Also, regarding the D&amp;D thing: I think it was one of their pre-built scenarios. My DM has tons of D&amp;D books from various editions, so I assume he'd put some of it to use. We were playing 5e and I can't recall if it was a village of cheese-makers. I just recall heading back to the village and being told by a splintered group of the bandits that the ones responsible for the killings were at a certain location. I, as acting leader with previous experience with those particular NPCs took their word for it, so we planned a huge attack with explosives and everything. We obliterated the troublemakers, only to find out the bandits that splintered off were the murderers and just wanted us to slaughter the nay-sayers.<br /> <br /> This was also followed by certain situations where I made decisions that just worked out in my favor. Like the classic &quot;this village is possessed by a demon&quot; and I happened to identify and shoot the demon in the face, knowing full well that I could be killed instantly for choosing the wrong person.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T10:51:39-07:00","id":1137758,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137690#post1137690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">In the case I gave you earlier: &quot;<i>Either you let me rip your spine out and eat your soul, or your family gets a vivisection in the next minute.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> I forgot to mention the choice isn't between killing or not killing the enemy. It is about KILLING the enemy, AND LOSING your family. Or letting the enemy kill you. You would have to imagine an enemy that has managed to separate you and your family from each other - and confronted you in a separate location. People can be crazy. Monsters can be unyielding and brutal.<br /> <br /> Either you let the enemy kill you and hopefully they honor their promise, or you kill them - knowing your family probably just died.</div> </div> </div> </div>Again different Dming styles, i would never run an scenario like that one. Unless is really REALLY something that had tons of build up and the players had miss all the chances to avoid the situation in one way or another.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137690#post1137690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It doesn't even have to be about your personal choices. It could be your reaction to the stupid shit people do around you.<br /> <br /> Another example? A person you've been pursuing for morally reprehensible crimes agrees to help you with something by giving you extremely pertinent information or helping you with a task that would let you deal with another more pressing matter - in exchange, they want you to vouch for them when their punishment is being decided so they don't suffer as badly. Let's say they can help you remove a deadly condition from someone you care about.<br /> <br /> Problem? One of your NPCs, or even one of your players, has suffered loss at the hands of this person you just made a deal with. Somehow, they end up killing your only lead before you get anything out of it.<br /> <br /> How do you respond? Is your character just going to eat this loss without any difficulty?</div> </div> </div> </div>In the case of the NPC, i would remind the player of the history between them (the NPCS) to see if he gonna think about devicing a countermeasure against the NPC. In case of PVP there is nothing i, as a DM, can or should do.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>kryptonitekrusher</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137690#post1137690\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">How isn't that making things too easy for them, though? You're literally letting them avoid horror by telling them, more or less, that it can be avoided - in a game about horror. World of Darkness games aren't &quot;You live in an okay world with some bad stuff on the side.&quot; It's more like &quot;You thought you lived in an okay world, but really it's a crap-sack.&quot;<br /> <br /> It's not really adversarial if the basic premise of playing Chronicles of Darkness (WoD games) is gritty and unforgiving reality. It isn't Disney's Halloweentown, where you could be spooked if you feel like it. This doesn't mean EVERY decision should wind up in horrible despair and tough choices, but the ST should be pressing the drama from time to time and making it hard to see how you benefit from the situation.</div> </div> </div> </div>Is a thin line difficult to express it without example so i am gonna use one i happen to experience in a VtM game. For reason i dont remember now (whether as a favor for an NPC or to weaken a coterie rival) my character had to kill the chief of police. So he followed on his car on the rocky roads near the forest (it was boston or vermont), no cars around. My PC put his car forward and pull the brakes slowly making the chief\u00b4s of police dent the back of his car. Both men stopped to exchange insurrance and see the damage but when the chief of police got out my character summoned a spirit bear and kill him.<br /> <br /> Now my DM subscribe to the same phylosophy that i do so he ask himself &quot;is there a logical way someone figure out foul play? Middle of nowhere, zone known to for having bears so thats a no&quot; The thing was done and it never was brought up again.<br /> <br /> Other DMing very trigger happy with consequences of action would have done &quot;oh you didnt say you check for traffic cams so you got on camera.....and now is a masquerade breach and the owner of the chief of police is gunning for you!&quot; or &quot;the owner of the chief of police hires the tremere/giovanni to cast a ritual to speak with the ghost/spirits to find out the if there was foul play&quot; or the old &quot;a nosferatu was following the chief or you and now they know and gonna black mail you&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T11:04:01-07:00","id":1137764,"title":"","content":"I would say neither of those reactions to your actions are inappropriate. The second one could be problematic if the ST is just gunning for a &quot;Gotcha!&quot; moment, but if handled well it just adds to the story.<br /> <br /> Edit: Come to think of it the first response might also be bad if the ST basically ignores a players attempts to make significant changes to the setting.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2632,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","user":"Mrmdubois","date":". 09-05-2017, 02:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T15:08:46-07:00","id":1137839,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137764#post1137764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I would say neither of those reactions to your actions are inappropriate. The second one could be problematic if the ST is just gunning for a &quot;Gotcha!&quot; moment, but if handled well it just adds to the story.</div> </div> </div> </div>YMMV, i find the later examples entirely forced. Makes me, as player, feel that i cant do anything without having long unintended derails of plot even when i plan for stuff (say, finding a desolated spot, make it look like a natural accident, etc)<br /> <br /> Gotcha moments is a good way to put it, Monkey paw Dming is basically turning most of the character actions into Gotcha! moments.<br /> <br /> Which seems something mage is counting on to function to a higher degree than others. As another poster put it more eloquently that as the monstrosity of mage is informed by us, we are given the tools to go for it but nothing stops us from not doing so beyond the DM making lots of &quot;gotcha moment!&quot;<br /> <br /> It is a flaw? In my opinion yes, because it relies on a particular style of Dming. However i wont say that people wont like it or that they like a flawed game because maybe it was entirely intentional. And this not even the first time a particular style of gaming is favored over the rest, as this also happens with Aspirations.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Mrmdubois</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137764#post1137764\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Edit: Come to think of it the first response might also be bad if the ST basically ignores a players attempts to make significant changes to the setting.</div> </div> </div> </div>The setting remains changed (no more police chief) but the player got away with it without consequences.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T15:14:14-07:00","id":1137842,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Malus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137626#post1137626\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If the Ghost Anchor is the very mistress you're proper screwed.</div> </div> </div> </div>I think you misunderstand &quot;destroy&quot; Anchor. Anchor is a Condition on a noun (hypothetically on the mistress in this case) and the Condition can be destroyed without harming the mistress at all. That's 2nd Ed for ya.<br /> <br /> I was thinking about my suggestion about making each Mana spent on Spell cause automatic successes for Paradox on top of normal Paradox rules for fostering Lovecraftian motifs (Unpredictable dangerous magic/&quot;science&quot; wielded by maniacs arrogant enough to meddle with forces beyond human fathoming), it also fosters Path specialization because all improvised magic outside of your Ruling Arcana are now sources of guaranteed Paradox, making Path and Legacy even more significant in defining. It also acts as a relative buff to Legacies that grant a third Ruling Arcanum (Relative because the third Ruling Arcanum provides some relief from the Hack). It also would mean that the relative mastery between having a Ruling Arcanum and not is way more significant than merely resource savings, it means having safe predictable spells only within your sphere unless you collect Rotes like crazy, which isn't encouraged enough in a setting that seems like it should be lore hungry. It also make Grimoires very important.<br /> <br /> I like role specialization, more important Grimoires, meaningful commitments to who the character fundamentally is at creation, and people needing to lean upon each other to cover each other's weaknesses, and it all comes with the bonus of making 2/3 or more of Magic less sane and safe for most Mages. Also 1/5 to almost 1/3 of all Magic if you have a Legacy is not a bad slice of power potential at the drop of a hat and still makes them at least on part with other Major Templates. Also since particularly potent effects frequently require Mana it makes their use both tempting and frightening is a way that makes Monkey Paw a part of the mechanics not just a Storytelling style.<br /> <br /> Also since this would be applied to all mages it doesn't affect the balance between mages except to make Scelesti terrifying.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T15:18:49-07:00","id":1137846,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137842#post1137842\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I was thinking about my suggestion about making each Mana spent on Spell cause automatic successes for Paradox on top of normal Paradox rules for fostering Lovecraftian motifs (Unpredictable dangerous magic/&quot;science&quot; wielded by maniacs arrogant enough to meddle with forces beyond human fathoming), it also fosters Path specialization because all improvised magic outside of your Ruling Arcana are now sources of guaranteed Paradox, making Path and Legacy even more significant in defining. It also acts as a relative buff to Legacies that grant a third Ruling Arcanum (Relative because the third Ruling Arcanum provides some relief from the Hack). It also would mean that the relative mastery between having a Ruling Arcanum and not is way more significant than merely resource savings, it means having safe predictable spells only within your sphere unless you collect Rotes like crazy, which isn't encouraged enough in a setting that seems like it should be lore hungry. It also make Grimoires very important.<br /> <br /> I like role specialization, more important Grimoires, meaningful commitments to who the character fundamentally is at creation, and people needing to lean upon each other to cover each other's weaknesses, and it all comes with the bonus of making 2/3 or more of Magic less sane and safe for most Mages. Also 1/5 to almost 1/3 of all Magic if you have a Legacy is not a bad slice of power potential at the drop of a hat and still makes them at least on part with other Major Templates. Also since particularly potent effects frequently require Mana it makes their use both tempting and frightening is a way that makes Monkey Paw a part of the mechanics not just a Storytelling style.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh my god i love this.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T15:22:41-07:00","id":1137850,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137839#post1137839\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> YMMV, i find the later examples entirely forced. Makes me, as player, feel that i cant do anything without having long unintended derails of plot even when i plan for stuff (say, finding a desolated spot, make it look like a natural accident, etc)<br /> <br /> Gotcha moments is a good way to put it, Monkey paw Dming is basically turning most of the character actions into Gotcha! moments.<br /> <br /> Which seems something mage is counting on to function to a higher degree than others. As another poster put it more eloquently that as the monstrosity of mage is informed by us, we are given the tools to go for it but nothing stops us from not doing so beyond the DM making lots of &amp;quot;gotcha moment!&amp;quot;<br /> <br /> It is a flaw? In my opinion yes, because it relies on a particular style of Dming. However i wont say that people wont like it or that they like a flawed game because maybe it was entirely intentional. And this not even the first time a particular style of gaming is favored over the rest, as this also happens with Aspirations.<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> The setting remains changed (no more police chief) but the player got away with it without consequences.</div> </div> </div> </div>Like I said, it's a matter of how it's handled, but either way of handling the situation can be appropriate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2632-mrmdubois","name":"Mrmdubois","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6827"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-05T16:56:32-07:00","id":1137868,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> YMMV, i find the later examples entirely forced. Makes me, as player, feel that i cant do anything without having long unintended derails of plot even when i plan for stuff (say, finding a desolated spot, make it look like a natural accident, etc)</div> </div> </div> </div>That's unfortunate, but in real-life and in games that parody real life, sometimes you do feel helpless. Sometimes natural accidents do happen. Again, I'm not seeing how it is adversarial or controlling if you're playing a game where the books all tell you over and over again that the world is a lot more fucked up than you thought.<br /> <br /> This doesn't mean that every decision you make ends up playing out this way, but it seems like the game almost necessitate a balance of both. They're both elements of dramatic storytelling.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">Which seems something mage is counting on to function to a higher degree than others. As another poster put it more eloquently that as the monstrosity of mage is informed by us, we are given the tools to go for it but nothing stops us from not doing so beyond the DM making lots of &quot;gotcha moment!&quot;<br /> <br /> It is a flaw? In my opinion yes, because it relies on a particular style of Dming. However i wont say that people wont like it or that they like a flawed game because maybe it was entirely intentional. And this not even the first time a particular style of gaming is favored over the rest, as this also happens with Aspirations.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's not really a flaw if the game lines pretty much all expect you to once in a while throw curve balls at your PCs. At this point the complaint as moved from <i>&quot;The base game doesn't really feel like it's about circumstances that lead you to become monstrous&quot;</i> to <i>&quot;If your ST isn't following what the books recommend, then it is easy to avoid becoming monstrous.&quot;</i><br /> <br /> If the game line is about a dark, gritty and visceral world and OFTEN discusses the need for STs to produce tough scenarios, but you're easily avoiding it, that means your ST isn't really emphasizing those particular aspects of the core-books.<br /> <br /> They aren't a bad ST or running the game incorrectly, but they are departing from the emphasized themes of the game, according to the book.<br /> <br /> Hubris includes all the choices we make knowingly, but it also includes the unintended consequences of those choices (commission and omission.)<br /> <br /> Hubris pertains to thinking you've got it all figured out, especially with your new found powers and insight, but really you don't.<br /> <br /> <br /> Again, it's right there in the essence of it being a world of darkness title with clear-cut horror themes. The fact that they're highlighted in the base material, but aren't focused on by your ST, is an issue you may need to resolve with your ST before saying the game relies on a style of play that is particular or too adversarial.<br /> <br /> <br /> Why play a game in a world where bad shit happens, then be unimpressed when things don't play out the way you intend or go south. Why assume the ST is being unfair or adversarial if it's the essence of the game lines?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Pale_Crusader</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">I like role specialization, more important Grimoires, meaningful commitments to who the character fundamentally is at creation, and people needing to lean upon each other to cover each other's weaknesses, and it all comes with the bonus of making 2/3 or more of Magic less sane and safe for most Mages. Also 1/5 to almost 1/3 of all Magic if you have a Legacy is not a bad slice of power potential at the drop of a hat and still makes them at least on part with other Major Templates. Also since particularly potent effects frequently require Mana it makes their use both tempting and frightening is a way that makes Monkey Paw a part of the mechanics not just a Storytelling style.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm not sold on all the particulars - but I do like the idea of increasing the importance of Grimoires.<br /> <br /> I think SoS is already going to elevate their importance, though.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">N/A</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11166-kryptonitekrusher","name":"kryptonitekrusher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11166_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"200"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-06T06:32:47-07:00","id":1138004,"title":"","content":"I think that the only &quot;nerf&quot; I might implement in my games is that the &quot;Exceptional Success overcomes Withstand&quot; effect never applies to the Insta-death spells. Other than that I am finding it works fine.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i><b>I</b>'m <b>S</b>o <b>M</b>eta <b>E</b>ven <b>T</b>his <b>A</b>cronym</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/195-thorbes","name":"Thorbes","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar195_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2982"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-09-06T14:42:27-07:00","id":1138112,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1135965-do-mages-need-a-bit-of-a-nerf-to-bring-them-in-line?p=1137846#post1137846\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Oh my god i love this.</div> </div> </div> </div><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MtA 2ed p. 86</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\">Mana</span><br /> Mana is the palpable, measurable bleed of the Supernal into the material. It\u2019s a Prime energy, <b>a quintessential force that comes from the energy trapped <u>when the Abyss came into existence</u></b>, or sometimes comes into existence from the glory and greatness of the world.</div> </div> </div> </div>I find that line of text interesting and the Hack could be seen as a mechanical reinforcement of Supernal energy contained by Abyssal pressure. It could be said that using Mana for a spell with my proposed Hack would be like draining the air from an underwater container, the substance applying pressure would flood in, water being the stand in for the Abyss in this metaphor. It is clear that the Lie contains some Truth, distorted as it may be, which is why you can gain Mana from Scourging your pattern or Sacrificing living Patterns and where I think the line of bolded text comes from. That is trapped energy. Refined Truth as a power source for Magic, but what if using that Truth allowed the Abyss to slip into the Fallen world, because it is the sliver of Truth within the Lie that keeps the Untruth of the Abyss at bay and once spent the Truth Mana represents is either gone or returns to the Supernal, leaving a vacuum in its wake for the Abyss to slip in.<br /> <br /> If people wanted some fluff metaphysics for the Hack, that's an idea.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><i>\u201cYouth ages, immaturity is outgrown, ignorance can be educated, and drunkenness sobered, but stupid lasts forever.\u201d </i>~ <b>Aristophanes</b><br /> <i>&quot;Virescit Vulnere Virtus&quot;</i> ~ <b>Stewart Clan Motto</b></div> <div align=\"center\"><span style=\"font-size:18px\"><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Frailty Chronicles</a></span><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">;</a><b><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/801713-homebrew-of-pale_crusader\" target=\"_blank\">Understanding the power of limitations.</a></b></div>","edited":{"userid":3279,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","user":"Pale_Crusader","date":". 09-06-2017, 06:22 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3279-pale_crusader","name":"Pale_Crusader","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3279_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"841"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}